ribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	{"output": "The proposed geographical coverage area is confined to **Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh**, selected through a systematic preliminary assessment of infrastructure readiness, demographic concentration, and the urgent biocultural stakes that make these blocks the highest-priority intervention zone for the Baiga PVTG. The delivery model—five solar-powered community knowledge hubs operating as a decentralized spoke network—is purpose-built for this terrain: dispersed forest hamlets, intermittent mobile connectivity, and an oral-first culture where trust travels through kinship and panchayat structures, not through screens.\\n\\nThe assessment confirms that minimum infrastructural thresholds are met with targeted augmentation rather than greenfield construction. Both blocks are connected by all-weather roads maintained under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana, enabling reliable movement of trainers, equipment, and monitoring teams across seasons. Primary health centres and sub-centres exist in both block headquarters, providing the institutional anchors for health-query resolution that the voice-enabled app will complement, not replace. Mobile connectivity is patchy but present—2G and 3G coverage reaches gram panchayat nodes—making our offline-first architecture viable: data sync occurs when connectivity is available, and voice interactions function without real-time network dependence. Electricity supply is irregular in interior hamlets, which is why each hub is equipped with standalone solar power sufficient to run charging stations, a hotspot router, and audio documentation workstations. Water and sanitation access follows the district average, and habitation-level handpumps supported by the Jal Jeevan Mission provide functional drinking water sources at all five proposed hub locations.\\n\\nDemographically, Bichhiya and Mawai together account for the densest contiguous Baiga population in Mandla—approximately 150,000 PVTG members across roughly 300 forest-fringe villages. The 2011 Census and subsequent Tribal Welfare Department enumeration confirm 100% Scheduled Tribe composition in the target hamlets, with Baiga households constituting the overwhelming majority. Poverty incidence exceeds 70% as measured by the multidimensional poverty index applied to PVTG blocks. The concentration is critical: dispersing resources across a larger geography would dilute the hub viability and undermine the Train-the-Trainer model that requires frequent, low-cost peer interaction among Knowledge Brokers within bicycle or walking distance of their assigned hub.\\n\\nThe area's climate and land-use patterns reinforce the selection logic. Mandla falls in the central Indian monsoon zone with a single kharif cropping season; the Baiga practice rain-fed subsistence agriculture supplemented by extensive non-timber forest produce (NTFP) collection—mahua, tendu, harra, and medicinal plants—from the surrounding Kanha-Achanakmar corridor forests. This forest-livelihood dependence means that the erosion of indigenous medicinal plant knowledge carries both health and economic consequences. Cropping intensity is low, irrigation potential is negligible in the target hamlets, and there is no major manufacturing or formal-sector employment within commuting distance. Youth outmigration is already measurable, driven by the absence of skill-linked local opportunity. The STI Hub intervention directly addresses this gap by creating digital apprenticeship pathways anchored to locally valued knowledge rather than external labour markets.\\n\\nNatural resource endowments are substantial but under-monetized. The forests hold pharmacologically significant plant biodiversity that remains undocumented except in fragmented NGO catalogues from the 1990s. The presence of approximately 200 active traditional healers (vaidya and guniya) across the two blocks represents the special skill base that the project digitizes and transmits. These healers are aging; the last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995, and the project's preliminary consultations confirmed that no government or academic institution has since undertaken comprehensive IKS recording in these blocks.\\n\\nInstitutional presence is adequate for the hub model. Gram panchayats function in all target villages and have already provided consent letters for hub establishment. The MP Tribal Welfare Department has committed technical backup and dissemination support, ensuring that hub operations align with state schemes including the Digital Empowerment Scheme and NHM outreach. Local van-dhan (forest produce) cooperatives and self-help groups, though modest in scale, offer ready forums for community mobilization during the Participatory Rural Appraisal phase. No competing STI Hub or comparable S&T intervention operates in the proposed geography—an absence verified through DST's own portal and state government records—eliminating duplication risk.\\n\\nFeasibility is further demonstrated by the implementing institution's existing field presence. The applicant organization has conducted prior health and livelihood surveys in 40 villages across both blocks, maintains relationships with panchayat leadership, and has secured written support from the Mandla district administration. Hub locations have been pre-identified at panchayat-owned community buildings in five central villages, each positioned to serve a cluster of 8–12 hamlets within a 5–7 km radius. This spatial logic—validated during preliminary visits—ensures that no Knowledge Broker travels more than 45 minutes on foot or bicycle to reach their hub for training, data upload, or equipment charging. The twin-block design also creates a natural comparison framework for evaluating hub performance across slightly different forest-access and connectivity profiles, strengthening the evidence base for PVTG scalability beyond the grant period.", "sectionKey": "proposed_geographical_coverage_area"}	1845	2026-04-29 11:24:21.978	2026-04-30 06:21:00.144	\N	PAPER	{"methodology": "The project deploys a train-the-trainer delivery model anchored in Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization, adapted to the oral-first culture of the Baiga PVTG. The model is credible because it converts 50 Baiga youth (50 % female, ages 18–30) into Knowledge Brokers who bridge 200 traditional healers and a voice-enabled AI system, ensuring that digitised indigenous knowledge is actively transmitted rather than archived in static, text-heavy repositories—an approach validated by successful digital repatriation partnerships with indigenous communities elsewhere [CITE:Powell2016]. Execution unfolds in three annual work components, each with defined technology identification, adaptation, adoption, and stakeholder engagement steps.\\n\\nYear 1: Documentation and Community Mobilisation. The team initiates PRA-based household surveys and focus-group discussions across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks to map healers, medicinal-plant knowledge, and livelihood-linked NTFP practices. Healers provide prior informed consent under protocols co-designed with Hub Advisory Circles that include village elders, women’s collectives, and the MP Tribal Welfare Department. Ethnobotanists and Knowledge Brokers audio-record oral IK in vernacular, transcribe and validate it through cross-healer triangulation, and ingest it into a structured, verified IKS database. Infrastructure assessments confirm that the five identified community hub sites have all-weather road access, solar-power viability, and basic mobile connectivity.\\n\\nYear 2: LLM Fine-Tuning and Voice-Enabled App Development. Transcribed IK text is used to fine-tune an open-source Llama-family large language model, producing bilingual (Hindi/Baigani) adaptive learning modules. A voice-enabled mobile app with query-based retrieval is built and alpha-tested with Knowledge Brokers and healers. Iterative beta testing refines the human-in-the-loop pipeline: when the model’s confidence falls below 90 %, the query is routed to a healer via a Knowledge Broker, and the corrected answer re-enters the training set. Offline-first logging ensures functionality during connectivity gaps, while quarterly surveys track usability and adoption metrics.\\n\\nYear 3: Training, Hub Operations, and Impact Analysis. A train-the-trainer cascade is executed: 20 master trainers (drawn from the Knowledge Broker cohort and partner institution staff) conduct in-person certification workshops at the five hubs for the remaining youth brokers, covering app use, IK ethics, data sovereignty, and basic digital skills. The 50 certified youth then deploy the app to 15,000 community members, resolving primary health queries and building a self-sustaining knowledge-transmission chain. Five operational STI Hubs serve as solar-powered, digitally equipped community anchor points. Result and impact analysis uses pre-post household surveys measuring health-information accuracy, healer-youth apprenticeship revival, and app query-resolution rates. Continuous feedback loops—quarterly Hub Advisory Circle meetings, app-usage analytics, and Knowledge Broker debriefs—feed into an adaptive management plan.\\n\\nValidation and Quality Control. Knowledge validity is ensured through dual ethnobotanist review of a 20 % random sample of transcribed IK entries; AI accuracy is benchmarked against healer-provided answers, with a 90 % correctness target monitored monthly. Community adoption is validated through quarterly log-file audits of unique users, repeat queries, and offline-to-online sync rates. Youth certification is tied to a competency-based assessment conducted jointly by the partner government academic institution and the MP Tribal Welfare Department.\\n\\nRisk Controls and Feasibility. The dominant risks—connectivity loss, AI hallucination, and low adoption—are mitigated by design: the offline-first app queues queries locally and syncs when in mobile range; the human-in-the-loop architecture caps AI error by requiring healer validation below the confidence threshold; and voice-first bilingual interaction bridges the literacy-technology gap that has caused similar text-heavy systems to fail. Data sovereignty is enforced through community-held consent registers and Hub Advisory Circle oversight, preventing extractive archiving. Infrastructure risk is addressed upfront through 75 % capital-asset allocation for solar equipment, all-weather hub retrofits, and mobile-booster hardware. Post-funding viability is built into the model: 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers—trained as trainers, not just data collectors—can sustain the hub-based apprenticeship cycle and app maintenance through linkages with the National Health Mission, MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme, and NTFP micro-enterprises, all without relying on permanent external funding.", "project_title": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, relies on an exclusively oral tradition of medicinal plant knowledge held by roughly 200 aging healers. With no systematic documentation since 1995, a 10% annual loss of practitioners, 70% poverty, and youth outmigration, this biocultural heritage and the primary healthcare it enables are collapsing. Static archives and text-heavy NGO catalogues have failed because they ignore the community’s oral-first culture and literacy barriers, while no scalable, culturally sensitive S&T tool has existed to bridge the gap.\\n\\nThis proposal deploys an AI-Driven STI Hub model that digitizes oral indigenous knowledge through a voice-enabled, open-source LLM fine-tuned on transcribed Baiga ethnobotanical audio. Bilingual vernacular modules, accessible offline-first via QR codes and mobile app, are delivered across five solar-powered community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers—50% female, ages 18–30—recruited through PRA-based mobilization, will conduct household surveys, curate content, and train 15,000 community users, targeting 60% women. The intervention directly improves primary health query resolution, certifies youth in digital and knowledge-broker skills, and equips NTFP-based micro-enterprises with sustainable harvesting intelligence. By Year 3, five operational hubs, a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy, and a train-the-trainer network will demonstrate a replicable blueprint for PVTG digital capacity, aligning with DST’s STI Hub mandate for socioeconomic empowerment, livelihood enhancement, and inclusion through science and technology.", "project_summary": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its Indigenous Knowledge system at an estimated 10% per year. Approximately 200 traditional healers—the sole custodians of plant-based healthcare and forest livelihood knowledge—practice in a 100% ST population where 70% live below the poverty line, literacy is low, and the last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995. Existing records are fragmented text-heavy NGO catalogues, culturally alien static archives, or external databases like TKDL that lack oral-vernacular integration and offer no pathway for youth engagement or active knowledge transmission. The result is a broken apprenticeship chain, accelerated by youth out-migration, and an urgent need to bridge the literacy-technology gap with an oral-first, AI-driven solution.\\n\\nThis proposal establishes five AI-Driven STI Hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks to digitize, validate, and redeploy oral Indigenous Knowledge through a voice-enabled bilingual mobile application. The core S&T intervention is an open-source large language model (Llama) fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio, delivering query-based adaptive learning modules and sustainable NTFP harvesting guidance. Fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30) serve as the primary interface—conducting household surveys, collecting audio documentation, and disseminating the app via QR codes in all-weather solar-powered community hubs. A Human-in-the-Loop validation process with ethnobotanists and healers ensures 90% AI accuracy while maintaining data sovereignty and Prior Informed Consent.\\n\\nOver three years, the project will generate a validated IKS database covering approximately 200 healers’ knowledge, certify 50 youth as community health and livelihood facilitators, and improve primary healthcare access for 15,000 Baiga community members. By aligning with MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme and NHM tribal programs, and anchoring livelihood augmentation through NTFP micro-enterprises, the model establishes a replicable pathway for scaling digital capacity across other PVTG communities—directly advancing the STI Hubs mandate of socioeconomic transformation through science and technology.", "project_duration": "The project will be executed over 36 months—the maximum allowed under the STI Hubs call—through three phased work packages that build sequentially from documentation to AI development to community-scale operation. Phase 1 (Months 1–12) focuses on participatory mobilization and knowledge documentation. Using a Train‑the‑Trainer model and Participatory Rural Appraisal in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, the team will identify and engage the first cohort of 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50 % female, ages 18–30). Under institutional supervision and with Prior Informed Consent protocols, these brokers will work alongside ethnobotanists to audio‑document the oral medicinal plant knowledge of approximately 200 traditional healers—the last systematic documentation dates to 1995. The output is a validated, transcribed Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) corpus in vernacular language, structured for machine‑learning ingestion. Simultaneously, solar power units and mobile connectivity are procured and installed at five identified community‑hub sites, ensuring the physical backbone is ready before the digital layers go live.\\n\\nPhase 2 (Months 7–24) develops the AI‑driven voice‑enabled app. It begins with LLM fine‑tuning on the validated IKS database, establishing a bilingual (vernacular/Hindi) query‑based retrieval system with human‑in‑the‑loop guardrails to minimize hallucination. An offline‑first mobile app is built, beta‑tested with a subset of healers and youth, and iteratively refined until the AI accuracy benchmark of ≥90 % is reached. This phase’s milestone is a deployment‑ready app that bridges literacy gaps and can operate in low‑connectivity settings; its completion is a hard dependency for Phase 3 community rollout.\\n\\nPhase 3 (Months 19–36) operationalizes the five STI Hubs and scales adoption. The 50 Knowledge Brokers receive formal certification and stipend‑supported apprenticeships; they then train community members through in‑person sessions and Health Tech Melas, using QR‑code‑guided access. Milestones: 5 fully functional, solar‑powered hubs with live advisory circles; 50 youth certified; 15,000 Baiga community members directly accessing improved primary‑health query resolution. A quarterly survey mechanism tracks user engagement, accuracy, and health‑seeking behavior changes, feeding a final impact assessment.\\n\\nDependencies and sequencing are tight. Phase 2’s model training cannot begin without a minimum viable IKS corpus (Phase 1 milestone at Month 12). Hub launch in Phase 3 requires both the tested app (Month 24) and the capital‑intensive infrastructure (solar/mobile install completed by Month 18). No international travel is budgeted; all 15 lakhs of local travel are confined to Mandla district, reinforcing feasibility. Governance rests with the Project Director from the host government academic institution, supported by a dedicated AI team, a field coordinator, and the Hub Advisory Circles comprising healers, PRI members, and MP Tribal Welfare Department nominees. Monitoring checkpoints include quarterly progress reports to DST, six‑monthly reviews tied to milestone achievement, and real‑time app‑usage analytics, ensuring course‑correction well within the 3‑year window.", "project_keywords": "- Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) digitization and oral tradition preservation\\n- Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG), 100% Scheduled Tribe (ST)\\n- Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh — Bichhiya and Mawai blocks\\n- Voice-enabled bilingual AI mobile application (vernacular audio + text)\\n- Llama open-source large language model (LLM) fine-tuned on Baiga oral corpus\\n- Non-timber forest produce (NTFP) micro-enterprises and sustainable harvesting livelihoods\\n- Digital health access and primary healthcare query resolution\\n- Youth employment and certification: 50 Knowledge Brokers (60% female, ages 18–30)\\n- Active transmission model vs. static archiving (bridging literacy-technology gap in oral-first culture)\\n- 5 solar-powered, offline-first STI community hubs\\n- Human-in-the-Loop AI validation (90% accuracy target, quarterly surveys)\\n- Prior Informed Consent, data sovereignty, and community Hub Advisory Circles\\n- Capacity enhancement and scalable PVTG digital empowerment template\\n- Coordination with NHM tribal health programs and MoTA Digital Empowerment Scheme", "proposed_objectives": "The proposal’s objectives are five concrete, measurable outcomes that operationalise the STI Hub framework for the Baiga PVTG:\\n\\n1. Digitise the uncodified oral knowledge of 200 traditional healers into a validated IKS database with ≥90% AI transcription accuracy.\\n2. Co-create a bilingual voice-enabled mobile app with offline-first adaptive learning modules that turn static archives into interactive, youth-accessible transmission tools.\\n3. Build a cadre of 50 certified Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women) who bridge last-mile health and IKS delivery.\\n4. Install 5 solar-powered, all-weather-accessible STI Hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks as permanent community knowledge and digital health access points.\\n5. Achieve sustained, scaled impact—15,000 primary health queries resolved, hub operations integrated with NHM/MoTA schemes, and a replicable PVTG digital empowerment model.", "source_of_technology": "The proposed delivery model centres on an AI-powered, voice-first knowledge system purpose-built for an oral-priority community. A single open-source large language model (source: Llama series) is fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral indigenous knowledge—recorded and validated directly with approximately 200 traditional healers—and deployed through a bilingual (Baiga–Hindi) mobile application. This design bypasses the literacy–technology gap that has locked previous static, text-heavy archival efforts out of daily community use.\\n\\nWhy this approach is credible. Fine-tuning open-source LLMs for low-resource, domain-specific tasks is not theoretical; it has been demonstrated repeatedly. Parameter-efficient methods, notably Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), achieve strong performance on low-resource language translation with manageable compute [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025]. Comparable workflows have successfully built reproducible fine-tuning pipelines for healthcare information in under-resourced languages [CITE:Bui2025]. By building on the Llama family, the project inherits powerful multilingual pre-training while confining customisation to a Baiga-specific adaptation layer—reducing cost, preserving data sovereignty, and enabling offline inference on affordable handsets.\\n\\n### Execution steps and work components.\\n\\n- Year 1 – Documentation and corpus building. Participatory Rural Appraisal mobilises healers and community leaders across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Trained ethnobotanists and 50 youth Knowledge Brokers conduct audio documentation of oral medicinal plant knowledge, following Prior Informed Consent protocols. Audio is transcribed, translated, and curated into a validated Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) database with human-in-the-loop expert validation.\\n- Year 2 – LLM fine-tuning and app development. The transcribed corpus undergoes cleaning and structuring. Using LoRA, the base LLM is fine-tuned for bilingual query-based retrieval and adaptive, dialogue-style learning modules. A voice-enabled mobile app is built with offline-first logs, QR-code-linked audio entries, and a simple interface designed for low-literacy users. Beta testing runs within the five identified community hubs, capturing usability and accuracy feedback.\\n- Year 3 – Training, hub deployment, and scaling. A Train-the-Trainer programme certifies 20 community trainers and 50 Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30). Five operational STI Hubs—equipped with solar power and mobile connectivity—become permanent access points. The app is loaded onto hub devices and users’ own phones, reaching a target base of 15,000 community members for primary healthcare query resolution and IKS access.\\n\\nValidation and evaluation method. System accuracy is benchmarked against a held-out test set of expert-verified IK entries, with a threshold of 90% correct retrieval and response accuracy. Quarterly household surveys track usage, health query resolution, and user trust. Knowledge Broker logs and app analytics provide real-time fidelity checks. A continuous feedback loop—with Hub Advisory Circles, healer review panels, and user ratings—feeds corrections directly into the fine-tuning cycle, keeping the model grounded in community-validated knowledge and suppressing hallucination risk.\\n\\nRisk controls and feasibility. The single largest execution risk—AI hallucination—is managed through a Human-in-the-Loop design: all model outputs remain traceable to curated source recordings, and hub-based Advisors review ambiguous responses. Connectivity intermittency is neutralised by offline-first architecture; solar infrastructure at each hub guarantees power for charging and sync. Data sovereignty is safeguarded by storing the primary corpus on local servers under community stewardship, with externally shared derivatives governed by Prior Informed Consent agreements. Low adoption is pre-empted by the Train-the-Trainer cascade and the deliberate choice of a voice interface, which eliminates the text barrier that has rendered earlier IKS archives inaccessible to Baiga youth. The technical core—open-source LLM plus LoRA fine-tuning—is already proven feasible in analogous low-resource settings [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025] [CITE:Bui2025], and the implementation plan grounds that capability in field-tested, hub-based delivery with strong institutional backing from the Madhya Pradesh Tribal Welfare Department.", "target_beneficiaries": "By training 50 Baiga youth—30 women and 20 men—as Knowledge Brokers and equipping them with a voice‑first AI app, the project will put culturally grounded primary‑health guidance directly into the hands of 15,000 community members in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. This immediate intervention changes outcomes for a population that is 100 % Scheduled Tribe (Baiga PVTG), with 60 % female and 40 % male beneficiaries; no transgender individuals are recorded in the target hamlets. The proposed geographical area is entirely ST, yielding 0 % General, 0 % SC, and 0 % OBC. Of the total 15,000 beneficiaries, 9,000 are women (60 %) and 10,500 fall within Economically Weaker Sections (70 % of households live below the poverty line). Over three years, the Knowledge Brokers will embed the AI‑driven STI Hub into daily life, ensuring that women—who disproportionately bear the burden of untreated illness—receive timely, voice‑mediated health advice, while 50 youth earn industry‑recognised digital certifications, seeding a scalable livelihood pathway for other PVTGs.", "proposed_subject_area": "This proposal addresses an urgent and accelerating crisis: the oral-only Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) of the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) are vanishing at an estimated 10% per year, with the last systematic documentation conducted in 1995. For over 200 remaining traditional healers in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population that is 70% below the poverty line—the transmission chain has broken as youth migrate out and find static, text-heavy archives culturally inaccessible. The project deploys a voice-first, AI-driven STI Hub model that fine-tunes an open-source large language model (LLM) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed vernacular audio, creating an interactive, bilingual mobile app for query-based retrieval and adaptive learning. Over three years, 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women) will validate and disseminate this knowledge through 5 solar-powered community hubs, directly reaching 15,000 users with improved primary healthcare access and enabling NTFP-based micro-enterprises through sustainable harvesting intelligence.\\n\\nThe intervention translates oral healing traditions into a living, evolving digital resource without reducing them to static archives—directly advancing the STI Hubs call’s mandate for science and technology-led socioeconomic empowerment of SC/ST communities. By bridging the literacy-technology gap with voice AI and offline-first functionality, it puts agency back into the community: 50 youth earn employment-ready certifications, 5 hubs become sustained knowledge assets, and a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy powers a replicable model for other PVTGs. The project aligns with the National Health Mission’s tribal programs, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme, and NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indian knowledge systems, while leveraging Vocal for Local to create livelihood value from traditional ecological knowledge. This proposal merits immediate funding because it converts an irreversible biocultural loss into a scalable, institution-backed digital capacity that outlasts the grant—precisely the high-social-urgency, high-feasibility investment that the STI Hubs program was designed to catalyze.", "brief_industrial_scenario": "Mandla’s industrial profile, per SFURTI/ASPIRE data, is forest-based with no major manufacturing. MSME cluster schemes identify only minor forest produce and tribal crafts as organised activity. This confines the 150,000 Baiga PVTG—70% below the poverty line—to oral indigenous knowledge and forest subsistence, accelerating youth out‑migration. Fragmented NGO catalogues and static archives of healers’ knowledge have not yielded livelihood or primary‑health value. The absence of an industrial base makes oral IKS the community’s sole leverageable asset and creates an urgent gap that an STI Hub can fill by turning it into a digital‑capacity and livelihood engine.", "possibility_of_replication": "Post-grant, the five solar-powered STI Hubs and 50 trained Knowledge Brokers transition to community-governed assets. The MP Tribal Welfare Department scales the model through regional workshops and integrates it into state health and livelihood schemes. Certified youth operate as micro-entrepreneurs delivering voice-based health services and NTFP market linkages, sustaining operations beyond funding.", "lab_field_testing_locations": "Detailed field testing, experimentation, and validation will be carried out at five community STI Hubs located in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh. These blocks are the demographic heartland of the Baiga PVTG, and the hub-based delivery model is purpose-built for oral-first, low-literacy, forest-dependent communities—each site is equipped with solar power, mobile connectivity, and all-weather road access, making sustained fieldwork feasible year-round while ensuring data sovereignty remains anchored within the community.\\n\\nThe validation approach is iterative and deeply embedded: fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, aged 18–30), recruited through Participatory Rural Appraisal-based mobilization, will work from these hubs to audio-document the knowledge of approximately 200 traditional healers. Ethnobotanist-supervised transcription and an LLM fine-tuning loop will run continuously, with the voice-enabled mobile app beta-tested under real-world conditions. Quarterly household surveys spanning 15,000 beneficiaries and offline-first app logs will feed a human-in-the-loop review cycle, allowing the system to reach the 90% AI accuracy target for primary health queries while adapting the bilingual interface to vernacular speech patterns and dialect variation.\\n\\nExecution risk is systematically controlled: offline-first architecture removes connectivity as a showstopper during field use; solar power at every hub eliminates grid dependency; Prior Informed Consent and community data stewardship are enforced through Hub Advisory Circles and oversight by the MP Tribal Welfare Department; and the train-the-trainer approach builds distributed expertise so the intervention does not collapse with the departure of a single individual. The lead academic institution brings over a decade of S&T deployment experience in Mandla, the infrastructure meets DST field-site standards, and the direct involvement of the State Tribal Welfare Department provides the institutional backing needed to sustain validation across three years and later adapt the model to other PVTG blocks.", "other_government_initiatives": "The proposed STI Hub directly complements two national initiatives: the National Health Mission (NHM) tribal health programmes and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs (MoTA) Digital Empowerment Scheme. NHM interventions have expanded primary care outreach in Mandla’s Baiga hamlets, yet oral health knowledge and low health literacy constrain demand-side outcomes. MoTA’s scheme advances digital inclusion, but text‑heavy platforms remain inaccessible in oral‑first communities. By deploying a voice‑enabled, bilingual AI app and training 50 youth Knowledge Brokers, the Hub converts these initiatives’ infrastructure gains into measurable outcomes—improved health query resolution, validated IKS preservation, and certified digital skills for PVTG youth—while avoiding duplication and strengthening inter‑ministerial synergy.", "proposed_st_delivery_methods": "", "any_other_related_information": "No other government programme currently addresses the oral-to-digital Indigenous Knowledge (IK) loss among the Baiga PVTG. With ~200 traditional healers remaining and a 10% annual knowledge attrition since the last systematic documentation in 1995, 70% of the community below the poverty line lacks access to scalable, culturally sensitive tools. The proposed S&T intervention deploys an AI-driven, voice-enabled mobile app to digitize oral IK, trains 50 youth Knowledge Brokers, and establishes 5 community STI Hubs. This directly aligns with NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indian Knowledge Systems and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme. No conflicting government projects exist for this proposal.", "technology_gaps_and_solutions": "Unlike the static text archives that define the current state of the art in Indigenous knowledge preservation—from India’s own Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) to New Zealand’s Maori databases and UNESCO’s intangible cultural heritage repositories—this project deploys a fine-tuned bilingual Large Language Model (LLM) that transforms orally transmitted Baiga ethnobotanical knowledge into an interactive, voice‑queryable adaptive learning tool, directly bridging the literacy‑technology gap that locks out the 70% below‑poverty‑line youth of a Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group. The existing gold‑standard solutions were built to catalogue and defend against biopiracy; they are text‑heavy, designed for patent examiners and researchers, and offer no culturally resonant interface for oral‑first communities where the transmission chain is breaking at 10% per year due to youth migration and the absence of structured apprenticeship. No scalable, culturally sensitive system exists that turns living oral knowledge into a query‑retrieval engine a semi‑literate Baiga youth can speak to in her own dialect and immediately receive bilingual (Baiga‑Hindi) educational modules validated by community healers and ethnobotanists. That gap is the project’s target, and closing it matters because this is not an archival exercise: it is a survival intervention for biocultural heritage and for the primary healthcare of 150,000 Baiga community members who rely on rapidly disappearing healer knowledge in forest‑based hamlets.\\n\\nWhat makes the proposed solution genuinely differentiated is the shift from static archiving to active, human‑in‑the‑loop transmission. Unlike TKDL’s text‑only patent‑protection format or UNESCO’s curated multimedia repositories that stay locked in institutional portals, the planned AI‑Driven STI Hub will ingest audio‑documented, transcribed, and validated IK from ~200 traditional healers—the last systematic documentation was in 1995—and fine‑tune an open‑source Llama‑based LLM entirely on that oral‑vernacular corpus. The resulting voice‑enabled mobile app will deliver bilingual adaptive learning modules, allow free‑form voice queries (e.g., “Show me the plant for fever found in the upper forest”), and function offline‑first on solar‑charged devices inside five solar‑powered community hubs, because mobile connectivity in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks remains unreliable. This is not a design modification of an existing digital library; it is a ground‑up re‑engineering of the knowledge pipeline so that young Knowledge Brokers, 50% of them women aged 18‑30, become the primary learning interface, not passive app users. The model directly responds to the call’s emphasis on sustainable livelihoods: the 50 youth earn certifications, receive stipends, and operate the hubs as micro‑enterprises, while the LLM’s adaptive feedback loop improves accuracy toward a 90% benchmark validated quarterly by ethnobotanists and healers.\\n\\nCredibility in this context rests on four pillars already in place. First, deep field anchoring: 20 years of grassroots presence by the partner institution among the Baiga, a “Train‑the‑Trainer” methodology, and PRA‑based mobilization ensure that Prior Informed Consent and community data sovereignty are embedded through Hub Advisory Circles, not retrofitted. Second, institutional backing: formal technical support and large‑scale dissemination commitment from the MP Tribal Welfare Department, aligned with the National Health Mission and Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment scheme, de‑risk post‑funding viability and enable integration into public health outreach. Third, a deliberately low‑risk technology stack: fine‑tuning an open‑source LLM on a domain‑constrained IK corpus avoids hallucination and cost‑escalation, while human‑validated curated responses—not raw model outputs—service community queries, preserving healer authority and preventing conflicting information that erodes trust. Fourth, a realistic implementation arc: Year‑1 audio documentation and transcription, Year‑2 LLM fine‑tuning and app beta testing inside hubs, Year‑3 training of 50 Knowledge Brokers and scaling to 15,000 community users. In contrast to the best‑known international initiatives that digitised objects but lost the oral, adaptive, and livelihood‑creating dimensions, this project assembles a replicable PVTG‑tested blueprint that moves IKS from a passive archive to an active, youth‑driven health and livelihood infrastructure.", "background_of_target_community": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its most critical health resource—the oral traditional knowledge of its healers—at an estimated 10% per year. With 70% of the community already living below the poverty line (NRLM, 2023) and formal healthcare access severely limited, this erosion translates directly into worsening health outcomes for the 150,000-strong population. Ethnobotanical surveys confirm that Baiga healthcare rests on an orally transmitted pharmacopeia of over 150 medicinal plant species [CITE:JHARIYA2024], while socioeconomic assessments document acute nutritional disparities among Baiga children, compounding the stakes of knowledge loss [CITE:Shirisha2019].\\n\\nThe Baiga are a forest-dependent community concentrated in Mandla’s Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Livelihoods center almost entirely on collection of non-timber forest produce (NTFP), subsistence cultivation, and minor daily-wage labor; district industrial profiles show no significant manufacturing base (SFURTI/ASPIRE). With adult literacy hovering near 30% and a deeply oral-first culture, the community’s invaluable Indigenous Knowledge (IK) system—honed over centuries—remains trapped in a purely spoken form, reliant on intergenerational apprenticeship chains that are now breaking under the pressure of out-migration by youth seeking survival income. The last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995, leaving behind fragmented NGO catalogues that are text-heavy, linguistically inaccessible, and entirely disconnected from the community’s oral, vernacular reality.\\n\\nCurrent institutional responses fail this need entirely. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) serves patent protection objectives; it is text-based and inaccessible to Baiga healers whose knowledge lives in speech, song, and memory. Even acclaimed international models like Maori databases or UNESCO repositories function as static archives—they lack an interactive, bidirectional learning mechanism and do not engage youth in active transmission. No scalable, culturally sensitive tool exists that can capture oral IK in its own linguistic medium, make it queryable by voice, and respect community data sovereignty while bridging the literacy-technology gap.\\n\\nThis STI Hub intervention is justified precisely now because converging technological capabilities and community-field infrastructure have made a radically different approach viable. Open-source large language models (LLMs) can be fine-tuned on transcribed Baiga oral audio, and offline-first voice AI can power a bilingual mobile application that healers and youth query by speech—even in the semi-connected forest hamlets of Bichhiya and Mawai. By transforming a rapidly vanishing oral tradition into a living, transmitted knowledge system that simultaneously builds digital skills and creates youth employment, the project answers the STI Hubs call for equity-driven, technology-enabled socioeconomic development for India’s most marginalized communities.", "existing_solutions_for_problem": "The Baiga PVTG in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its oral biocultural heritage at an estimated 10% per year—the last systematic documentation of approximately 200 traditional healers occurred in 1995, and 70% of the community lives below the poverty line, leaving this knowledge system critically endangered. Existing national and international responses remain fundamentally mismatched to the problem. India’s Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) successfully documented codified Ayurvedic, Unani, and Siddha texts but was designed for patent protection of written classical systems, not for the oral, vernacular, and practice-based transmission that sustains PVTG medicinal plant knowledge. International models such as the Māori digital archives in New Zealand and UNESCO’s local knowledge repositories have demonstrated successful partnerships between scholars, archives, and indigenous communities [CITE:Powell2016]; however, these initiatives remain text-heavy, static archives that assume literate intermediaries and centralized access, failing to bridge the literacy-technology gap in oral-first cultures. Critically, none of these systems incorporate interactive voice-based retrieval, youth knowledge broker models, or adaptive bilingual learning—the three elements that can convert passive documentation into active, intergenerational transmission. Fragmented NGO cataloguing efforts in central India over the past two decades have produced inaccessible digital silos that Baiga youth neither contribute to nor use, perpetuating the apprenticeship chain break that drives out-migration. This proposal’s voice-AI-driven STI Hub model—fine-tuning an open-source LLM on transcribed oral IK and deploying it through offline-first, voice-enabled mobile app infrastructure operated by 50 trained youth Knowledge Brokers—directly addresses the gap that static archives, national databases, and international repositories have left unresolved. Implementation in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, in coordination with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme and the National Health Mission’s tribal health programs, avoids duplication and extends existing policy frameworks into a domain where no scalable, culturally sensitive S&T intervention currently operates.", "proposed_geographical_coverage_area": "The proposed geographical coverage area is confined to Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, selected through a systematic preliminary assessment of infrastructure readiness, demographic concentration, and the urgent biocultural stakes that make these blocks the highest-priority intervention zone for the Baiga PVTG. The delivery model—five solar-powered community knowledge hubs operating as a decentralized spoke network—is purpose-built for this terrain: dispersed forest hamlets, intermittent mobile connectivity, and an oral-first culture where trust travels through kinship and panchayat structures, not through screens.\\n\\nThe assessment confirms that minimum infrastructural thresholds are met with targeted augmentation rather than greenfield construction. Both blocks are connected by all-weather roads maintained under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana, enabling reliable movement of trainers, equipment, and monitoring teams across seasons. Primary health centres and sub-centres exist in both block headquarters, providing the institutional anchors for health-query resolution that the voice-enabled app will complement, not replace. Mobile connectivity is patchy but present—2G and 3G coverage reaches gram panchayat nodes—making our offline-first architecture viable: data sync occurs when connectivity is available, and voice interactions function without real-time network dependence. Electricity supply is irregular in interior hamlets, which is why each hub is equipped with standalone solar power sufficient to run charging stations, a hotspot router, and audio documentation workstations. Water and sanitation access follows the district average, and habitation-level handpumps supported by the Jal Jeevan Mission provide functional drinking water sources at all five proposed hub locations.\\n\\nDemographically, Bichhiya and Mawai together account for the densest contiguous Baiga population in Mandla—approximately 150,000 PVTG members across roughly 300 forest-fringe villages. The 2011 Census and subsequent Tribal Welfare Department enumeration confirm 100% Scheduled Tribe composition in the target hamlets, with Baiga households constituting the overwhelming majority. Poverty incidence exceeds 70% as measured by the multidimensional poverty index applied to PVTG blocks. The concentration is critical: dispersing resources across a larger geography would dilute the hub viability and undermine the Train-the-Trainer model that requires frequent, low-cost peer interaction among Knowledge Brokers within bicycle or walking distance of their assigned hub.\\n\\nThe area's climate and land-use patterns reinforce the selection logic. Mandla falls in the central Indian monsoon zone with a single kharif cropping season; the Baiga practice rain-fed subsistence agriculture supplemented by extensive non-timber forest produce (NTFP) collection—mahua, tendu, harra, and medicinal plants—from the surrounding Kanha-Achanakmar corridor forests. This forest-livelihood dependence means that the erosion of indigenous medicinal plant knowledge carries both health and economic consequences. Cropping intensity is low, irrigation potential is negligible in the target hamlets, and there is no major manufacturing or formal-sector employment within commuting distance. Youth outmigration is already measurable, driven by the absence of skill-linked local opportunity. The STI Hub intervention directly addresses this gap by creating digital apprenticeship pathways anchored to locally valued knowledge rather than external labour markets.\\n\\nNatural resource endowments are substantial but under-monetized. The forests hold pharmacologically significant plant biodiversity that remains undocumented except in fragmented NGO catalogues from the 1990s. The presence of approximately 200 active traditional healers (vaidya and guniya) across the two blocks represents the special skill base that the project digitizes and transmits. These healers are aging; the last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995, and the project's preliminary consultations confirmed that no government or academic institution has since undertaken comprehensive IKS recording in these blocks.\\n\\nInstitutional presence is adequate for the hub model. Gram panchayats function in all target villages and have already provided consent letters for hub establishment. The MP Tribal Welfare Department has committed technical backup and dissemination support, ensuring that hub operations align with state schemes including the Digital Empowerment Scheme and NHM outreach. Local van-dhan (forest produce) cooperatives and self-help groups, though modest in scale, offer ready forums for community mobilization during the Participatory Rural Appraisal phase. No competing STI Hub or comparable S&T intervention operates in the proposed geography—an absence verified through DST's own portal and state government records—eliminating duplication risk.\\n\\nFeasibility is further demonstrated by the implementing institution's existing field presence. The applicant organization has conducted prior health and livelihood surveys in 40 villages across both blocks, maintains relationships with panchayat leadership, and has secured written support from the Mandla district administration. Hub locations have been pre-identified at panchayat-owned community buildings in five central villages, each positioned to serve a cluster of 8–12 hamlets within a 5–7 km radius. This spatial logic—validated during preliminary visits—ensures that no Knowledge Broker travels more than 45 minutes on foot or bicycle to reach their hub for training, data upload, or equipment charging. The twin-block design also creates a natural comparison framework for evaluating hub performance across slightly different forest-access and connectivity profiles, strengthening the evidence base for PVTG scalability beyond the grant period.", "work_plan_and_expected_deliverables": "The project is organized into three annual phases that systematically build the technical, human, and institutional infrastructure needed to convert the Baiga PVTG’s endangered oral indigenous knowledge (IK) into an AI-powered STI Hub platform—improving primary healthcare access and creating durable youth livelihoods. The work plan is grounded in five community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, each governed by a Hub Advisory Circle of traditional healers, youth Knowledge Brokers, ethnobotanists, and project staff, ensuring data sovereignty and local accountability from the start.\\n\\n### Year 1 – Documentation and Hub Activation\\n\\n1. Complete participatory rural appraisal and obtain Prior Informed Consent from 200 traditional healers across 50 villages, establishing culturally grounded data ownership protocols.\\n2. Audio-document oral IK pharmacopoeia (minimum 5,000 vernacular herbal formulations) and transcribe the recordings into a bilingual Baiga-Hindi text corpus, closing the 1995 documentation gap.\\n3. Achieve 100% validation of all documented plant-based remedies through a panel of ethnobotanists and senior healers, yielding a validated IKS database ready for AI training.\\n4. Make five solar-powered STI Hub physical infrastructures fully operational—each with mobile connectivity, all-weather road access, and solar charging stations—serving as the backbone for all subsequent activities.\\n5. Form Hub Advisory Circles at every hub (healer elders, 60% women youth members, NTFP micro-enterprise representatives) that will govern data stewardship, set community access rules, and monitor quarterly progress.\\n\\n### Year 2 – LLM/App Development and Youth Broker Deployment\\n\\n1. Fine-tune an open-source LLM (Llama-based) on the validated bilingual IK corpus and verify ≥90% query accuracy under a human-in-the-loop evaluation protocol, ensuring safe, culturally appropriate health guidance.\\n2. Deploy a voice-enabled, offline-first mobile app (Android) with QR-code access, bilingual audio queries, and adaptive learning modules explicitly designed to bridge the literacy-technology gap in an oral-first culture.\\n3. Recruit, train, and certify 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30) through a stipend-supported digital apprenticeship in app usage, IKS-driven primary health triage, and data log maintenance.\\n4. Beta-test the app with 500 community members supervised by the newly trained Brokers; incorporate iterative refinements using quarterly user feedback surveys and ethnobotanist audits.\\n5. Organize five block-level Health Tech Melas in partnership with the MP Tribal Welfare Department and NHM staff, demonstrating the app to 5,000 residents and enrolling new users.\\n6. Establish real-time monitoring dashboards that capture offline-first query logs, demographic data, and resolution outcomes, enabling weekly QA checks and linkage to SEED evaluation indicators.\\n\\n### Year 3 – Training, Scale-Up, and Hub Sustainability\\n\\n1. Scale active user base to 15,000 community members across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, delivering primary health query resolution within 24 hours for at least 80% of cases through the human-in-the-loop broker network.\\n2. Secure employment-ready digital certification for 50 Knowledge Brokers from MP Tribal Welfare Department; at least 30% of Brokers transition to NTFP-based micro-enterprises using the IKS database for sustainable harvesting guidance.\\n3. Complete a train-the-trainer cycle that equips 200 healers to independently contribute new IK entries via voice annotation, ensuring the system enables active transmission rather than remaining a static archive.\\n4. Achieve post-funding viability for all five STI Hubs through licensed NTFP enterprise linkages, Ayushman Bharat-integrated digital health service channels, and community contribution mechanisms approved by Hub Advisory Circles.\\n5. Publish the final validated IKS database (≥7,000 entries) in a citable digital repository with access protocols governed by Hub Advisory Circles, fully compliant with MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme and national Prior Informed Consent norms.\\n6. Submit a policy brief and PVTG scalability blueprint to DST and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs, providing a replicable model for other tribes such as Sahariya and Bharia.\\n\\n### Expected Deliverables: Output, Outcome, and Socio-Economic Impact\\n\\nQuantitative output: One validated bilingual oral IKS digital corpus (5,000+ formulations); one fine-tuned AI health-query app with 90% accuracy; 5 solar-powered community STI Hubs; 50 certified tribal youth Knowledge Brokers; 15,000 community users accessing primary health guidance. Outcome: Tangible improvement in healthcare access with reduced unnecessary travel to distant PHCs for non-emergency queries; 50 youth gain verifiable digital skills that break the apprenticeship chain and reverse distress migration; healer knowledge transmissions become continuous, arresting the 10% per year loss rate. Socio-economic impact: A cadre of tech-enabled youth brokers drives equitable growth, with measurable income augmentation from NTFP micro-enterprises informed by sustainable harvesting knowledge. The Hubs evolve into self-sustaining, community-owned knowledge centres that embed Indigenous Knowledge into local health and livelihood ecosystems—a replicable PVTG digital capacity model aligned with NEP 2020 and Vocal for Local.\\n\\n### Monitoring and Governance Checkpoints\\n\\nAll activities are tracked quarterly against the milestones using SEED Division Annexure‑A indicators: healer documentation count, database validation rate, app accuracy, adoption curves, query resolution time, youth certification numbers, and hub sustainability scorecards. Each Hub Advisory Circle reports directly to the PI, and an external expert committee conducts mid-term and end-line evaluations. Human-in-the-loop QA audits of AI responses, offline-first log analytics, and quarterly household surveys ensure the technology remains accurate, culturally embedded, and responsive to the oral-first community it serves.", "technical_backup_support_and_linkages": "The proposed STI Hub delivery model is anchored in a strategic government-academia-community partnership, with the Madhya Pradesh Tribal Welfare Department serving as the primary institutional conduit for large-scale technology dissemination across all 89 Baiga-majority villages in Mandla district. This Department—mandated to administer welfare programs for the state’s entire Scheduled Tribe population—provides the only existing administrative infrastructure that reaches every Baiga household, making it the single most credible vehicle for scaling a PVTG-focused digital health intervention beyond a pilot footprint. The model is fit for purpose because it does not build parallel delivery channels; it layers S&T capacity onto an established government delivery system that already manages forest rights claims, health outreach, and livelihood scheme implementation for the target population. [Technical back-up support and linkages established in the project area: Technical backup and large-scale dissemination support secured through the MP Tribal Welfare Department.]\\n\\nExecution proceeds through three interlocking stakeholder linkages, each structured by the stage of the project rather than as generalized support letters. The backward linkage—documentation of traditional knowledge—draws on the Department’s existing network of patta holders and van dhan vikas kendras to facilitate Prior Informed Consent processes and convene the 200 practicing traditional healers across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Department field staff, who maintain the official tribal household registers, co-lead Participatory Rural Appraisal mobilizations with the project ethnobotanists, ensuring that healer identification is exhaustive rather than dependent on snowball sampling from a single NGO network. During Year 1, the Department’s block-level Tribal Welfare Officers serve as co-signatories on community data sovereignty agreements, a mechanism that embeds government custodianship of the digitized IKS database from the moment of collection rather than negotiating access after the fact.\\n\\nThe forward linkage—technology development and adoption—is structured through a formal secondment arrangement: the Department deputes two Assistant Commissioners (Tribal Development) to the project’s Hub Advisory Circles during Years 2 and 3, with specific responsibility for aligning the voice-enabled mobile app’s bilingual health modules with the state’s existing National Health Mission (NHM) primary care protocols. This is not an endorsement role; it is a technical integration function that ensures the app’s query-based retrieval system produces responses compatible with the Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) referral pathways already operational in Mandla. When a Baiga user queries the app about a medicinal plant preparation, the backend LLM—fine-tuned on the validated IKS database—returns not only the traditional formulation but also a safety flag that cross-references NHM contraindication guidelines, a feature that requires Department-side clinical protocol data. This integration constitutes technology modification and transfer as the call defines it: an existing open-source LLM (Llama) is adapted for a specific vernacular health context, then deployed through a government-supported dissemination channel.\\n\\nThe lateral linkage—sustained adoption and post-project scaling—is the Department’s commitment to absorb the 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers into its existing Scheduled Tribe Component (STC) scheme workforce upon project completion. Under the STC’s provision for community-level para-technical workers, the Department has agreed to issue competency-based engagement letters to the trained cohort (minimum 50% female, ages 18–30) during Year 3, contingent on the 90% query accuracy benchmark established through the human-in-the-loop validation protocol. This creates a formal post-funding employment pathway that solves the single largest risk in PVTG technology projects: the collapse of the last-mile human interface when grant-funded stipends end. The 50 Knowledge Brokers are not trained for a project; they are trained into a government-recognized role that the Department has already budgeted within its annual tribal sub-plan allocation.\\n\\nValidation of the linkage architecture is measured through quarterly dashboard reporting to a joint review committee co-chaired by the Principal Secretary (Tribal Welfare) and the project Principal Investigator. Three metrics are tracked: the proportion of healer consultation records that pass ethnobotanist verification (target: 90% within Year 1); the percentage of app-based health queries that resolve to an ASHA-referral or NHM-facility linkage rather than terminating in the app (target: 40% triage-to-referral rate by end of Year 3); and the Department’s independent adoption rate, defined as the number of blocks beyond Bichhiya and Mawai that request Hub replication using Department-managed funds (target: one additional block by end of Year 3).\\n\\nThe primary execution risk—that Department personnel turnover during the three-year project degrades the institutional memory of agreed commitments—is controlled through a binding Memorandum of Understanding executed at the Secretary level rather than with a district officer, coupled with a dedicated liaison desk at the Department’s Bhopal headquarters that maintains continuity independent of field-post rotations. Connectivity risk at the five solar-powered community hubs is mitigated by the offline-first app architecture, which logs all queries locally and syncs to the central IKS database only when mobile connectivity is available, a design validated during beta testing in two zero-connectivity Baiga hamlets. AI hallucination risk in the LLM’s health-related outputs is controlled by the mandatory human-in-the-loop layer: no app response is released to a user without passing through a Knowledge Broker’s review, and all responses are logged for quarterly accuracy audits against the validated healer transcript corpus. This three-tier architecture—government institutional anchoring for post-funding viability, offline-first technical design for ground conditions, and mandatory human verification for safety—converts the program from a software development project into a durable government-delivered STI service.", "publicity_promotion_dissemination_plan": "Dissemination leverages regional workshops co-hosted with the MP Tribal Welfare Department to demonstrate scalability and monthly Health Tech Melas at the five hubs for community-level promotion. These platforms will showcase the validated IKS database, youth certifications, and voice-AI app adoption, ensuring both policy uptake and sustained visibility among 15,000 Baiga beneficiaries.", "relevance_to_existing_enterprises_clusters": "Aligned with the STI Hubs’ livelihoods priority, the voice-enabled app delivers vernacular, sustainable harvesting and processing guidance directly to existing NTFP-based micro-enterprises. This culturally adaptive digital tool augments informal NTFP livelihood clusters across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, strengthening forest-dependent incomes and women’s enterprise participation.", "self_sustainability_revenue_generation_plan": "Post-grant continuity is anchored in converting the five solar-powered STI Hubs into self-sustaining micro-enterprise centres managed by the 50 certified Knowledge Brokers. With 75% of the ₹2 Crore budget invested in durable capital assets—solar infrastructure, mobile connectivity, and hardware—recurrent costs drop sharply after Year 3. The techno-economic viability rests on a fee-for-service model: voice-enabled health queries, NTFP market linkages, and digital skill certifications generate community revenue, while institutional buy-back arrangements with the MP Tribal Welfare Department and NHM for validated IKS data and last-mile health outreach create anchor subscriptions. This asset-light, broker-owned cooperative structure ensures operational sustainability without perpetual grant dependence.", "target_population_problem_socioeconomic_status": "The accelerating oral-only transmission loss among the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is erasing the community’s primary healthcare system. Knowledge of over 200 medicinal plant species is held by approximately 200 aging traditional healers, with no systematic documentation completed since 1995. The cost of inaction is direct: 70 percent of the 150,000 Baiga community members live below the poverty line, and as elder healers pass away without apprentices, curative knowledge is declining at an estimated ten percent annually, leaving families with no accessible health alternative in remote forest settlements [CITE:Shirisha2019].\\n\\nThe Baiga economy remains entirely forest-based, reliant on non-timber forest produce (NTFP) with no major manufacturing or industrial base as confirmed by SFURTI and ASPIRE district profiles. In Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, nutrition insecurity and health disparities are severe, compounded by low literacy and the near-total absence of culturally intelligible digital health resources. Existing interventions—fragmented NGO plant catalogues and centralised text-heavy databases such as the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library—are static, text-dependent, and structurally inaccessible to an oral-first culture. No existing tool provides an interactive, voice-based, bilingual retrieval system adapted to Baiga epistemological frameworks.\\n\\nThis proposal is justified now because the intersection of field readiness, open-source AI capacity, and institutional partnerships makes a scalable S&T response achievable for the first time. Our fieldwork and prior household surveys, triangulated with DTWO census 2022 and NRLM 2023 data, confirm that youth are willing knowledge brokers if given voice-driven digital apprenticeship pathways that bridge the literacy-technology gap. Without immediate digitization and active transmission infrastructure, this irreplaceable biocultural heritage will be lost within a generation.", "involvement_and_participation_of_target_population": "Improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 Baiga members is delivered via 50 Knowledge Brokers (60% women, ages 18–30) who serve as the primary interface for oral health documentation, household surveys, and app dissemination. Year 1 surveys cover Bichhiya and Mawai blocks; by Year 3, the voice-enabled app provides query resolution, and all 50 Brokers earn digital employment certifications. PRA-based mobilization, train-the-trainer workshops at five solar hubs, and monthly Health Tech Melas co-led with the MP Tribal Welfare Department ensure sustained participation. Quarterly surveys verify 90% AI accuracy, building a scalable model for other PVTGs.", "part_3_problem_identification_and_proposed_st_interventions": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population of approximately 150,000 spread across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—is losing an estimated 10% of its ethnomedicinal knowledge every year. The last systematic documentation of Baiga healing traditions was conducted in 1995; since then, nearly 200 remaining traditional healers have continued to practice exclusively through oral transmission, with no scalable, culturally-sensitive tool to capture or share this knowledge. Seventy percent of Baiga households live below the poverty line, and with youth migration accelerating and the traditional apprenticeship chain broken, the community’s biocultural heritage faces a silent collapse that also deepens the primary healthcare access gap in one of India’s most marginalized regions.\\n\\nExisting responses are structurally inadequate for an oral-first, low-literacy community. Static digital repositories—whether national initiatives like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library or fragmented NGO catalogues—remain text-heavy, English-indexed archives that cannot be accessed by Baiga youth or healers. Even recent ethnobotanical inventories that confirm the richness of Baiga medicinal plant use [CITE:JHARIYA2024] sit as printed or PDF records, inert and unavailable for real-time health queries. None of these approaches bridge the literacy–technology gap, offer vernacular voice interaction, or provide adaptive learning pathways that keep indigenous knowledge alive as a living transmission, not a museum artifact. Without a tool that speaks the community’s language and adapts to its oral culture, the knowledge will continue to vanish, and the promised benefits of digital empowerment will bypass the very people they are meant to reach.\\n\\nA new angle is now possible—and urgently justified—by the convergence of social need and scientific readiness. Advances in parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) for low-resource languages, using methods like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on open-source large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated that it is feasible to build high-accuracy bilingual systems from small, domain-specific oral corpora [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025]. Furthermore, fine-tuning LLMs on localized healthcare datasets has already proven effective in bridging medical information gaps for underserved populations [CITE:Bui2025]. Capitalizing on these breakthroughs, our proposed S&T intervention will fine-tune an open-source LLM (e.g., Llama) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral IK—complemented by vernacular audio recordings—to generate bilingual (local language and English) adaptive learning modules delivered through a voice-enabled mobile app. Unlike static PDF archives, the system provides query-based retrieval and adaptive learning: a community member can ask a spoken health question and receive a context-aware, healer-validated answer, while youth Knowledge Brokers use the same platform to deepen their own learning and earn certifications. This transitions indigenous knowledge from passive preservation to active, community-owned transmission.\\n\\nThe intervention is designed to be offline-first, solar-powered, and anchored in five physical STI Hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai, making it resilient to connectivity gaps. Fifty trained Baiga youth (50% female, aged 18–30) will act as Knowledge Brokers, collecting oral recordings in Year 1 while simultaneously building capacity through a digital apprenticeship model. By Year 3, the validated IKS database will power a scalable, query-capable tool that reaches 15,000 community users, directly improves primary health query resolution, and creates employment-ready certifications for tribal youth. This approach advances beyond any existing digital archive by embedding indigenous knowledge in an interactive, voice-first system that strengthens both cultural continuity and economic opportunity, aligning with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme and the National Health Mission’s tribal outreach, while fulfilling the STI Hubs mandate for science-and-technology-driven socio-economic transformation of PVTG communities."}
cmoi872qv00br40wtxpm5t0s9	cmodzbetg008nxrslnjerg39y	1	Grant: STI Hubs	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	PROJECT SUMMARY\n\nThis proposal establishes a Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI) Hub to address critical livelihood challenges for 2,000 marginalized women Phulkari artisans in Jalandhar, Punjab. The current manual production methods create significant output restrictions and low wages (under ₹5,000/month), leading to artisans abandoning the craft. Our S&T intervention, sourced from in-house adaptive R&D, introduces context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools like ergonomic winders and eco-friendly dye baths, designed to boost production by 50% and double wages without compromising traditional aesthetics. These tools are complemented by standardized jig systems for motif alignment, low-cost frame stabilizers to reduce hand strain, and modular drying racks that shorten dye-to-embroidery cycle time while maintaining colorfastness. Process improvements are paired with quality assurance protocols—such as thread tensile testing and shade-card referencing—to ensure market-ready finishes aligned with heritage design standards.\nTechnology adoption and artisan mobilization are facilitated through existing Self-Help Groups (SHGs), ensuring immediate trust and effective uptake, with SHG leaders actively participating in the Hub Steering Committee for genuine artisan governance. The Hub will deploy on-site demonstration units, peer-led training cohorts, and a train-the-trainer model, supported by vernacular SOPs, safety modules, and micro-maintenance toolkits to minimize downtime and ensure continuity. A digital micro-asset registry will track tool usage, piece-rate productivity, and income gains, enabling real-time performance dashboards for SHGs and the Steering Committee. Market enablement will include design co-creation labs with local designers, small-batch sampling for buyer validation, and e-commerce onboarding through curated artisan storefronts, thereby diversifying sales channels beyond local intermediaries.\nThis project directly synergizes with the MSME SFURTI scheme to augment existing textile micro-clusters, aligning with DST priorities for socio-economic development and generational preservation of the Phulkari craft. The Hub will leverage common facility centers under SFURTI, integrate green dye waste management and water-recycling measures, and introduce carbon- and water-use baselines to quantify environmental co-benefits. A robust monitoring and evaluation framework will measure outcomes on productivity, income, work hours, occupational health, and youth engagement, with grievance redressal built into SHG governance. The expected payoff is enhanced livelihoods, capacity building, and a scalable blueprint for community-managed technology adoption in MSME clusters, contributing to sustainable development. Over a 24-month timeline, we anticipate measurable increases in unit throughput, stabilized income streams via bulk orders and repeat buyers, and documented skill advancement, creating a replicable model for other embroidery traditions while safeguarding cultural integrity and artisan agency.\n\nPROPOSED OBJECTIVES\n\nThis proposal aims to achieve the following core objectives: 1) Design and validate context-appropriate, semi-mechanized yarn preparation tools and eco-friendly, temperature-controlled dye baths specifically for the Phulkari craft. 2) Establish a centralized STI Hub in Jalandhar to serve as a nexus for technology adoption, training, and knowledge dissemination for 2,000 marginalized women artisans. 3) Enhance the socio-economic empowerment of these artisans by increasing their average monthly income by 50% and fostering generational preservation of the Phulkari heritage through S&T interventions. 4) Develop a scalable and sustainable model for technology transfer and artisan governance within MSME micro-clusters, aligning with national initiatives like SFURTI. Success will be recognized through the quantifiable adoption of new technologies, demonstrable income increases, the successful operation of the Hub, and the documented preservation of Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) related to the craft.\n\nSOURCE OF TECHNOLOGY\n\nThe proposed technology, specifically semi-mechanized yarn processing tools and eco-friendly dyeing systems, is sourced directly from in-house adaptive R&D conducted at Lovely Professional University (LPU) . This approach ensures that the technologies are context-appropriate, addressing the specific needs and constraints of the Phulkari artisan community in Jalandhar. LPU’s established expertise in developing and validating technologies for rural and MSME sectors underpins the credibility of this source. The adaptive R&D process inherently involves iterative design and testing within comparable settings, mitigating the risk of a top-down R&D disconnect often seen in technology transfer models . This in-house approach allows for the fine-tuning of technologies like ergonomic winders and temperature-controlled dye baths to preserve the traditional Phulkari aesthetic patterns while enhancing productivity and reducing occupational hazards. The validation of these technologies through participatory action research (PAR) and community-managed demonstration units within the Jalandhar micro-clusters further solidifies their feasibility and suitability for widespread adoption by 2,000 artisans.\n\nPROPOSED SUBJECT AREA\n\nThis proposal establishes a fundable STI Hub for the Phulkari craft, directly addressing the urgent need for technology adoption among 2,000 marginalized women artisans in Punjab to ensure generational preservation and socio-economic upliftment. Our intervention leverages context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools and eco-friendly dyeing processes, delivered through existing Self-Help Groups (SHGs) to foster trust and rapid uptake. This approach aligns with the national MSME SFURTI initiative and will result in a measurable 50% increase in artisan production and doubled wages, thereby enhancing livelihoods and preserving a vital cultural heritage. The explicit inclusion of SHG leaders in the Hub Steering Committee ensures artisan governance, validating a scalable model for community-led technology adoption and socio-economic development within MSME clusters.\n\nBRIEF INDUSTRIAL SCENARIO\n\nThe Phulkari craft sector in Jalandhar's textile micro-clusters faces significant challenges, including low wages (under ₹5,000/month) and manual output restrictions that threaten generational preservation [CITE:Shrivastava2020]. While MSME schemes like SFURTI exist, they often overlook the specific needs of micro-clusters, leading to a technology gap and slow artisan adoption. This proposal’s STI Hub directly addresses this by aligning with existing frameworks and offering context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools, ensuring sustainability and addressing unmet needs within this vital sector.\n\nPOSSIBILITY OF REPLICATION\n\nScalability will be driven by state government partnerships for broad dissemination and market integration via MSME trade fairs. Artisan governance through the Hub Steering Committee ensures continued adoption, paving the way for replication in other MSME clusters and fostering long-term craft preservation.\n\nTECHNOLOGY GAPS AND SOLUTIONS\n\nOur project introduces a differentiated technological intervention by developing and deploying context-appropriate, ergonomic, semi-mechanized yarn winders specifically for the Phulkari craft micro-clusters. This moves beyond existing approaches, which often fail to adequately address the physical strain and output limitations of purely manual yarn preparation [CITE:Pansera2016]. While some initiatives have explored mechanization, fully industrial models are typically unavailable to or destructive of the traditional aesthetics valued by these artisans, creating a significant technology gap [CITE:Shafi2022].\n\nThis differentiation matters because it directly tackles the bottlenecks that restrict productivity and contribute to low wages (under ₹5,000/month), pushing artisans, particularly women, to abandon the craft. By bridging this identified problem of manual processing with a concrete S&T solution, our project aims to achieve a 50% production increase and doubled wages for 2,000 Phulkari women artisans, directly aligning with the call's emphasis on S&T for socio-economic development and livelihood generation. The proposed semi-mechanized winders, alongside eco-friendly dye baths, represent an adaptive R&D approach that enhances output without compromising the heritage and traditional aesthetics of the Phulkari craft.\n\nBACKGROUND OF TARGET COMMUNITY\n\nThe target community comprises approximately 2,000 Phulkari artisan women in the Jalandhar region of Punjab. These women, predominantly from Scheduled Caste (SC) and Other Backward Classes (OBC) communities (70% of the total), alongside 20% General and 10% Economically Weaker Sections (EWS), possess deep-rooted Indigenous Knowledge (IK) and skills in Phulkari embroidery, a craft integral to their cultural heritage. However, their current livelihood conditions are precarious, with average monthly incomes falling below ₹5,000. This low income, stemming from manual, labor-intensive processes in yarn preparation and dyeing that restrict output and productivity, forces many artisans to abandon the craft in search of more sustainable livelihoods, threatening generational preservation. Existing responses, such as broader MSME initiatives, often fail to address the specific technological bottlenecks and ergonomic challenges faced by these micro-cluster artisans, leading to a technology gap and a disconnect between traditional aesthetics and the demands of modern markets. This proposal is justified now to address this urgent need by introducing context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools and eco-friendly processing methods, thereby enhancing productivity, increasing incomes, and ensuring the continuity of the Phulkari heritage. [CITE:Mamidipudi2016] [CITE:Shrivastava2020]\n\nEXISTING SOLUTIONS FOR PROBLEM\n\nExisting regional solutions for the Phulkari craft, primarily found in MSME clusters, rely on fully industrial models that often destroy traditional aesthetics . This approach is unavailable to micro-clusters and fails to address the specific needs of 2,000 marginalized women artisans, leading to slow technology adoption and a risk of abandoning the craft due to low wages. While national initiatives like the MSME SFURTI scheme aim for cluster development, they typically do not offer context-appropriate, semi-mechanized solutions tailored for traditional crafts, creating a significant technology gap. The urgency arises from the potential loss of this invaluable Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) and the socio-economic precarity of the artisans. This proposal is justified now by its focus on developing and disseminating context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools that respect traditional aesthetics, thereby providing a necessary baseline for why a new S&T intervention is required.\n\nRELEVANCE TO EXISTING ENTERPRISES CLUSTERS\n\nThe proposed S&T intervention directly synergizes with existing MSME SFURTI schemes to augment Jalandhar's textile micro-clusters, enhancing livelihood generation for 2,000 Phulkari artisans. .\n\nTARGET POPULATION PROBLEM SOCIOECONOMIC STATUS\n\nThe predominant livelihood for approximately 2,000 Phulkari artisan women in the Jalandhar region is severely hampered by inefficient manual yarn preparation, a critical bottleneck [CITE:Mamidipudi2016]. This reliance on slow, unoptimized manual processes directly restricts output, leading to persistently low wages, often below ₹5,000 per month, forcing many to abandon the craft [CITE:Dash2021]. Existing responses have failed to adequately address these fundamental production limitations within micro-clusters, often proposing fully industrial models that destroy traditional aesthetics or are unavailable to these small-scale MSMEs. This leaves artisans vulnerable to economic instability and jeopardizes the generational preservation of their rich heritage. Our proposal directly addresses this urgent socioeconomic gap by introducing context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools.\n\nPART 3 PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED ST INTERVENTIONS\n\nManual yarn preparation and small-batch dyeing are the binding constraints in the Phulkari value chain in Jalandhar’s textile micro-clusters: they cap daily output, keep monthly earnings for many women artisans under ₹5,000, and accelerate craft abandonment as younger family members see no viable livelihood pathway. The cost of inaction is not only continued low and volatile incomes for 2,000 women artisans (100% women; 70% SC/OBC, 20% General, 10% EWS) but an irreversible erosion of Phulkari as a living Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) as production shifts away from home-based micro-enterprise to either low-paid piecework or complete exit from the craft economy.\n\nThe unmet need is specific and practical. Artisans are not asking for full industrialization; they need context-appropriate productivity improvements that preserve traditional aesthetics, allow home/SHG-based production rhythms, and reduce drudgery and process variability. Yet the dominant response pattern seen in artisan clusters remains fragmented: (i) market-facing support without production bottleneck removal, (ii) “push” technology transfers that do not fit micro-cluster realities, and (iii) isolated equipment placements without governance, training depth, or maintenance pathways. Evidence from innovation studies in traditional craft enterprises consistently flags this gap—innovation improves competitiveness only when it is embedded in adoption systems and matched to local constraints, not treated as a one-time technology drop [CITE:Shafi2022]. In practice, most current interventions under-serve women-led micro-clusters because they are not designed around the weakest links (yarn processing and dye process control) and do not build local capability to sustain the change.\n\nThis proposal’s S&T angle is deliberately different from “generic craft upgradation”: it targets the two highest-friction process steps with semi-mechanized, ergonomic tools and controlled process equipment, and it couples equipment with an STI Hub delivery model that formalizes artisan governance (SHG leaders on the Hub Steering Committee) and creates a repeatable adoption pipeline at scale. The intervention is justified now because (a) the cluster is already organized through SHGs and MSME-aligned micro-clusters, enabling rapid diffusion once the bottleneck is removed; (b) the DST STI Hub instrument can fund the missing middle—adaptive R&D, field validation, equipment deployment, and capacity-building as a single integrated package; and (c) scalable livelihood impact through appropriate, frugal, user-centered technologies is demonstrably feasible in Indian grassroots settings when design is anchored in real constraints and local operating capability [CITE:Pansera2016]. The broader sectoral context also underscores the livelihood stakes: India’s craft-based livelihoods are economically significant and sensitive to productivity and market shocks, making focused, adoption-ready innovation materially consequential for artisan incomes and continuity of practice [CITE:Mamidipudi2016].\n\n### Proposed S&T interventions (problem-to-solution fit)\n\n- Semi-mechanized, ergonomic yarn winders (context-appropriate mechanization): Replace slow, drudgery-intensive manual yarn processing with semi-mechanized winders designed for SHG/shared use, directly addressing the throughput ceiling that limits piece completion rates and suppresses wages.\n- Temperature-controlled, eco-friendly dye baths (process control + quality consistency): Introduce controlled dyeing to reduce batch variability, rework, and quality loss—critical for improving acceptance in MSME supply chains and enabling repeat orders without diluting traditional Phulkari aesthetics.\n- Hub-and-spoke deployment through a centralized STI Hub in Jalandhar: Use a Common Facility Center (CFC)-style hub for demonstration, preventive maintenance routines, and hands-on training while enabling decentralized field validation and SHG-level adoption in micro-clusters—moving beyond one-off distribution to a managed adoption system.\n- Participatory Action Research (PAR) for fit, safety, and uptake: Co-design operating protocols with artisans to ensure usability, safety/ergonomics, and cultural fidelity; iterate based on field feedback to prevent the common failure mode of low utilization after installation.\n- IKS-aligned documentation and skill transfer (preservation through modernization): Document and standardize a small set of traditional dye knowledge and process parameters alongside tool use, so productivity gains do not come at the cost of cultural erasure; this directly supports generational preservation as an explicit long-term outcome.\n\nAcross the 3-year horizon, the near-term, reviewer-testable outcome is straightforward: successful adoption of the semi-mechanized technology by 2,000 artisans, evidenced through training completion and routine use at SHG/micro-cluster level. The longer-term effect is equally clear and aligned to the call: generational preservation of the Phulkari craft by making the livelihood sufficiently productive and dignified to retain and replenish practitioner households rather than lose them.	f	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - POSSIBILITY OF REPLICATION OF PROJECT IN SIMILAR AREAS\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: 2,000 artisans, Technology adoption, Generational 