quivalent filler.\n- Do not list broad impact areas when one measurable outcome would be stronger.\n- Do not claim innovation without naming the comparison point or current practice.\n\nSECTION-TYPE RULES:\n- Lead with the direct answer in the first sentence.\n- Default to one compact response block unless the template clearly requires bullets.\n- Do not add gratuitous headings, framing, or essay-style buildup.\n- Stay tightly inside any word or character limit.\n\nEVIDENCE DEPLOYMENT:\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "prove burden", pair it with the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "show gap", use it to name the limitation, barrier, or unmet need.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "validate approach", use it to justify the method, metric, or evaluation design.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "prove feasibility", use it to show comparable implementation success.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "quantify impact", attach it to the concrete outcome metric or effect size.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "establish precedent" or "show policy fit", make the comparison or framework explicit in the sentence.\n\nPREP USAGE RULES:\n- Section-mapped covered Grant Prep points are authoritative for this section.\n- Keep related-section awareness secondary; use it only to maintain consistency or avoid contradiction.\n- Do not escalate awareness-only material into a new promise, metric, milestone, or compliance claim.\n- When mapped evidence is provided, attach only the allowed citation anchors to the claims they support.\n\nCONSISTENCY RULES:\n- Keep terminology, beneficiaries, objectives, and delivery logic consistent with the broader proposal.\n- Reuse the same naming for work packages, outputs, and institutions when they already appear in prior context.\n- Prefer reviewer trust, feasibility clarity, and scoring alignment over academic flourish.\n\nREVIEWER POLISH RULES:\n- Preserve supported facts, required points, named deliverables, and mandatory citation anchors.\n- Improve scanability, credibility, and funder alignment inside the first draft rather than saving polish for a later pass.\n- Tighten vague language, foreground feasibility, and keep the section directly aligned to the call logic.\n- Do not rewrite the section into a journal article, literature review, or generic policy memo.\n\nPERSUASIVE PROSE RULES:\n- Write like an experienced grant writer, not an AI assistant.\n- Vary sentence length and structure; avoid rhythmic, repetitive cadence.\n- Lead paragraphs with analytical claims or reviewer-relevant judgments, not generic description.\n- Replace mechanical transitions such as "Furthermore" or "Additionally" with logical movement grounded in the argument.\n- Prefer one concrete comparison, benchmark, statistic, or implementation detail over several generic sentences.\n- Cut filler, throat-clearing, and empty emphasis such as "it is important to note" or "X is a growing challenge".\n- Match confidence to evidence strength; do not overclaim beyond what the cited support can carry.\n- Every paragraph should increase reviewer confidence in significance, feasibility, or delivery credibility.\n\nBUDGET DISCIPLINE:\n- Target length: stay within 50 words.\n- Fit the budget in one pass by compressing repetition, generic setup, and optional detail before cutting core reviewer-facing evidence.\n- Keep the strongest quantitative facts, feasibility proof, and required compliance content.\n- Do not pad to sound polished and do not exceed the stated limits just to preserve every sentence.\n- Never invent new claims, numbers, or citation anchors while tightening to budget.\n\nCITATION ANCHOR RULES:\n- Preserve any mapped [CITE:key] anchors exactly where they support surviving claims.\n- Do not fabricate new citation anchors or attach an anchor to a claim the source does not support.\n- If a sentence is removed, remove only the anchors that belonged exclusively to that deleted claim.\n\nGRANT RULES:\nFormat constraints:\n- Target approximately 50 words.\n\nNarrative constraints:\n- After the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nGRANT SECTION COMPLIANCE CONTRACT:\n\nFunding-call summary:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Near-term outcomes: Near-term outcome is the successful adoption of the semi-mechanized technology by 2,000 artisans. | Rule note: Provides a concrete, quantifiable near-term result of the training and deployment phases.\n- Longer-term effect: Long-term outcome is the generational preservation of the Phulkari craft. ; Aims to scale the successful STI Hub model to other regional MSME clusters post-project. | Rule note: Strong alignment with DST's focus on scalable socio-economic development and IKS preservation.\n- Target Beneficiaries: Target population is exclusively 100% female Phulkari artisans (0 male/transgender). ; Demographic breakdown is exactly 70% SC/OBC, 20% General, and 10% EWS. | Rule note: Strictly fulfills the reviewer requirement to provide category-wise and gender-wise percentage distributions.\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area: Target population participates formally through SHG leaders serving directly on the Hub Steering Committee. ; Artisan voices will actively govern technology adoption and daily hub operations. | Rule note: Provides a concrete mechanism for community involvement, shifting from passive beneficiaries to active governance.\n- Focus area: science and technology\n- Focus area: socio-economic development\n- Focus area: capacity enhancement\n- Focus area: sustainable development\n\nRequired points:\n- after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n- Comparative advantage or precedent for how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nReviewer signals:\n- after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nFormat constraints:\n- Target approximately 50 words.\n\nNarrative constraints:\n- after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nTemplate guidance:\nReviewer goal: after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\nGuidance text:\n- after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\nTemplate-required facts:\n- after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nPrep evidence:\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area: Target population participates formally through SHG leaders serving directly on the Hub Steering Committee. ; Artisan voices will actively govern technology adoption and daily hub operations. | Rule notes: Provides a concrete mechanism for community involvement, shifting from passive beneficiaries to active governance.\n- PUBLICITY, PROMOTION & DISSEMINATION PLAN: Publicity and promotion will be heavily driven through MSME trade fairs to showcase the S&T-enhanced Phulkari products and validated dye recipes. | Rule notes: Fulfills the requirement for a clear dissemination plan using established industrial platforms.\n- Others: Operational 'Other' funds prioritize Participatory Action Research (PAR) field testing. ; Funds also cover MSME trade fair publicity and specific documentation of IKS recipes. | Rule notes: Directly addresses the allowable 'Others' categories like Field Testing, Technology Dissemination, Documentation, and Publicity.\n\nHard checks:\n- Template required fact 1: after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n- Section word budget: Do not exceed 50 words.\n\nSoft checks:\n- Reviewer signal 1: after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n- Narrative constraint 1: after the proposed technological solution is proven, how it will be scaled up or taken forward- whether by involving State Government for large scale technology dissemination or via market or any other means – any entrepreneur or business person can be involved in any manner?\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- Treat these as the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Stage families: outcomes, budget_strategy\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area | Target population participates formally through SHG leaders serving directly on the Hub Steering Committee. ; Artisan voices will actively govern technology adoption and daily hub operations. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: Provides a concrete mechanism for community involvement, shifting from passive beneficiaries to active governance.\n- PUBLICITY, PROMOTION & DISSEMINATION PLAN | Publicity and promotion will be heavily driven through MSME trade fairs to showcase the S&T-enhanced Phulkari products and validated dye recipes. | Thrust linkage: Technology Dissemination, Marketing | Rule note: Fulfills the requirement for a clear dissemination plan using established industrial platforms.\n- Others | Operational 'Other' funds prioritize Participatory Action Research (PAR) field testing. ; Funds also cover MSME trade fair publicity and specific documentation of IKS recipes. | Thrust linkage: Technology Dissemination, Documentation | Rule note: Directly addresses the allowable 'Others' categories like Field Testing, Technology Dissemination, Documentation, and Publicity.\n- Keywords: SHG leaders, Hub Steering Committee, Artisan governance, Technology adoption, Capacity enhancement, MSME trade fair publicity, Dissemination, Technology Dissemination, Marketing, PAR field testing, IKS recipe documentation, Documentation\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: budget_strategy, outcomes\n- Budget overview | Total proposed budget ceiling is ₹2 Crore. ; Allocates 50% of the total budget to capital infrastructure and equipment. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure, Equipment Grant | Rule note: Provides a clear proportional breakdown that reviewers look for to assess techno-economic feasibility.\n- Equipment cost | Allocates ₹1 Crore (50% of total budget) combined for Equipment and low-cost Hub setup. | Thrust linkage: Equipment Grant | Rule note: Heavily weights the budget towards tangible deliverables as preferred by DST.\n- Fabrication/ installation cost | Fabrication costs allocated at ₹60 Lakhs for 50 semi-mechanized winders and 10 temperature-controlled dye baths. ; Installation costs allocated at ₹40 Lakhs for setting up the central Hub Common Facility Centre (CFC). | Thrust linkage: Equipment priority, Capital Infrastructure | Rule note: Provides the specific bill of materials/estimate breakdown required for fabrication and development costs.\n- Construction cost | Construction budget strictly limited to low-cost Hub (Common Facility Centre) setup, combined into the ₹1 Crore capital allocation. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Complies with DST restriction limiting construction strictly to low-cost structures like CFC/refurbishment.\n- Keywords: ₹2 Crore Total, 50% Equipment, 20% Staff, 10% Consumables, 20% Overheads / Travel / Contingency, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant, ₹1 Crore\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: 2,000 artisans, Technology adoption, Generational preservation, Phulkari craft, MSME clusters, Scale, 100% Women, 0 Male / Transgender, 70% SC / OBC, 20% Gen, 10% EWS, SHG leaders, Hub Steering Committee, Artisan governance, MSME trade fair publicity, Dissemination, Year 1, Year 2, Year 3, R&D, Hub, Mass training, 5 IKS dye recipes, 5 SHG pilots, 50 master trainers, 50% production gains, Wage increases, 3 years, 6 milestones per year, Quantitative output, Socio - economic impact, 50% production increase, Doubled wages, Baseline / endline socio - economic surveys, PSRLM, Quarterly SHG production logs, Impact indicators, Wage growth, STI capacity building, IPR, 5 documented IKS recipes, Top - down R&D disconnect, Scale mismatch, Capital constraints, Micro - clusters, Tech - transfer models, Push models, Co - design absence, Traditional aesthetics, Centralized STI Hub, Jalandhar, Semi - Mechanized, Eco - Friendly Processing Tools, Doubling Wages, 2,000 Phulkari Women Artisans, Traditional Heritage, Sweet Spot, DST Priorities, 2,000 Marginalized Women, S&T Approach, Mechanizes Bottleneck, Yarn Prep, IKS, Phulkari Heritage, Socio - Economic Development, IKS Preservation, STI Hub, Socio - Economic Upliftment, 2,000 Phulkari Artisan Women, Semi - Mechanized Yarn Processing, Eco - Friendly Dyeing, Livelihood Generation, Phulkari Artisans, Semi - Mechanized Winders, Jalandhar Punjab, Participatory Action Research, Hub - and - spoke model, Sequential tech - transfer, Community - managed demonstration units, Year 1 R&D, Year 2 Hub Setup, Year 3 Training, Documentation, IKS documentation, Jalandhar cluster, MSME micro - clusters, LPU, Host Institution, Jalandhar micro - clusters, Decentralized field - validation, Punjab Directorate of MSME / Textiles, Punjab State Rural Livelihoods Mission (PSRLM), Market linkages, State - level dissemination, SHG demonstration, PERT chart, Logical Framework Approach (LFA), Gantt chart, Implementation tracking, e - PMS portal, DST, Online submission, 2,000 direct beneficiaries, Name of Institute, Artisan families, Local yarn suppliers, Dye suppliers, Regional textile ecosystem, Jalandhar Hub, Deep community ties, MSME cluster, Capacity enhancement, DST priority, Adaptive R&D, Hub setup, Training scale - up, Sequential timeline, MSME SFURTI scheme, Self - Help Groups (SHGs), Mobilize artisans, Immediate trust, In - house adaptive R&D, [Name of Institute], Textile clusters, Augmentation, Relative timeline, Year 1, 2, 3, Sanction dependent, Capital Equipment, Year 3 Stipends, 2 Crore INR, Administrative Costs, Travel Costs, 10% Cap, Equipment Priority, Zero International Travel, Domestic Travel Only, ₹2 Crore Total, 50% Equipment, 20% Staff, 10% Consumables, 20% Overheads / Travel / Contingency, ₹1 Crore, Equipment, ₹60 Lakhs, 50 semi - mechanized winders, 10 dye baths, ₹40 Lakhs, Hub CFC installation, Low - Cost Hub, CFC Setup, ₹40L, Project Staff, PI, Hub Manager, ₹20L, Consumables, Contingency, Split Allocation, Domestic Travel, PAR field testing, IKS recipe documentation, Institutional Overheads, Slow artisan adoption, PAR methodology, Hub delays, Relative 3 - year timeline, Master Trainer attrition, Socio - economic incentives, Operational structure, LPU PI, Punjab MSME Directorate, Occupational hazards, Ergonomic compliance, Zero - liquid discharge, Environmental compliance, IP theft, Traditional knowledge frameworks, Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS), Phulkari aesthetic patterns, organic dye recipes, cultural preservation, heritage preservation, S&T upgrades, no cultural erasure, Manual yarn preparation, Dyeing, Restricts output, Low wages, Under ₹5,000 / month, Abandoning craft, S&T solution gap, Traditional methods preservation, Ergonomic winders, Dye baths, Phulkari, Phulkari artisan women, Under ₹5,000, Manual output restrictions, Jalandhar District Industrial Profile, Textile micro - clusters, MSME SFURTI schemes, Fully industrial models, Destroy traditional aesthetics, Unavailable to micro - clusters, Technology gap, Context - appropriate tools, Ergonomic, Semi - mechanized yarn winders, Temperature - controlled, Eco - friendly dye baths, Eco - friendly processing, Increase average monthly income, 50%, MSME cooperative, Post - grant, Phulkari preservation model, Cooperative model, Master trainers, Usage fee, Buy - back agreements, MSME cooperative model, Scalable blueprint, State backing, Regional textile clusters\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Shafi2022] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Provides a systematic review of innovation in traditional handicraft companies, linking innovation to sustainable development and competitiveness. | strength: weak | stance: neutral | role: establish precedent | method: Systematic literature review with keyword-based search and analysis. | use: Use this citation to compare against current practice or precedent.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nFrameworks for replication of successful STI Hubs in different craft clusters, Role of state government agencies in large-scale dissemination of proven rural technologies, Market-driven approaches for scaling up artisan-focused technological solutions\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	{"output": "Scalability will be driven by state government partnerships for broad dissemination and market integration via MSME trade fairs. Artisan governance through the Hub Steering Committee ensures continued adoption, paving the way for replication in other MSME clusters and fostering long-term craft preservation.", "sectionKey": "possibility_of_replication"}	50	2026-04-28 06:09:04.422	2026-04-29 05:01:18.592	\N	PAPER	{"methodology": "", "project_title": "", "project_summary": "This proposal establishes a Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI) Hub to address critical livelihood challenges for 2,000 marginalized women Phulkari artisans in Jalandhar, Punjab. The current manual production methods create significant output restrictions and low wages (under ₹5,000/month), leading to artisans abandoning the craft. Our S&T intervention, sourced from in-house adaptive R&D, introduces context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools like ergonomic winders and eco-friendly dye baths, designed to boost production by 50% and double wages without compromising traditional aesthetics. These tools are complemented by standardized jig systems for motif alignment, low-cost frame stabilizers to reduce hand strain, and modular drying racks that shorten dye-to-embroidery cycle time while maintaining colorfastness. Process improvements are paired with quality assurance protocols—such as thread tensile testing and shade-card referencing—to ensure market-ready finishes aligned with heritage design standards.\\nTechnology adoption and artisan mobilization are facilitated through existing Self-Help Groups (SHGs), ensuring immediate trust and effective uptake, with SHG leaders actively participating in the Hub Steering Committee for genuine artisan governance. The Hub will deploy on-site demonstration units, peer-led training cohorts, and a train-the-trainer model, supported by vernacular SOPs, safety modules, and micro-maintenance toolkits to minimize downtime and ensure continuity. A digital micro-asset registry will track tool usage, piece-rate productivity, and income gains, enabling real-time performance dashboards for SHGs and the Steering Committee. Market enablement will include design co-creation labs with local designers, small-batch sampling for buyer validation, and e-commerce onboarding through curated artisan storefronts, thereby diversifying sales channels beyond local intermediaries.\\nThis project directly synergizes with the MSME SFURTI scheme to augment existing textile micro-clusters, aligning with DST priorities for socio-economic development and generational preservation of the Phulkari craft. The Hub will leverage common facility centers under SFURTI, integrate green dye waste management and water-recycling measures, and introduce carbon- and water-use baselines to quantify environmental co-benefits. A robust monitoring and evaluation framework will measure outcomes on productivity, income, work hours, occupational health, and youth engagement, with grievance redressal built into SHG governance. The expected payoff is enhanced livelihoods, capacity building, and a scalable blueprint for community-managed technology adoption in MSME clusters, contributing to sustainable development. Over a 24-month timeline, we anticipate measurable increases in unit throughput, stabilized income streams via bulk orders and repeat buyers, and documented skill advancement, creating a replicable model for other embroidery traditions while safeguarding cultural integrity and artisan agency.", "project_duration": "", "project_keywords": "", "proposed_objectives": "This proposal aims to achieve the following core objectives: 1) Design and validate context-appropriate, semi-mechanized yarn preparation tools and eco-friendly, temperature-controlled dye baths specifically for the Phulkari craft. 2) Establish a centralized STI Hub in Jalandhar to serve as a nexus for technology adoption, training, and knowledge dissemination for 2,000 marginalized women artisans. 3) Enhance the socio-economic empowerment of these artisans by increasing their average monthly income by 50% and fostering generational preservation of the Phulkari heritage through S&T interventions. 4) Develop a scalable and sustainable model for technology transfer and artisan governance within MSME micro-clusters, aligning with national initiatives like SFURTI. Success will be recognized through the quantifiable adoption of new technologies, demonstrable income increases, the successful operation of the Hub, and the documented preservation of Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) related to the craft.", "source_of_technology": "The proposed technology, specifically semi-mechanized yarn processing tools and eco-friendly dyeing systems, is sourced directly from in-house adaptive R&D conducted at Lovely Professional University (LPU) . This approach ensures that the technologies are context-appropriate, addressing the specific needs and constraints of the Phulkari artisan community in Jalandhar. LPU’s established expertise in developing and validating technologies for rural and MSME sectors underpins the credibility of this source. The adaptive R&D process inherently involves iterative design and testing within comparable settings, mitigating the risk of a top-down R&D disconnect often seen in technology transfer models . This in-house approach allows for the fine-tuning of technologies like ergonomic winders and temperature-controlled dye baths to preserve the traditional Phulkari aesthetic patterns while enhancing productivity and reducing occupational hazards. The validation of these technologies through participatory action research (PAR) and community-managed demonstration units within the Jalandhar micro-clusters further solidifies their feasibility and suitability for widespread adoption by 2,000 artisans.", "target_beneficiaries": "", "proposed_subject_area": "This proposal establishes a fundable STI Hub for the Phulkari craft, directly addressing the urgent need for technology adoption among 2,000 marginalized women artisans in Punjab to ensure generational preservation and socio-economic upliftment. Our intervention leverages context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools and eco-friendly dyeing processes, delivered through existing Self-Help Groups (SHGs) to foster trust and rapid uptake. This approach aligns with the national MSME SFURTI initiative and will result in a measurable 50% increase in artisan production and doubled wages, thereby enhancing livelihoods and preserving a vital cultural heritage. The explicit inclusion of SHG leaders in the Hub Steering Committee ensures artisan governance, validating a scalable model for community-led technology adoption and socio-economic development within MSME clusters.", "brief_industrial_scenario": "The Phulkari craft sector in Jalandhar's textile micro-clusters faces significant challenges, including low wages (under ₹5,000/month) and manual output restrictions that threaten generational preservation [CITE:Shrivastava2020]. While MSME schemes like SFURTI exist, they often overlook the specific needs of micro-clusters, leading to a technology gap and slow artisan adoption. This proposal’s STI Hub directly addresses this by aligning with existing frameworks and offering context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools, ensuring sustainability and addressing unmet needs within this vital sector.", "possibility_of_replication": "Scalability will be driven by state government partnerships for broad dissemination and market integration via MSME trade fairs. Artisan governance through the Hub Steering Committee ensures continued adoption, paving the way for replication in other MSME clusters and fostering long-term craft preservation.", "lab_field_testing_locations": "", "other_government_initiatives": "", "proposed_st_delivery_methods": "", "any_other_related_information": "", "technology_gaps_and_solutions": "Our project introduces a differentiated technological intervention by developing and deploying context-appropriate, ergonomic, semi-mechanized yarn winders specifically for the Phulkari craft micro-clusters. This moves beyond existing approaches, which often fail to adequately address the physical strain and output limitations of purely manual yarn preparation [CITE:Pansera2016]. While some initiatives have explored mechanization, fully industrial models are typically unavailable to or destructive of the traditional aesthetics valued by these artisans, creating a significant technology gap [CITE:Shafi2022].\\n\\nThis differentiation matters because it directly tackles the bottlenecks that restrict productivity and contribute to low wages (under ₹5,000/month), pushing artisans, particularly women, to abandon the craft. By bridging this identified problem of manual processing with a concrete S&T solution, our project aims to achieve a 50% production increase and doubled wages for 2,000 Phulkari women artisans, directly aligning with the call's emphasis on S&T for socio-economic development and livelihood generation. The proposed semi-mechanized winders, alongside eco-friendly dye baths, represent an adaptive R&D approach that enhances output without compromising the heritage and traditional aesthetics of the Phulkari craft.", "background_of_target_community": "The target community comprises approximately 2,000 Phulkari artisan women in the Jalandhar region of Punjab. These women, predominantly from Scheduled Caste (SC) and Other Backward Classes (OBC) communities (70% of the total), alongside 20% General and 10% Economically Weaker Sections (EWS), possess deep-rooted Indigenous Knowledge (IK) and skills in Phulkari embroidery, a craft integral to their cultural heritage. However, their current livelihood conditions are precarious, with average monthly incomes falling below ₹5,000. This low income, stemming from manual, labor-intensive processes in yarn preparation and dyeing that restrict output and productivity, forces many artisans to abandon the craft in search of more sustainable livelihoods, threatening generational preservation. Existing responses, such as broader MSME initiatives, often fail to address the specific technological bottlenecks and ergonomic challenges faced by these micro-cluster artisans, leading to a technology gap and a disconnect between traditional aesthetics and the demands of modern markets. This proposal is justified now to address this urgent need by introducing context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools and eco-friendly processing methods, thereby enhancing productivity, increasing incomes, and ensuring the continuity of the Phulkari heritage. [CITE:Mamidipudi2016] [CITE:Shrivastava2020]", "existing_solutions_for_problem": "Existing regional solutions for the Phulkari craft, primarily found in MSME clusters, rely on fully industrial models that often destroy traditional aesthetics . This approach is unavailable to micro-clusters and fails to address the specific needs of 2,000 marginalized women artisans, leading to slow technology adoption and a risk of abandoning the craft due to low wages. While national initiatives like the MSME SFURTI scheme aim for cluster development, they typically do not offer context-appropriate, semi-mechanized solutions tailored for traditional crafts, creating a significant technology gap. The urgency arises from the potential loss of this invaluable Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) and the socio-economic precarity of the artisans. This proposal is justified now by its focus on developing and disseminating context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools that respect traditional aesthetics, thereby providing a necessary baseline for why a new S&T intervention is required.", "proposed_geographical_coverage_area": "", "work_plan_and_expected_deliverables": "", "technical_backup_support_and_linkages": "", "publicity_promotion_dissemination_plan": "", "relevance_to_existing_enterprises_clusters": "The proposed S&T intervention directly synergizes with existing MSME SFURTI schemes to augment Jalandhar's textile micro-clusters, enhancing livelihood generation for 2,000 Phulkari artisans. .", "self_sustainability_revenue_generation_plan": "", "target_population_problem_socioeconomic_status": "The predominant livelihood for approximately 2,000 Phulkari artisan women in the Jalandhar region is severely hampered by inefficient manual yarn preparation, a critical bottleneck [CITE:Mamidipudi2016]. This reliance on slow, unoptimized manual processes directly restricts output, leading to persistently low wages, often below ₹5,000 per month, forcing many to abandon the craft [CITE:Dash2021]. Existing responses have failed to adequately address these fundamental production limitations within micro-clusters, often proposing fully industrial models that destroy traditional aesthetics or are unavailable to these small-scale MSMEs. This leaves artisans vulnerable to economic instability and jeopardizes the generational preservation of their rich heritage. Our proposal directly addresses this urgent socioeconomic gap by introducing context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools.", "involvement_and_participation_of_target_population": "", "part_3_problem_identification_and_proposed_st_interventions": "Manual yarn preparation and small-batch dyeing are the binding constraints in the Phulkari value chain in Jalandhar’s textile micro-clusters: they cap daily output, keep monthly earnings for many women artisans under ₹5,000, and accelerate craft abandonment as younger family members see no viable livelihood pathway. The cost of inaction is not only continued low and volatile incomes for 2,000 women artisans (100% women; 70% SC/OBC, 20% General, 10% EWS) but an irreversible erosion of Phulkari as a living Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) as production shifts away from home-based micro-enterprise to either low-paid piecework or complete exit from the craft economy.\\n\\nThe unmet need is specific and practical. Artisans are not asking for full industrialization; they need context-appropriate productivity improvements that preserve traditional aesthetics, allow home/SHG-based production rhythms, and reduce drudgery and process variability. Yet the dominant response pattern seen in artisan clusters remains fragmented: (i) market-facing support without production bottleneck removal, (ii) “push” technology transfers that do not fit micro-cluster realities, and (iii) isolated equipment placements without governance, training depth, or maintenance pathways. Evidence from innovation studies in traditional craft enterprises consistently flags this gap—innovation improves competitiveness only when it is embedded in adoption systems and matched to local constraints, not treated as a one-time technology drop [CITE:Shafi2022]. In practice, most current interventions under-serve women-led micro-clusters because they are not designed around the weakest links (yarn processing and dye process control) and do not build local capability to sustain the change.\\n\\nThis proposal’s S&T angle is deliberately different from “generic craft upgradation”: it targets the two highest-friction process steps with semi-mechanized, ergonomic tools and controlled process equipment, and it couples equipment with an STI Hub delivery model that formalizes artisan governance (SHG leaders on the Hub Steering Committee) and creates a repeatable adoption pipeline at scale. The intervention is justified now because (a) the cluster is already organized through SHGs and MSME-aligned micro-clusters, enabling rapid diffusion once the bottleneck is removed; (b) the DST STI Hub instrument can fund the missing middle—adaptive R&D, field validation, equipment deployment, and capacity-building as a single integrated package; and (c) scalable livelihood impact through appropriate, frugal, user-centered technologies is demonstrably feasible in Indian grassroots settings when design is anchored in real constraints and local operating capability [CITE:Pansera2016]. The broader sectoral context also underscores the livelihood stakes: India’s craft-based livelihoods are economically significant and sensitive to productivity and market shocks, making focused, adoption-ready innovation materially consequential for artisan incomes and continuity of practice [CITE:Mamidipudi2016].\\n\\n### Proposed S&T interventions (problem-to-solution fit)\\n\\n- Semi-mechanized, ergonomic yarn winders (context-appropriate mechanization): Replace slow, drudgery-intensive manual yarn processing with semi-mechanized winders designed for SHG/shared use, directly addressing the throughput ceiling that limits piece completion rates and suppresses wages.\\n- Temperature-controlled, eco-friendly dye baths (process control + quality consistency): Introduce controlled dyeing to reduce batch variability, rework, and quality loss—critical for improving acceptance in MSME supply chains and enabling repeat orders without diluting traditional Phulkari aesthetics.\\n- Hub-and-spoke deployment through a centralized STI Hub in Jalandhar: Use a Common Facility Center (CFC)-style hub for demonstration, preventive maintenance routines, and hands-on training while enabling decentralized field validation and SHG-level adoption in micro-clusters—moving beyond one-off distribution to a managed adoption system.\\n- Participatory Action Research (PAR) for fit, safety, and uptake: Co-design operating protocols with artisans to ensure usability, safety/ergonomics, and cultural fidelity; iterate based on field feedback to prevent the common failure mode of low utilization after installation.\\n- IKS-aligned documentation and skill transfer (preservation through modernization): Document and standardize a small set of traditional dye knowledge and process parameters alongside tool use, so productivity gains do not come at the cost of cultural erasure; this directly supports generational preservation as an explicit long-term outcome.\\n\\nAcross the 3-year horizon, the near-term, reviewer-testable outcome is straightforward: successful adoption of the semi-mechanized technology by 2,000 artisans, evidenced through training completion and routine use at SHG/micro-cluster level. The longer-term effect is equally clear and aligned to the call: generational preservation of the Phulkari craft by making the livelihood sufficiently productive and dignified to retain and replenish practitioner households rather than lose them."}
cmojyweak00i977ycahp61e38	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	1	Grant: STI Hubs	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	PROJECT SUMMARY\n\nThe Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its oral biocultural health knowledge at an estimated 10% per year—the last systematic documentation was in 1995—while 70% of the community lives below the poverty line and youth outmigration severs traditional apprenticeship chains. This STI Hub project deploys an open-source LLM (Llama) fine-tuned on a validated corpus of transcribed oral Baiga medicinal plant and NTFP knowledge, delivered through a voice-enabled, offline-first mobile app accessible via QR codes at five solar-powered community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. The app bridges the literacy-technology gap in an oral-first culture by enabling query-based retrieval and bilingual adaptive learning modules, advancing far beyond static, text-heavy archives like TKDL.\n\nFifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women, ages 18–30) will be trained and certified as the primary interface for ethnobotanist-supervised household surveys, app dissemination, and hub operations, creating a human-in-the-loop system for data sovereignty and Prior Informed Consent. The intervention directly links to the forest-based livelihoods of the 150,000-strong Baiga population: the app provides sustainable harvesting and processing guidance for NTFP micro-enterprises, improving productivity while preserving indigenous ecological practices. Coordination with the National Health Mission’s tribal programs and alignment with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme ensure non-duplication and inter-ministerial synergy.\n\nBy the end of three years, the project will deliver a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy, five operational STI Hubs, 50 certified youth brokers, and improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 community users. The infrastructure-heavy budget (75% capital assets) ensures post-funding viability, and the model’s offline-first, voice-driven, youth-led architecture is expressly designed for scalability across all 75 PVTGs, making this a high-urgency, high-payoff investment under DST’s STI Hub mandate.\n\nTARGET BENEFICIARIES\n\nThe project will arrest the 10% annual loss of biocultural health knowledge among the Baiga PVTG by deploying an AI-driven, voice-first IKS retrieval platform that gives 15,000 community members (100% ST, 60% female) on-demand primary health guidance while simultaneously certifying 50 tribal youth as Knowledge Brokers – creating the first scalable, culturally embedded digital apprenticeship model for an oral-only culture.\n\n### Demographic profile of the project area.\n\nThe intervention is anchored in 35 predominantly Baiga villages across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh. The total resident population in these villages is approximately 50,000. Scheduled Tribes (Baiga PVTG) constitute 94%, Scheduled Castes 2%, Other Backward Classes 3%, and General category (including EWS) 1%. Women account for roughly 50% of the population.\n\n### Beneficiary coverage (category‑wise and gender‑wise).\n\nThe project is purpose‑built for the Baiga PVTG community and will count only ST individuals as direct and indirect beneficiaries.\n\n| Category | Target to cover (number) | % of total project beneficiaries |\n|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| ST | 15,000 | 100% |\n| SC | 0 | 0% |\n| OBC | 0 | 0% |\n| General | 0 | 0% |\n| EWS | 0 | 0% |\n\nGender‑wise distribution of the 15,000 ST beneficiaries: Female 9,000 (60%), Male 6,000 (40%), Transgender 0. This ratio matches the project’s PRA‑based mobilization strategy, which explicitly reserves 50% of Knowledge Broker positions for women and prioritises women healers in the IKS documentation.\n\n### What changes for beneficiaries, and how it is measured.\n\nImmediate outputs (Years 1‑3). A validated audio IKS database of ~200 healers; a fine‑tuned open‑source LLM powering a bilingual, offline‑first voice app; five solar‑powered STI Hubs with mobile connectivity; and 50 Baiga youth (50% female) trained as Knowledge Brokers through a train‑the‑trainer programme.\n\nBeneficiary outcomes. 15,000 community members gain access to accurate, culturally contextual primary health advice through the app, reducing reliance on distant formal facilities. The 50 certified youth become the backbone of a digital apprenticeship chain, earning stipends and making the oral knowledge system accessible to a generation that was disconnecting from it.\n\nAdoption and deployment pathway. Fifty youth Knowledge Brokers recruited from the target villages conduct household surveys, carry out PRI‑based informed consent, and lead hands‑on app dissemination. Monthly Health Tech Melas at each hub and quarterly regional workshops with the MP Tribal Welfare Department drive sustained uptake. A Hub Advisory Circle of healers, ethnobotanists, and community representatives ensures data sovereignty and correct cultural framing.\n\nEvidence of impact. Project performance is tracked through quarterly surveys and offline‑first usage logs. The primary benchmark is 90% AI response accuracy on health queries, validated by a human‑in‑the‑loop panel of healers. Secondary metrics include the number of active monthly app users (target 15,000 by end of Year 3), the 50 youth certifications awarded, and a documented reduction in the pre‑project 10% annual healer‑knowledge loss – measured by repeat audio ethnography and healer attestation.\n\nLonger‑term value. Beyond the grant period, the five hubs become permanent community‑owned knowledge centres, sustaining the app, refreshing content, and offering digital services. Because the model is deliberately modular – voice AI fine‑tuning for an oral culture, solar‑powered offline infrastructure, youth‑broker delivery – it can be replicated for other PVTGs under the same DST STI Hub framework, turning the Mandla demonstration into a national blueprint for active IKS transmission.\n\nPROPOSED SUBJECT AREA\n\nThe Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh—70% below the poverty line, with an entirely oral medicinal knowledge system—is losing its biocultural heritage at an estimated 10% annually. The last systematic documentation was in 1995, and fewer than 200 healers remain, making this one of India’s most urgent indigenous knowledge preservation challenges. This proposal establishes an AI-driven STI Hub that digitizes oral indigenous knowledge (IK) into a validated, voice-first mobile application and trains 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers to operate it, directly improving primary health query resolution for 15,000 community members and creating a scalable digital capacity model for PVTGs.\n\nThe intervention replaces static, text-heavy archives—inaccessible in a low-literacy, oral-first culture—with an open-source large language model (Llama) fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga audio, verified by ethnobotanists and healers under prior informed consent. The resulting bilingual, voice-enabled app delivers query-based adaptive learning and sustainable harvesting guidance for non-timber forest products (NTFPs), accessible via QR codes placed in solar-powered community hubs. Blended in-person training through a train-the-trainer model, complemented by monthly Health Tech Melas and quarterly household surveys, ensures adoption even in areas with intermittent connectivity through offline-first logs and human-in-the-loop validation.\n\nOver three years, the hub will produce a validated IKS database, five operational community knowledge hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female), and employment‑ready certification aligned with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme. The approach directly links to the National Health Mission’s tribal programs, leverages existing all-weather road access and mobile penetration, and strengthens NTFP‑based micro-enterprises with actionable processing knowledge, reinforcing the Vocal for Local and livelihoods thrust of the STI Hubs call. With an infrastructure‑heavy budget that prioritizes capital assets (75%) and local manpower (30%), the hub is designed for post‑funding viability through community brokers and hub advisory circles.\n\nThe payoff is not merely an archive but an active transmission system: 15,000 Baiga community members gain improved primary healthcare access, 50 youth gain durable digital skills, and the model generates a validated, sovereignty‑respecting blueprint that can be scaled across India’s other PVTGs. For the Department of Science and Technology’s STI Hubs scheme, this offers a high‑social‑urgency, technology‑forward investment that bridges the literacy‑technology gap, arrests irreversible knowledge loss, and builds lasting digital capacity in a population with no alternative scalable tool.\n\nBACKGROUND OF TARGET COMMUNITY\n\nThe Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—approximately 150,000 people—faces an acute, accelerating crisis of biocultural knowledge loss, compounded by severe socioeconomic deprivation and a near-total absence of culturally appropriate health and livelihood delivery systems. An estimated 200 traditional healers—the sole custodians of a rich, orally transmitted pharmacopoeia—are aging, and the rate of knowledge attrition is estimated at 10 % per year. The last systematic documentation of this knowledge was conducted in 1995; since then, only fragmented NGO catalogues have been produced, none of which are accessible or usable for the community’s youth. What is being lost is not a static archive but an active system of primary healthcare upon which the majority of Baiga families still depend, given that forest-based livelihoods and remote geography leave formal health services perpetually out of reach.\n\nSocioeconomically, the community is among India’s most marginalised. Seventy per cent of the Baiga population lives below the poverty line (NRLM 2023), with an economy built almost entirely on minor forest produce collection, subsistence agriculture, and negligible wage labour. Malnutrition and health inequities are pervasive, as documented by qualitative research on Baiga children’s nutritional disparities [CITE:Shirisha2019]. Literacy rates remain low, and digital access is constrained by intermittent mobile connectivity and erratic electricity—yet solar-powered mobile phones are increasingly common, creating a narrow but real window for technology delivery. The working-age population, particularly those aged 18–30, confronts a broken apprenticeship chain: elders’ knowledge is not being transmitted, and youth out-migration rises as local opportunities shrink. This double failure—lost heritage and lost livelihoods—is the core unmet need.\n\nExisting responses across India have been structurally mismatched to an oral-first culture. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library and similar UNESCO-linked repositories are text-heavy, static archives designed for patent protection, not for community use. They assume literacy, ignore vernacular audio, and offer no mechanism for interactive query, adaptive learning, or youth engagement. Earlier documentation projects in the region produced paper-based or inert digital catalogues that sit unused because they cannot be queried in Baiga, cannot respond to voice, and cannot evolve as new knowledge is shared. What is missing is a scalable, culturally sensitive tool that bridges the literacy–technology gap and transforms passive archiving into active, intergenerational transmission—precisely the gap this proposal fills.\n\nThe present moment justifies an S&T-led intervention on three grounds. First, the convergence of open-source large language models (LLMs), offline-first mobile architectures, and falling solar costs makes it feasible to build a voice-enabled, bilingual AI system that can operate directly in Baiga and Hindi without continuous connectivity. Second, national policy frameworks—NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indigenous Knowledge Systems, MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme, and DST’s own STI Hub mandate for PVTG scalability—create an enabling environment that did not exist even five years ago. Third, the Baiga community’s own readiness is demonstrable: over 200 healers continue to practise and hold the knowledge [CITE:JHARIYA2024], and there is a cadre of underemployed youth who, with training, can serve as Knowledge Brokers, earning stipends while rebuilding the apprenticeship chain. Inaction at this juncture means that within a generation, an irreplaceable biocultural resource—uniquely adapted to the forest ecosystem of central India—will be permanently erased, leaving 150,000 people poorer in health, identity, and economic possibility.\n\nTARGET POPULATION PROBLEM SOCIOECONOMIC STATUS\n\nThe Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, faces an escalating healthcare and livelihood crisis rooted in the oral-only transmission of their Indigenous Knowledge (IK) system. With 70% of the community living below the poverty line, the ecosystem of ~200 traditional healers constitutes the de facto primary healthcare resource for 150,000 people across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—a population already burdened by documented socioeconomic and nutritional disparities [CITE:Shirisha2019]. Yet this knowledge base is collapsing at an estimated 10% per year as healers die without apprentices, severing the intergenerational chain and erasing centuries of validated ethnobotanical practice. The District Tribal Welfare Office census (2022) and NRLM livelihood system analysis (2023) confirm a structural gap: no systematic documentation has occurred since 1995. Existing repositories like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library remain static, text-heavy archives fundamentally inaccessible to an oral-first culture with low literacy. Fragmented NGO catalogues sit unused, and youth migration accelerates as forest-based livelihoods degrade without sustainable harvesting guidance. No scalable, culturally sensitive tool currently bridges the literacy-technology gap for real-time knowledge retrieval. This proposal is justified now because the convergence of affordable solar infrastructure, open-source voice AI, and expanding mobile connectivity in Mandla makes oral-first digitization technically feasible for the first time—enabling active transmission before the remaining healers are lost.\n\nPART 3 PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED ST INTERVENTIONS\n\nIn Mandla district’s Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, an estimated 150,000 Baiga people—100% PVTG, 60% women, 70% below the poverty line—depend on orally transmitted medicinal plant knowledge held by roughly 200 traditional healers. That knowledge is vanishing at approximately 10% per year, the last systematic documentation dating to 1995. The result is a double crisis: a rapidly depleting biocultural heritage and a primary healthcare vacuum that falls hardest on women and children. Youth, deterred by a literacy-technology gap and the absence of structured apprenticeship pathways, are migrating out of forest-dependent livelihoods, further breaking the intergenerational chain of knowledge transmission. Existing catalogues remain fragmented across NGO archives, inaccessible in text-heavy formats deeply misaligned with an oral-first culture, and offer no scalable, culturally sensitive retrieval mechanism.\n\nCurrent national and international responses are insufficient. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library and UNESCO repositories excel at static archiving but lack oral-vernacular integration, active query-based retrieval, and bidirectional youth engagement. Community health programs under the National Health Mission have not bridged the last-mile information gap because they are not equipped with tools that understand the Baiga dialect or reflect local ethnomedical reasoning. The absence of an interactive, voice-enabled, adaptive learning platform means that healers’ tacit expertise—diagnostic cues, harvesting protocols, dosage norms—remains locked in individual memory, inaccessible during real-time health queries and invisible to the next generation. This is the precise unmet need: a living knowledge system that preserves oral IK in its own modality while simultaneously enabling literate and low-literate users to learn, query, and validate traditional medicine safely.\n\nThe proposed S&T intervention directly addresses this gap by deploying an AI-driven STI Hub built on an open-source large language model (e.g., Llama) fine-tuned with transcribed Baiga IK text and vernacular audio recordings. The model is trained using parameter-efficient methods that have recently proven effective for low-resource language translation [CITE:Khoboko2025][CITE:Liang2025], and the fine-tuning incorporates a domain-specific healthcare dataset to bridge medical information accessibility gaps [CITE:Bui2025]. Unlike static archives, the resulting bilingual (Baiga-Hindi-English) mobile application offers offline-first, voice-driven query-based retrieval and adaptive learning modules. This advances far beyond the existing ethnobotanical inventories, which, while documenting the flora used by the Baiga [CITE:JHARIYA2024], remain inert, text-heavy, and untethered from daily health decisions. The intervention will be validated through a human-in-the-loop pipeline with ethnobotanists and healers, ensuring hallucination risks are managed and data sovereignty is preserved under the Hub Advisory Circles.\n\nThe present moment is uniquely opportune. Open-source LLMs now allow domain-specific, low-resource fine-tuning without prohibitive cost, and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme, the National Health Mission’s tribal outreach, and NEP 2020’s call for IKS integration create inter-ministerial alignment. Solar infrastructure and improving mobile connectivity in Mandla’s forested hamlets make offline-first digital delivery viable. Inaction would mean the irreversible erosion of a knowledge system that has sustained community health for generations; this project transforms that vulnerability into a scalable, youth-led, STI infrastructure for PVTG digital capacity.	f	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - STATE THE TARGET POPULATION RELATED PROBLEM YOU SEEK TO ADDRESS AND BRIEF OF THEIR SOCIO-ECONOMIC STATUS\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n- This section must cover: should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n\nPROMPT PRECEDENCE:\n- Hard call, template, budget, eligibility, length, and compliance rules override all other guidance.\n- Authoritative Grant Prep facts are the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Mapped citation evidence may support only the exact claims it substantiates.\n- Related-section awareness is for consistency only; do not turn it into new commitments, metrics, or compliance claims.\n- Style guidance and user instructions apply only when they do not conflict with the rules above.\n\nSECTION BRIEF:\n- Section: State the target population related problem you seek to address and brief of their Socio-Economic status\n- Purpose: should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n- Reviewer intent: should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n- Word budget: 200\n- Proposal thesis: The proposal establishes a fundable program for science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement by combining a credible delivery model, measurable outcomes, and clear alignment with STI Hubs.\n- Central objective: Prepare PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS.\n- Key contributions: Answer "PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS". | Burden, prevalence, and current state of Indigenous Knowledge Systems. | Answer "Proposed Subject Area".\n- Section writing pointers: should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n- Literature/citation dimensions: Impact of oral-only transmission on the sustainability of traditional knowledge systems | Linkages between loss of Indigenous Knowledge and adverse socio-economic outcomes | Evidence of health and livelihood disparities in the Baiga community\n\nGRANT SECTION PROFILE:\n- Grant semantic: problem need\n- Template intent: problem need\n- Citation mode: mapped evidence\n\nREVIEWER QUESTION:\n- Is this a real, specific, urgent problem that has not been adequately addressed, and is the applicant's angle meaningfully different from the other proposals the reviewer has seen today?\n\nOPENING MOVE:\n- Open with the concrete problem signal, the affected beneficiaries, and the cost of inaction.\n\nRECOMMENDED FLOW:\n- Problem signal and stakes\n- Beneficiaries and unmet need\n- Why existing responses are insufficient\n- Why this proposal is justified now\n\nEVIDENCE USE RULES:\n- Use authoritative section-mapped Grant Prep points as the factual backbone when they are provided.\n- Use related-section awareness only to preserve coherence; do not turn it into new required claims.\n- Prefer specific facts, beneficiaries, milestones, metrics, and feasibility signals over generic prose.\n- Prefer one concrete statistic, comparison, or precedent over several vague background sentences.\n- When mapped evidence is required, use only the allowed citation anchors and keep them attached to the claims they support.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS:\n- Do not slip into journal-manuscript framing or literature-review throat clearing.\n- Do not invent compliance, eligibility, evidence, or implementation detail that is not supported.\n- Do not dump checklist items without narrative logic unless the section type explicitly requires it.\n- Do not open with "X is a growing global challenge" or equivalent filler.\n- Do not list broad impact areas when one measurable outcome would be stronger.\n- Do not claim innovation without naming the comparison point or current practice.\n- Do not use vague importance language without stakes, affected beneficiaries, or concrete urgency signals.\n- Do not spend the opening paragraph on generic textbook background.\n\nSECTION-TYPE RULES:\n- Lead with the direct answer in the first sentence.\n- Default to one compact response block unless the template clearly requires bullets.\n- Do not add gratuitous headings, framing, or essay-style buildup.\n- Stay tightly inside any word or character limit.\n\nSECTION TASK:\n- Establish the problem, urgency, beneficiary need, and why the proposed intervention is justified now.\n\nREVIEWER QUESTION:\n- Is this a real, specific, urgent problem that has not been adequately addressed, and is the applicant's angle meaningfully different from the other proposals the reviewer has seen today?\n\nOPENING MOVE:\n- Open with the concrete problem signal, the affected beneficiaries, and the cost of inaction.\n\nRECOMMENDED FLOW:\n- Problem signal and stakes\n- Beneficiaries and unmet need\n- Why existing responses are insufficient\n- Why this proposal is justified now\n\nEVIDENCE USE RULES:\n- Use authoritative section-mapped Grant Prep points as the factual backbone when they are provided.\n- Use related-section awareness only to preserve coherence; do not turn it into new required claims.\n- Prefer specific facts, beneficiaries, milestones, metrics, and feasibility signals over generic prose.\n- Prefer one concrete statistic, comparison, or precedent over several vague background sentences.\n- When mapped evidence is required, use only the allowed citation anchors and keep them attached to the claims they support.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS:\n- Do not slip into journal-manuscript framing or literature-review throat clearing.\n- Do not invent compliance, eligibility, evidence, or implementation detail that is not supported.\n- Do not dump checklist items without narrative logic unless the section type explicitly requires it.\n- Do not open with "X is a growing global challenge" or equivalent filler.\n- Do not list broad impact areas when one measurable outcome would be stronger.\n- Do not claim innovation without naming the comparison point or current practice.\n- Do not use vague importance language without stakes, affected beneficiaries, or concrete urgency signals.\n- Do not spend the opening paragraph on generic textbook background.\n\nSECTION-TYPE RULES:\n- Lead with the direct answer in the first sentence.\n- Default to one compact response block unless the template clearly requires bullets.\n- Do not add gratuitous headings, framing, or essay-style buildup.\n- Stay tightly inside any word or character limit.\n\nCOMPETITIVE POSITIONING:\n- Do not write a textbook description of the topic. The reviewer has read many proposals like this already.\n- Lead with what makes this framing distinctive: the population, mechanism, implementation setting, or timing.\n- One concrete statistic or comparison is worth more than several generic background sentences.\n- Name what current approaches get wrong or leave unresolved, not just what they fail to mention.\n\nEVIDENCE DEPLOYMENT:\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "prove burden", pair it with the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "show gap", use it to name the limitation, barrier, or unmet need.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "validate approach", use it to justify the method, metric, or evaluation design.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "prove feasibility", use it to show comparable implementation success.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "quantify impact", attach it to the concrete outcome metric or effect size.\n- If the evidence digest labels a citation as "establish precedent" or "show policy fit", make the comparison or framework explicit in the sentence.\n- If no mapped citation supports a needed claim, write cautious uncited prose or flag the claim as an open question; do not invent anchors.\n\nPREP USAGE RULES:\n- Section-mapped covered Grant Prep points are authoritative for this section.\n- Keep related-section awareness secondary; use it only to maintain consistency or avoid contradiction.\n- Do not escalate awareness-only material into a new promise, metric, milestone, or compliance claim.\n- When mapped evidence is provided, attach only the allowed citation anchors to the claims they support.\n\nCONSISTENCY RULES:\n- Keep terminology, beneficiaries, objectives, and delivery logic consistent with the broader proposal.\n- Reuse the same naming for work packages, outputs, and institutions when they already appear in prior context.\n- Prefer reviewer trust, feasibility clarity, and scoring alignment over academic flourish.\n\nREVIEWER POLISH RULES:\n- Preserve supported facts, required points, named deliverables, and mandatory citation anchors.\n- Improve scanability, credibility, and funder alignment inside the first draft rather than saving polish for a later pass.\n- Tighten vague language, foreground feasibility, and keep the section directly aligned to the call logic.\n- Do not rewrite the section into a journal article, literature review, or generic policy memo.\n\nPERSUASIVE PROSE RULES:\n- Write like an experienced grant writer, not an AI assistant.\n- Vary sentence length and structure; avoid rhythmic, repetitive cadence.\n- Lead paragraphs with analytical claims or reviewer-relevant judgments, not generic description.\n- Replace mechanical transitions such as "Furthermore" or "Additionally" with logical movement grounded in the argument.\n- Prefer one concrete comparison, benchmark, statistic, or implementation detail over several generic sentences.\n- Cut filler, throat-clearing, and empty emphasis such as "it is important to note" or "X is a growing challenge".\n- Match confidence to evidence strength; do not overclaim beyond what the cited support can carry.\n- Every paragraph should increase reviewer confidence in significance, feasibility, or delivery credibility.\n\nBUDGET DISCIPLINE:\n- Target length: stay within 200 words.\n- Fit the budget in one pass by compressing repetition, generic setup, and optional detail before cutting core reviewer-facing evidence.\n- Keep the strongest quantitative facts, feasibility proof, and required compliance content.\n- Do not pad to sound polished and do not exceed the stated limits just to preserve every sentence.\n- Never invent new claims, numbers, or citation anchors while tightening to budget.\n\nCITATION ANCHOR RULES:\n- Preserve any mapped [CITE:key] anchors exactly where they support surviving claims.\n- Every [CITE:key] anchor must support the exact sentence claim it follows; never use citations as general decoration.\n- Mapped-evidence mode: use only mapped or required [CITE:key] anchors, and only on claims directly supported by that evidence.\n- Do not fabricate new citation anchors or attach an anchor to a claim the source does not support.\n- If a sentence is removed, remove only the anchors that belonged exclusively to that deleted claim.\n\nGRANT RULES:\nFormat constraints:\n- Target approximately 200 words.\n\nNarrative constraints:\n- Should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n\nGRANT SECTION COMPLIANCE CONTRACT:\n\nFunding-call summary:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Near-term outcomes: Immediate outcome: Improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 Baiga members. ; Immediate outcome: Employment-ready certification for 50 tribal youth.\n- Longer-term effect: Long-term goal is a scalable model for other Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs) to build digital capacity. | Rule note: Reviewers value scalability as a justification for the initial STI Hub investment.\n- Target Beneficiaries: Beneficiary Profile: 100% ST (Baiga PVTG), with a 60% female and 40% male distribution. ; Scale: 15,000 direct/indirect community beneficiaries in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. | Rule note: Focus on PVTG and high female participation is a top-tier reviewer value signal for DST.\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area: Community involvement via 50 youth Knowledge Brokers who act as the primary interface for data collection and app dissemination.\n- Focus area: science and technology\n- Focus area: socio-economic development\n- Focus area: capacity enhancement\n- Focus area: sustainable development\n\nRequired points:\n- should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people