hood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- Treat these as the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Stage families: problem_definition\n- State the target population related problem you seek to address and brief of their Socio-Economic status | Identified weakest link: oral-only transmission of IK leading to loss of bioprospecting and primary healthcare for poor population. ; Evidence: DTWO census 2022 and NRLM 2023 cited. | Thrust linkage: Directly addresses gap in predominant livelihood (forest-based) and health (loss of primary healthcare resource). | Rule note: Supports problem identification with evidence-based data.\n- Keywords: weakest link, oral - only transmission, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, Directly addresses gap in predominant livelihood (forest-based) and health (loss of primary healthcare resource).\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: problem_definition, fit_and_scope\n- Proposed Objectives | Objective 1: Digitize oral IK of 200 Baiga healers. ; Objective 2: Develop bilingual adaptive learning modules. | Thrust linkage: Five objectives directly aligned with title and S&T intervention. | Rule note: Exactly five objectives, aligned with project title.\n- Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project | Source: Open-source LLM (e.g., Llama). ; Customization: Fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio. | Thrust linkage: science and technology | Rule note: Using open-source models reduces cost and increases sustainability.\n- Brief Industrial Scenario of the target area | Mandla industrial profile per SFURTI/ASPIRE data: primary livelihoods from forest-based activities, no major manufacturing. | Thrust linkage: Mandla's industrial profile is consistent with a predominantly forest-based economy; no industrial base. | Rule note: User confirmed referencing SFURTI/ASPIRE data.\n- Any other Government National Initiatives related to proposed activities to solve this problem | Coordination with National Health Mission (NHM) tribal programs. ; Alignment with Ministry of Tribal Affairs (MoTA) Digital Empowerment Scheme. | Thrust linkage: capacity enhancement | Rule note: Reviewers reward non-duplication and inter-ministerial synergy.\n- Keywords: digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Five objectives directly aligned with title and S&T intervention., Llama, open - source LLM\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Shirisha2019] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Documents socioeconomic and nutritional disparities among Baiga children using qualitative interviews. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove burden | method: Qualitative design with 20 in-depth interviews with mothers. | use: Attach this citation to the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nImpact of oral-only transmission on the sustainability of traditional knowledge systems, Linkages between loss of Indigenous Knowledge and adverse socio-economic outcomes\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	{"output": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, faces an escalating healthcare and livelihood crisis rooted in the oral-only transmission of their Indigenous Knowledge (IK) system. With 70% of the community living below the poverty line, the ecosystem of ~200 traditional healers constitutes the de facto primary healthcare resource for 150,000 people across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—a population already burdened by documented socioeconomic and nutritional disparities [CITE:Shirisha2019]. Yet this knowledge base is collapsing at an estimated 10% per year as healers die without apprentices, severing the intergenerational chain and erasing centuries of validated ethnobotanical practice. The District Tribal Welfare Office census (2022) and NRLM livelihood system analysis (2023) confirm a structural gap: no systematic documentation has occurred since 1995. Existing repositories like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library remain static, text-heavy archives fundamentally inaccessible to an oral-first culture with low literacy. Fragmented NGO catalogues sit unused, and youth migration accelerates as forest-based livelihoods degrade without sustainable harvesting guidance. No scalable, culturally sensitive tool currently bridges the literacy-technology gap for real-time knowledge retrieval. This proposal is justified now because the convergence of affordable solar infrastructure, open-source voice AI, and expanding mobile connectivity in Mandla makes oral-first digitization technically feasible for the first time—enabling active transmission before the remaining healers are lost.", "sectionKey": "target_population_problem_socioeconomic_status"}	751	2026-04-29 11:24:21.98	2026-04-29 11:38:25.593	\N	PAPER	{"methodology": "", "project_summary": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its oral biocultural health knowledge at an estimated 10% per year—the last systematic documentation was in 1995—while 70% of the community lives below the poverty line and youth outmigration severs traditional apprenticeship chains. This STI Hub project deploys an open-source LLM (Llama) fine-tuned on a validated corpus of transcribed oral Baiga medicinal plant and NTFP knowledge, delivered through a voice-enabled, offline-first mobile app accessible via QR codes at five solar-powered community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. The app bridges the literacy-technology gap in an oral-first culture by enabling query-based retrieval and bilingual adaptive learning modules, advancing far beyond static, text-heavy archives like TKDL.\\n\\nFifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women, ages 18–30) will be trained and certified as the primary interface for ethnobotanist-supervised household surveys, app dissemination, and hub operations, creating a human-in-the-loop system for data sovereignty and Prior Informed Consent. The intervention directly links to the forest-based livelihoods of the 150,000-strong Baiga population: the app provides sustainable harvesting and processing guidance for NTFP micro-enterprises, improving productivity while preserving indigenous ecological practices. Coordination with the National Health Mission’s tribal programs and alignment with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme ensure non-duplication and inter-ministerial synergy.\\n\\nBy the end of three years, the project will deliver a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy, five operational STI Hubs, 50 certified youth brokers, and improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 community users. The infrastructure-heavy budget (75% capital assets) ensures post-funding viability, and the model’s offline-first, voice-driven, youth-led architecture is expressly designed for scalability across all 75 PVTGs, making this a high-urgency, high-payoff investment under DST’s STI Hub mandate.", "project_duration": "", "source_of_technology": "", "target_beneficiaries": "The project will arrest the 10% annual loss of biocultural health knowledge among the Baiga PVTG by deploying an AI-driven, voice-first IKS retrieval platform that gives 15,000 community members (100% ST, 60% female) on-demand primary health guidance while simultaneously certifying 50 tribal youth as Knowledge Brokers – creating the first scalable, culturally embedded digital apprenticeship model for an oral-only culture.\\n\\n### Demographic profile of the project area.\\n\\nThe intervention is anchored in 35 predominantly Baiga villages across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh. The total resident population in these villages is approximately 50,000. Scheduled Tribes (Baiga PVTG) constitute 94%, Scheduled Castes 2%, Other Backward Classes 3%, and General category (including EWS) 1%. Women account for roughly 50% of the population.\\n\\n### Beneficiary coverage (category‑wise and gender‑wise).\\n\\nThe project is purpose‑built for the Baiga PVTG community and will count only ST individuals as direct and indirect beneficiaries.\\n\\n| Category | Target to cover (number) | % of total project beneficiaries |\\n|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|\\n| ST | 15,000 | 100% |\\n| SC | 0 | 0% |\\n| OBC | 0 | 0% |\\n| General | 0 | 0% |\\n| EWS | 0 | 0% |\\n\\nGender‑wise distribution of the 15,000 ST beneficiaries: Female 9,000 (60%), Male 6,000 (40%), Transgender 0. This ratio matches the project’s PRA‑based mobilization strategy, which explicitly reserves 50% of Knowledge Broker positions for women and prioritises women healers in the IKS documentation.\\n\\n### What changes for beneficiaries, and how it is measured.\\n\\nImmediate outputs (Years 1‑3). A validated audio IKS database of ~200 healers; a fine‑tuned open‑source LLM powering a bilingual, offline‑first voice app; five solar‑powered STI Hubs with mobile connectivity; and 50 Baiga youth (50% female) trained as Knowledge Brokers through a train‑the‑trainer programme.\\n\\nBeneficiary outcomes. 15,000 community members gain access to accurate, culturally contextual primary health advice through the app, reducing reliance on distant formal facilities. The 50 certified youth become the backbone of a digital apprenticeship chain, earning stipends and making the oral knowledge system accessible to a generation that was disconnecting from it.\\n\\nAdoption and deployment pathway. Fifty youth Knowledge Brokers recruited from the target villages conduct household surveys, carry out PRI‑based informed consent, and lead hands‑on app dissemination. Monthly Health Tech Melas at each hub and quarterly regional workshops with the MP Tribal Welfare Department drive sustained uptake. A Hub Advisory Circle of healers, ethnobotanists, and community representatives ensures data sovereignty and correct cultural framing.\\n\\nEvidence of impact. Project performance is tracked through quarterly surveys and offline‑first usage logs. The primary benchmark is 90% AI response accuracy on health queries, validated by a human‑in‑the‑loop panel of healers. Secondary metrics include the number of active monthly app users (target 15,000 by end of Year 3), the 50 youth certifications awarded, and a documented reduction in the pre‑project 10% annual healer‑knowledge loss – measured by repeat audio ethnography and healer attestation.\\n\\nLonger‑term value. Beyond the grant period, the five hubs become permanent community‑owned knowledge centres, sustaining the app, refreshing content, and offering digital services. Because the model is deliberately modular – voice AI fine‑tuning for an oral culture, solar‑powered offline infrastructure, youth‑broker delivery – it can be replicated for other PVTGs under the same DST STI Hub framework, turning the Mandla demonstration into a national blueprint for active IKS transmission.", "proposed_subject_area": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh—70% below the poverty line, with an entirely oral medicinal knowledge system—is losing its biocultural heritage at an estimated 10% annually. The last systematic documentation was in 1995, and fewer than 200 healers remain, making this one of India’s most urgent indigenous knowledge preservation challenges. This proposal establishes an AI-driven STI Hub that digitizes oral indigenous knowledge (IK) into a validated, voice-first mobile application and trains 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers to operate it, directly improving primary health query resolution for 15,000 community members and creating a scalable digital capacity model for PVTGs.\\n\\nThe intervention replaces static, text-heavy archives—inaccessible in a low-literacy, oral-first culture—with an open-source large language model (Llama) fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga audio, verified by ethnobotanists and healers under prior informed consent. The resulting bilingual, voice-enabled app delivers query-based adaptive learning and sustainable harvesting guidance for non-timber forest products (NTFPs), accessible via QR codes placed in solar-powered community hubs. Blended in-person training through a train-the-trainer model, complemented by monthly Health Tech Melas and quarterly household surveys, ensures adoption even in areas with intermittent connectivity through offline-first logs and human-in-the-loop validation.\\n\\nOver three years, the hub will produce a validated IKS database, five operational community knowledge hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female), and employment‑ready certification aligned with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme. The approach directly links to the National Health Mission’s tribal programs, leverages existing all-weather road access and mobile penetration, and strengthens NTFP‑based micro-enterprises with actionable processing knowledge, reinforcing the Vocal for Local and livelihoods thrust of the STI Hubs call. With an infrastructure‑heavy budget that prioritizes capital assets (75%) and local manpower (30%), the hub is designed for post‑funding viability through community brokers and hub advisory circles.\\n\\nThe payoff is not merely an archive but an active transmission system: 15,000 Baiga community members gain improved primary healthcare access, 50 youth gain durable digital skills, and the model generates a validated, sovereignty‑respecting blueprint that can be scaled across India’s other PVTGs. For the Department of Science and Technology’s STI Hubs scheme, this offers a high‑social‑urgency, technology‑forward investment that bridges the literacy‑technology gap, arrests irreversible knowledge loss, and builds lasting digital capacity in a population with no alternative scalable tool.", "brief_industrial_scenario": "", "possibility_of_replication": "", "other_government_initiatives": "", "proposed_st_delivery_methods": "", "any_other_related_information": "", "technology_gaps_and_solutions": "", "background_of_target_community": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—approximately 150,000 people—faces an acute, accelerating crisis of biocultural knowledge loss, compounded by severe socioeconomic deprivation and a near-total absence of culturally appropriate health and livelihood delivery systems. An estimated 200 traditional healers—the sole custodians of a rich, orally transmitted pharmacopoeia—are aging, and the rate of knowledge attrition is estimated at 10 % per year. The last systematic documentation of this knowledge was conducted in 1995; since then, only fragmented NGO catalogues have been produced, none of which are accessible or usable for the community’s youth. What is being lost is not a static archive but an active system of primary healthcare upon which the majority of Baiga families still depend, given that forest-based livelihoods and remote geography leave formal health services perpetually out of reach.\\n\\nSocioeconomically, the community is among India’s most marginalised. Seventy per cent of the Baiga population lives below the poverty line (NRLM 2023), with an economy built almost entirely on minor forest produce collection, subsistence agriculture, and negligible wage labour. Malnutrition and health inequities are pervasive, as documented by qualitative research on Baiga children’s nutritional disparities [CITE:Shirisha2019]. Literacy rates remain low, and digital access is constrained by intermittent mobile connectivity and erratic electricity—yet solar-powered mobile phones are increasingly common, creating a narrow but real window for technology delivery. The working-age population, particularly those aged 18–30, confronts a broken apprenticeship chain: elders’ knowledge is not being transmitted, and youth out-migration rises as local opportunities shrink. This double failure—lost heritage and lost livelihoods—is the core unmet need.\\n\\nExisting responses across India have been structurally mismatched to an oral-first culture. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library and similar UNESCO-linked repositories are text-heavy, static archives designed for patent protection, not for community use. They assume literacy, ignore vernacular audio, and offer no mechanism for interactive query, adaptive learning, or youth engagement. Earlier documentation projects in the region produced paper-based or inert digital catalogues that sit unused because they cannot be queried in Baiga, cannot respond to voice, and cannot evolve as new knowledge is shared. What is missing is a scalable, culturally sensitive tool that bridges the literacy–technology gap and transforms passive archiving into active, intergenerational transmission—precisely the gap this proposal fills.\\n\\nThe present moment justifies an S&T-led intervention on three grounds. First, the convergence of open-source large language models (LLMs), offline-first mobile architectures, and falling solar costs makes it feasible to build a voice-enabled, bilingual AI system that can operate directly in Baiga and Hindi without continuous connectivity. Second, national policy frameworks—NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indigenous Knowledge Systems, MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme, and DST’s own STI Hub mandate for PVTG scalability—create an enabling environment that did not exist even five years ago. Third, the Baiga community’s own readiness is demonstrable: over 200 healers continue to practise and hold the knowledge [CITE:JHARIYA2024], and there is a cadre of underemployed youth who, with training, can serve as Knowledge Brokers, earning stipends while rebuilding the apprenticeship chain. Inaction at this juncture means that within a generation, an irreplaceable biocultural resource—uniquely adapted to the forest ecosystem of central India—will be permanently erased, leaving 150,000 people poorer in health, identity, and economic possibility.", "existing_solutions_for_problem": "", "proposed_geographical_coverage_area": "", "technical_backup_support_and_linkages": "", "publicity_promotion_dissemination_plan": "", "relevance_to_existing_enterprises_clusters": "", "self_sustainability_revenue_generation_plan": "", "target_population_problem_socioeconomic_status": "The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, faces an escalating healthcare and livelihood crisis rooted in the oral-only transmission of their Indigenous Knowledge (IK) system. With 70% of the community living below the poverty line, the ecosystem of ~200 traditional healers constitutes the de facto primary healthcare resource for 150,000 people across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—a population already burdened by documented socioeconomic and nutritional disparities [CITE:Shirisha2019]. Yet this knowledge base is collapsing at an estimated 10% per year as healers die without apprentices, severing the intergenerational chain and erasing centuries of validated ethnobotanical practice. The District Tribal Welfare Office census (2022) and NRLM livelihood system analysis (2023) confirm a structural gap: no systematic documentation has occurred since 1995. Existing repositories like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library remain static, text-heavy archives fundamentally inaccessible to an oral-first culture with low literacy. Fragmented NGO catalogues sit unused, and youth migration accelerates as forest-based livelihoods degrade without sustainable harvesting guidance. No scalable, culturally sensitive tool currently bridges the literacy-technology gap for real-time knowledge retrieval. This proposal is justified now because the convergence of affordable solar infrastructure, open-source voice AI, and expanding mobile connectivity in Mandla makes oral-first digitization technically feasible for the first time—enabling active transmission before the remaining healers are lost.", "involvement_and_participation_of_target_population": "", "part_3_problem_identification_and_proposed_st_interventions": "In Mandla district’s Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, an estimated 150,000 Baiga people—100% PVTG, 60% women, 70% below the poverty line—depend on orally transmitted medicinal plant knowledge held by roughly 200 traditional healers. That knowledge is vanishing at approximately 10% per year, the last systematic documentation dating to 1995. The result is a double crisis: a rapidly depleting biocultural heritage and a primary healthcare vacuum that falls hardest on women and children. Youth, deterred by a literacy-technology gap and the absence of structured apprenticeship pathways, are migrating out of forest-dependent livelihoods, further breaking the intergenerational chain of knowledge transmission. Existing catalogues remain fragmented across NGO archives, inaccessible in text-heavy formats deeply misaligned with an oral-first culture, and offer no scalable, culturally sensitive retrieval mechanism.\\n\\nCurrent national and international responses are insufficient. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library and UNESCO repositories excel at static archiving but lack oral-vernacular integration, active query-based retrieval, and bidirectional youth engagement. Community health programs under the National Health Mission have not bridged the last-mile information gap because they are not equipped with tools that understand the Baiga dialect or reflect local ethnomedical reasoning. The absence of an interactive, voice-enabled, adaptive learning platform means that healers’ tacit expertise—diagnostic cues, harvesting protocols, dosage norms—remains locked in individual memory, inaccessible during real-time health queries and invisible to the next generation. This is the precise unmet need: a living knowledge system that preserves oral IK in its own modality while simultaneously enabling literate and low-literate users to learn, query, and validate traditional medicine safely.\\n\\nThe proposed S&T intervention directly addresses this gap by deploying an AI-driven STI Hub built on an open-source large language model (e.g., Llama) fine-tuned with transcribed Baiga IK text and vernacular audio recordings. The model is trained using parameter-efficient methods that have recently proven effective for low-resource language translation [CITE:Khoboko2025][CITE:Liang2025], and the fine-tuning incorporates a domain-specific healthcare dataset to bridge medical information accessibility gaps [CITE:Bui2025]. Unlike static archives, the resulting bilingual (Baiga-Hindi-English) mobile application offers offline-first, voice-driven query-based retrieval and adaptive learning modules. This advances far beyond the existing ethnobotanical inventories, which, while documenting the flora used by the Baiga [CITE:JHARIYA2024], remain inert, text-heavy, and untethered from daily health decisions. The intervention will be validated through a human-in-the-loop pipeline with ethnobotanists and healers, ensuring hallucination risks are managed and data sovereignty is preserved under the Hub Advisory Circles.\\n\\nThe present moment is uniquely opportune. Open-source LLMs now allow domain-specific, low-resource fine-tuning without prohibitive cost, and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme, the National Health Mission’s tribal outreach, and NEP 2020’s call for IKS integration create inter-ministerial alignment. Solar infrastructure and improving mobile connectivity in Mandla’s forested hamlets make offline-first digital delivery viable. Inaction would mean the irreversible erosion of a knowledge system that has sustained community health for generations; this project transforms that vulnerability into a scalable, youth-led, STI infrastructure for PVTG digital capacity."}
