cmojkndzo003gg8i65ul6yftr	cmojkl8yw003cg8i6i16kb91u	problem_definition	assistant	You haven't proposed a specific problem yet, so let’s look at what the DST STI Hubs call demands. This funder isn't looking for basic scientific research; they require S&T interventions that directly solve socio-economic and livelihood problems for a specific target community. If you pitch a generic issue like "rural poverty" or "agricultural inefficiency," reviewers will reject it. You must identify a quantified problem, a clear technology gap, and the target demographic's exact socio-economic status.\n\nTo stand out from hundreds of standard proposals, your problem should highlight a specific failure in current practices or an untapped Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) that, with your S&T intervention, will measurably improve local livelihoods. Which specific socio-economic or technological gap does your institution want to solve? Look at the options below to see how a competitive problem statement bundles the core issue, scale, and target beneficiaries to satisfy the evaluation panel. \n\n**Which of these directions best represents the S&T-driven livelihood problem you want to solve?**\n\n**A)** "I approve this bundle: Core Problem: High post-harvest losses and low yield in a specific local crop; Scale/Urgency: Outdated practices cause significant annual income loss for local farmers; Gap: Lack of localized, low-cost S&T processing tools; Beneficiaries: Marginal farmers in our target district currently earning below average income."\n*(Strongest for agricultural/rural livelihood hubs, easily quantified for reviewers)*\n\n**B)** "I approve this bundle: Core Problem: Decline of a regional traditional trade/craft due to inefficient processing methods; Scale/Urgency: Artisan livelihoods are threatened by modernized competitors; Gap: Need for S&T-backed mechanization that scales production while preserving Indigenous Knowledge (IKS); Beneficiaries: Specific artisan or tribal communities i