by_percentile_year": {"max": 99, "min": 90}, "corresponding_author_ids": ["https://openalex.org/A5011554122"], "countries_distinct_count": 1, "institutions_distinct_count": 6, "corresponding_institution_ids": [], "sustainable_development_goals": [], "citation_normalized_percentile": {"value": 0.99264471, "is_in_top_1_percent": true, "is_in_top_10_percent": true}}, "abstract": "Abstract Sub-Saharan Africa began its post-independence economic development with a strong commitment to industry. The terms of trade shocks and economic crises of the 1980s brought with them two decades of macroeconomic stabilization, trade liberalization, and privatization. Growth was slow and uncertain and there was little private investment in industry. When Africa re-emerged around the turn of the twenty-first century, African industry was no longer competing with the high-wage industrial north. It was competing with Asia. Slow growth and fiscal austerity also meant that there was a growing gap in the basics—infrastructure, human capital, and institutions—between Africa and the rest of the developing world. The initial conditions for industrialization were less favourable in relative terms than they had been in the 1960s. From the point of view of industrial development the timing of the region’s economic decline and recovery was unlucky to say the least.", "citationCount": 33, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Economics", "Austerity", "Liberalization", "Industrialisation", "Investment (military)"], "publicationDate": "2016-07-28", "publicationType": "book-chapter"}, {"id": "oa_W3185444974", "doi": "10.1561/9781680838596", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1561/9781680838596", "year": 2021, "title": "A Roadmap for US Robotics – From Internet to Robotics 2020 Edition", "pdfUrl": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt3jf9849m/qt3jf9849m.pdf?t=s1oxpr", "source": "openalex", "authors": ["Henrik I. Christensen", "Nancy M. Amato", "Holly A. Yanco", "Maja Matarić", "Howie Choset", "Ann W. Drobnis", "Ken Goldberg", "Jes