": "Building reputations", "venue": "Manchester University Press", "source": "crossref", "authors": ["Lucey, Conor"], "edition": "1", "rawData": {"DOI": "10.7228/manchester/9781526119940.001.0001", "URL": "https://doi.org/10.7228/manchester/9781526119940.001.0001", "ISBN": ["9781526119940", "9781526138699"], "type": "edited-book", "score": 25.91099, "title": ["Building reputations"], "author": [{"given": "Conor", "family": "Lucey", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": []}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2018, 8, 1]]}, "member": "4230", "prefix": "10.7228", "source": "Crossref", "created": {"date-time": "2019-01-25T00:53:23Z", "timestamp": 1548377603000, "date-parts": [[2019, 1, 25]]}, "indexed": {"date-time": "2024-05-18T10:36:21Z", "timestamp": 1716028581840, "date-parts": [[2024, 5, 18]]}, "abstract": "<p>This book advances an innovative look at a well known, if arguably often misunderstood, historic building typology: the eighteenth-century brick terraced (or row) house. Created for the upper tier of the social spectrum, these houses were largely designed and built by what is customarily regarded as the lower tier of the architectural hierarchy; that is, by artisan communities of bricklayers, carpenters, plasterers and related tradesmen. From London and Dublin to Boston and Philadelphia, these houses collectively formed the streets and squares that became the links and pivots of ‘enlightened’ city plans, and remain central to their respective historic and cultural identities. But while the scenographic quality of Bath and the stuccoed interiors of Dublin have long enjoyed critical approbation, the ‘typical’ house is understood less in terms of design and more in terms of production: consequently, historians have emphasized the commercial motivations of this artisan class at the expense of how they satisfied the demands of an elite, and taste-conscious, real estate market. Drawing on extensive primary source material, from property deeds and architectural drawings to trade cards and newspaper advertising, this book rehabilitates the status of the house builder by examining his negotiation of both the manual and intellectual dimensions of the building process. For the first time, <italic>Building Reputations</italic> considers the artisan as both a figure of building production and an agent of architectural taste.</p>", "language": "en", "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://academic.oup.com/manchester-scholarship-online/book/18356"}}, "subtitle": ["Architecture and the artisan, 1750-1830"], "deposited": {"date-time": "2022-07-28T16:08:07Z", "timestamp": 1659024487000, "date-parts": [[2022, 7, 28]]}, "isbn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "9781526119940"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "9781526138699"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2018, 8, 1]]}, "publisher": "Manchester University Press", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "edition-number": "1", "original-title": ["Building reputations"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2018, 8, 1]]}, "reference-count": 0, "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2019, 1, 24]]}, "references-count": 0, "is-referenced-by-count": 1}, "abstract": "<p>This book advances an innovative look at a well known, if arguably often misunderstood, historic building typology: the eighteenth-century brick terraced (or row) house. Created for the upper tier of the social spectrum, these houses were largely designed and built by what is customarily regarded as the lower tier of the architectural hierarchy; that is, by artisan communities of bricklayers, carpenters, plasterers and related tradesmen. From London and Dublin to Boston and Philadelphia, these houses collectively formed the streets and squares that became the links and pivots of ‘enlightened’ city plans, and remain central to their respective historic and cultural identities. But while the scenographic quality of Bath and the stuccoed interiors of Dublin have long enjoyed critical approbation, the ‘typical’ house is understood less in terms of design and more in terms of production: consequently, historians have emphasized the commercial motivations of this artisan class at the expense of how they satisfied the demands of an elite, and taste-conscious, real estate market. Drawing on extensive primary source material, from property deeds and architectural drawings to trade cards and newspaper advertising, this book rehabilitates the status of the house builder by examining his negotiation of both the manual and intellectual dimensions of the building process. For the first time, <italic>Building Reputations</italic> considers the artisan as both a figure of building production and an agent of architectural taste.</p>", "publisher": "Manchester University Press", "citationCount": 1, "publicationDate": "2018-08-01"}, {"id": "cr_10.4324/9780203818299-10", "doi": "10.4324/9780203818299-10", "url": "https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203818299-10", "isbn": "9780203818299", "year": 2011, "pages": "54-78", "title": "Urbanisation, rural–urban linkages and the urban labour market", "venue": "Small Town China", "source": "crossref", "authors": [], "edition": "0", "rawData": {"DOI": "10.4324/9780203818299-10", "URL": "https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203818299-10", "ISBN": ["9780203818299"], "page": "54-78", "type": "book-chapter", "score": 27.430609, "title": ["Urbanisation, rural–urban linkages and the urban labour market"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2011, 4, 19]]}, "member": "301", "prefix": "10.4324", "source": "Crossref", "created": {"date-time": "2020-12-01T22:20:24Z", "timestamp": 1606861224000, "date-parts": [[2020, 12, 1]]}, "indexed": {"date-time": "2024-05-04T12:10:14Z", "timestamp": 1714824614089, "date-parts": [[2024, 5, 4]]}, "language": "en", "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/9781136735158/chapters/10.4324/9780203818299-10"}}, "deposited": {"date-time": "2020-12-01T22:20:27Z", "timestamp": 1606861227000, "date-parts": [[2020, 12, 1]]}, "isbn-type":