], "notes": [98], "which": [9, 58, 115], "wild,": [87], "(AFM),": [54], "coined": [26], "contra": [51], "social": [75], "article": [1, 78], "between": [102], "certain": [64], "control": [93], "history": [56], "patient": [89, 106], "primary": [37], "produce": [15], "reverse": [145], "special": [30], "version": [31], "wild”": [24], "Finally,": [118], "compares": [80], "examines": [2, 120], "interest": [90], "intimate": [100], "research": [44, 82, 84, 113], "suitable": [61], "connected": [46], "exploring": [63], "genetics,": [124], "identity.": [153], "knowledge": [73], "Française": [50], "Myopathies": [53], "collective": [110, 152], "identities": [107], "integrated": [129], "knowledge.": [18], "laboratory": [81], "mechanisms": [65, 142], "production": [140], "scientific": [72], "scientists": [13], "Association": [49], "collective,": [135], "disseminate": [17], "emphasizing": [88], "identities.": [76], "maintaining": [92], "phenomenon.": [35], "“research": [21], "constitution": [149], "construction": [104, 132], "cooperation.": [95], "illustration": [38], "participate.": [117], "particularly": [60], "co-production": [70], "interactions,": [7], "interrelations": [101], "non-scientists": [10], "exclusion—the": [144], "techno-science-society": [6]}, "cited_by_percentile_year": {"max": 100, "min": 99}, "corresponding_author_ids": ["https://openalex.org/A5110349370"], "countries_distinct_count": 1, "institutions_distinct_count": 2, "corresponding_institution_ids": ["https://openalex.org/I70768539"], "sustainable_development_goals": [{"id": "https://metadata.un.org/sdg/10", "score": 0.8199999928474426, "display_name": "Reduced inequalities"}], "citation_normalized_percentile": {"value": 0.9623389, "is_in_top_1_percent": false, "is_in_top_10_percent": true}}, "abstract": "This article examines new forms of techno-science-society interactions, in which non-scientists work with scientists to produce and disseminate knowledge. The term “research in the wild” is coined to name a special version of this new phenomenon. The primary illustration for this new form of research is connected with the Association Française contra les Myopathies (AFM), the history of which is particularly suitable for exploring certain mechanisms at work in the co-production of scientific knowledge and social identities. The article first compares laboratory research with research in the wild, emphasizing patient interest in maintaining control over cooperation. It then notes the intimate interrelations between the construction of patient identities and the collective form of research in which they participate. Finally, it examines the role of genetics, both as it is integrated into the construction of the collective, and also into the production of mechanisms of exclusion—the reverse side of the constitution of a collective identity.", "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "citationCount": 348, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Constitution", "Identity (music)", "Collective identity", "Sociology", "Knowledge production"], "publicationDate": "2003-04-01", "publicationType": "journal-article", "journalAbbreviation": "Technology in Society"}, {"id": "cr_10.14434/10.14434/mar.v10i2.20268", "doi": "10.14434/10.14434/mar.v10i2.20268", "url": "https://doi.org/10.14434/10.14434/mar.v10i2.20268", "issn": "1938-5145", "year": 2016, "issue": "2", "pages": "66-90", "title": "Digital Knowledge Sharing: Forging Partnerships between Scholars, Archives, and Indigenous Communities", "venue": "Museum Anthropology Review", "pdfUrl": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/mar/article/download/20269/28963", "source": "crossref", "volume": "10", "authors": ["Powell, Timothy B."], "rawData": {"DOI": "10.14434/10.14434/mar.v10i2.20268", "URL": "https://doi.org/10.14434/10.14434/mar.v10i2.20268", "ISSN": ["1938-5145"], "link": [{"URL": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/mar/article/download/20269/28963", "content-type": "application/pdf", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/mar/article/download/20269/28963", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "page": "66-90", "type": "journal-article", "issue": "2", "score": 26.364828, "title": ["Digital Knowledge Sharing: Forging Partnerships between Scholars, Archives, and Indigenous Communities"], "author": [{"given": "Timothy B.", "family": "Powell", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": []}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2016, 12, 31]]}, "member": "5570", "prefix": "10.14434", "source": "Crossref", "volume": "10", "created": {"date-time": "2017-01-11T04:10:42Z", "timestamp": 1484107842000, "date-parts": [[2017, 1, 11]]}, "indexed": {"date-time": "2024-03-31T06:56:00Z", "timestamp": 1711868160348, "date-parts": [[2024, 3, 31]]}, "license": [{"URL": "http://creativecommons.org/ licenses/by/4.0/", "start": {"date-time": "2016-12-31T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1483142400000, "date-parts": [[2016, 12, 31]]}, "delay-in-days": 0, "content-version": "unspecified"}], "abstract": "<jats:p>The article reviews a digital repatriation project carried out by the Center for Native American and Indigenous Research at the American Philosophical Society over the course of eight years (2008-present). The project focused on building digital archives in four indigenous communities: Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians, Penobscot Nation, Tuscarora Nation, and Ojibwe communities in both the United States and Canada. The article features insights from traditional knowledge keepers who helped to create a new system of co-stewarding the APS’ indigenous archival materials and recounts how the APS established protocols for cultural sensitivity. A new model of community-based scholarship is proposed to create a more equal and respectful relationship between indigenous communities, scholars, and archives.</jats:p>", "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/journals/index.php/mar/article/view/20269"}}, "deposited": {"date-time": "2019-04-29T19:00:49Z", "timestamp": 1556564449000, "date-parts": [[2019, 4, 29]]}, "issn-type": [{"type": "electronic", "value": "1938-5145"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2016, 12, 31]]}, "publisher": "IUScholarWorks", "journal-issue": {"issue": "2", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2016, 12, 31]]}}, "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "container-title": ["Museum Anthropology Review"], "reference-count": 0, "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2016, 12, 31]]}, "references-count": 0, "short-container-title": ["MAR"], "is-referenced-by-count": 8}, "abstract": "<jats:p>The article reviews a digital repatriation project carried out by the Center for Native American and Indigenous Research at the American Philosophical Society over the course of eight years (2008-present). The project focused on building digital archives in four indigenous communities: Eastern Band of Cherokee Indians, Penobscot Nation, Tuscarora Nation, and Ojibwe communities in both the United States and Canada. The article features insights from traditional knowledge keepers who helped to create a new system of co-stewarding the APS’ indigenous archival materials and recounts how the APS established protocols for cultural sensitivity. A new model of community-based scholarship is proposed to create a more equal and respectful relationship between indigenous communities, scholars, and archives.</jats:p>", "publisher": "IUScholarWorks", "citationCount": 8, "publicationDate": "2016-12-31", "journalAbbreviation": "MAR"}]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-29 11:10:28.42	2026-04-29 11:10:28.42	cmojydbj100e677ycvkvrqvjg
