del, Cooperative model, Master trainers, Usage fee, Buy - back agreements, MSME cooperative model, Scalable blueprint, State backing, Regional textile clusters\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Pansera2016]: BACKGROUND | Provides empirical evidence of frugal innovations by grassroots entrepreneurs in India, showing how non-formally educated individuals develop appropriate technologies for underserved communities. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: show gap | method: Qualitative multiple-case study using primary and secondary data. | use: Use this citation to name the limitation, barrier, or unmet need.\n- [Shafi2022] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Provides a systematic review of innovation in traditional handicraft companies, linking innovation to sustainable development and competitiveness. | strength: medium | stance: neutral | role: show gap | method: Systematic literature review with keyword-based search and analysis. | use: Use this citation to name the limitation, barrier, or unmet need.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\n(none detected)\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	Our project introduces a differentiated technological intervention by developing and deploying context-appropriate, ergonomic, semi-mechanized yarn winders specifically for the Phulkari craft micro-clusters. This moves beyond existing approaches, which often fail to adequately address the physical strain and output limitations of purely manual yarn preparation [CITE:Pansera2016]. While some initiatives have explored mechanization, fully industrial models are typically unavailable to or destructive of the traditional aesthetics valued by these artisans, creating a significant technology gap [CITE:Shafi2022].\n\nThis differentiation matters because it directly tackles the bottlenecks that restrict productivity and contribute to low wages (under ₹5,000/month), pushing artisans, particularly women, to abandon the craft. By bridging this identified problem of manual processing with a concrete S&T solution, our project aims to achieve a 50% production increase and doubled wages for 2,000 Phulkari women artisans, directly aligning with the call's emphasis on S&T for socio-economic development and livelihood generation. The proposed semi-mechanized winders, alongside eco-friendly dye baths, represent an adaptive R&D approach that enhances output without compromising the heritage and traditional aesthetics of the Phulkari craft.	254	DRAFT	f	6	2026-04-28 06:10:06.04	2026-04-28 12:05:04.286	2026-04-28 12:05:04.279	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-28T12:05:04.260Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "mention importance of the project in context of the current status, and demonstrate how the project will progress beyond the “state-of-art” or the best initiative tried by others in providing new innovative technological solution such as design modification, improvement in existing solutions etc. to the identified problem and user needs"}, {"key": "required_mention importance of th", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: mention importance of the project in context of the current status, and demonstrate how the project will progress beyond the “state-of-art” or the best initiative tried by others in providing new innovative technological solution such as design modification, improvement in existing solutions etc. to the identified problem and user needs", "ruleText": "mention importance of the project in context of the current sta