cmojzbfa900m377ycvy9qj7ns	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	background_of_target_community	Background Of Target Community	The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its most critical health resource—the oral traditional knowledge of its healers—at an estimated 10% per year. With 70% of the community already living below the poverty line (NRLM, 2023) and formal healthcare access severely limited, this erosion translates directly into worsening health outcomes for the 150,000-strong population. Ethnobotanical surveys confirm that Baiga healthcare rests on an orally transmitted pharmacopeia of over 150 medicinal plant species [CITE:JHARIYA2024], while socioeconomic assessments document acute nutritional disparities among Baiga children, compounding the stakes of knowledge loss [CITE:Shirisha2019].\n\nThe Baiga are a forest-dependent community concentrated in Mandla’s Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Livelihoods center almost entirely on collection of non-timber forest produce (NTFP), subsistence cultivation, and minor daily-wage labor; district industrial profiles show no significant manufacturing base (SFURTI/ASPIRE). With adult literacy hovering near 30% and a deeply oral-first culture, the community’s invaluable Indigenous Knowledge (IK) system—honed over centuries—remains trapped in a purely spoken form, reliant on intergenerational apprenticeship chains that are now breaking under the pressure of out-migration by youth seeking survival income. The last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995, leaving behind fragmented NGO catalogues that are text-heavy, linguistically inaccessible, and entirely disconnected from the community’s oral, vernacular reality.\n\nCurrent institutional responses fail this need entirely. The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) serves patent protection objectives; it is text-based and inaccessible to Baiga healers whose knowledge lives in speech, song, and memory. Even acclaimed international models like Maori databases or UNESCO repositories function as static archives—they lack an interactive, bidirectional learning mechanism and do not engage youth in active transmission. No scalable, culturally sensitive tool exists that can capture oral IK in its own linguistic medium, make it queryable by voice, and respect community data sovereignty while bridging the literacy-technology gap.\n\nThis STI Hub intervention is justified precisely now because converging technological capabilities and community-field infrastructure have made a radically different approach viable. Open-source large language models (LLMs) can be fine-tuned on transcribed Baiga oral audio, and offline-first voice AI can power a bilingual mobile application that healers and youth query by speech—even in the semi-connected forest hamlets of Bichhiya and Mawai. By transforming a rapidly vanishing oral tradition into a living, transmitted knowledge system that simultaneously builds digital skills and creates youth employment, the project answers the STI Hubs call for equity-driven, technology-enabled socioeconomic development for India’s most marginalized communities.	400	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - BACKGROUND OF TARGET COMMUNITY\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for e