n\nPERSUASIVE PROSE RULES:\n- Write like an experienced grant writer, not an AI assistant.\n- Vary sentence length and structure; avoid rhythmic, repetitive cadence.\n- Lead paragraphs with analytical claims or reviewer-relevant judgments, not generic description.\n- Replace mechanical transitions such as "Furthermore" or "Additionally" with logical movement grounded in the argument.\n- Prefer one concrete comparison, benchmark, statistic, or implementation detail over several generic sentences.\n- Cut filler, throat-clearing, and empty emphasis such as "it is important to note" or "X is a growing challenge".\n- Match confidence to evidence strength; do not overclaim beyond what the cited support can carry.\n- Every paragraph should increase reviewer confidence in significance, feasibility, or delivery credibility.\n\nCITATION ANCHOR RULES:\n- Preserve any mapped [CITE:key] anchors exactly where they support surviving claims.\n- Every [CITE:key] anchor must support the exact sentence claim it follows; never use citations as general decoration.\n- Mapped-evidence mode: use only mapped or required [CITE:key] anchors, and only on claims directly supported by that evidence.\n- Do not fabricate new citation anchors or attach an anchor to a claim the source does not support.\n- If a sentence is removed, remove only the anchors that belonged exclusively to that deleted claim.\n\nGRANT SECTION COMPLIANCE CONTRACT:\n\nFunding-call summary:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Near-term outcomes: Immediate outcome: Improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 Baiga members. ; Immediate outcome: Employment-ready certification for 50 tribal youth.\n- Longer-term effect: Long-term goal is a scalable model for other Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs) to build digital capacity. | Rule note: Reviewers value scalability as a justification for the initial STI Hub investment.\n- Target Beneficiaries: Beneficiary Profile: 100% ST (Baiga PVTG), with a 60% female and 40% male distribution. ; Scale: 15,000 direct/indirect community beneficiaries in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. | Rule note: Focus on PVTG and high female participation is a top-tier reviewer value signal for DST.\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area: Community involvement via 50 youth Knowledge Brokers who act as the primary interface for data collection and app dissemination.\n- Focus area: science and technology\n- Focus area: socio-economic development\n- Focus area: capacity enhancement\n- Focus area: sustainable development\n\nRequired points:\n- Directly answer "Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project".\n\nTemplate guidance:\nTemplate-required facts:\n- Directly answer "Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project".\n\nPrep evidence:\n- Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project: Source: Open-source LLM (e.g., Llama). ; Customization: Fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio. | Rule notes: Using open-source models reduces cost and increases sustainability.\n\nHard checks:\n- Template required fact 1: Directly answer "Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project".\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- Treat these as the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Stage families: fit_and_scope\n- Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project | Source: Open-source LLM (e.g., Llama). ; Customization: Fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio. | Thrust linkage: science and technology | Rule note: Using open-source models reduces cost and increases sustainability.\n- Keywords: Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, science and technology\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: methodology\n- Methodology | Uses a 'Train-the-Trainer' model for community knowledge transfer. ; Implementation begins with Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: PRA is a standard DST requirement for community-based STI Hubs to ensure local buy-in.\n- Institution/ places where detailed lab/ field testing or experimentations and validation will be carried out | Field testing and validation will occur at 5 designated community hubs within the target blocks. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Directly addresses the call's requirement for field testing locations.\n- Proposed Geographical coverage Area | Geographical focus: Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, Mandla, MP. ; Infrastructure: Hubs equipped with basic solar power and mobile connectivity. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Reviewers require specific infrastructure assessments to ensure hub sustainability.\n- Technical back-up support and linkages established in the project area | Technical backup and large-scale dissemination support secured through the MP Tribal Welfare Department. | Thrust linkage: Socio-economic development | Rule note: State government involvement is a high-value signal for DST reviewers.\n- Keywords: Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, Capacity enhancement, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Infrastructure\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Khoboko2025] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Provides a comparative analysis of PEFT methods for low-resource language translation, showing that Mistral 7B with LoRA achieves strong performance. | strength: medium | stance: neutral | role: prove feasibility | method: Experimental comparison of several PEFT strategies on a low-resource translation | use: Use this citation to show a comparable implementation worked.\n- [Bui2025]: BACKGROUND | Provides a reproducible methodology for fine-tuning LLMs on low-resource language healthcare datasets to bridge medical information accessibility gaps. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove feasibility | method: Selects a base LLM, compiles a domain-specific dataset, and fine-tunes using sta | use: Use this citation to show a comparable implementation worked.\n- [Liang2025]: BACKGROUND | Introduces Language-Specific Fine-Tuning with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to improve machine translation for low-resource languages using open-source LLMs. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove feasibility | method: Uses LoRA to train a subset of parameters, comparing performance against general | use: Use this citation to show a comparable implementation worked.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\n(none detected)\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The proposed delivery model centres on an AI-powered, voice-first knowledge system purpose-built for an oral-priority community. A single open-source large language model (source: Llama series) is fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral indigenous knowledge—recorded and validated directly with approximately 200 traditional healers—and deployed through a bilingual (Baiga–Hindi) mobile application. This design bypasses the literacy–technology gap that has locked previous static, text-heavy archival efforts out of daily community use.\n\nWhy this approach is credible. Fine-tuning open-source LLMs for low-resource, domain-specific tasks is not theoretical; it has been demonstrated repeatedly. Parameter-efficient methods, notably Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), achieve strong performance on low-resource language translation with manageable compute [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025]. Comparable workflows have successfully built reproducible fine-tuning pipelines for healthcare information in under-resourced languages [CITE:Bui2025]. By building on the Llama family, the project inherits powerful multilingual pre-training while confining customisation to a Baiga-specific adaptation layer—reducing cost, preserving data sovereignty, and enabling offline inference on affordable handsets.\n\n### Execution steps and work components.\n\n- Year 1 – Documentation and corpus building. Participatory Rural Appraisal mobilises healers and community leaders across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Trained ethnobotanists and 50 youth Knowledge Brokers conduct audio documentation of oral medicinal plant knowledge, following Prior Informed Consent protocols. Audio is transcribed, translated, and curated into a validated Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) database with human-in-the-loop expert validation.\n- Year 2 – LLM fine-tuning and app development. The transcribed corpus undergoes cleaning and structuring. Using LoRA, the base LLM is fine-tuned for bilingual query-based retrieval and adaptive, dialogue-style learning modules. A voice-enabled mobile app is built with offline-first logs, QR-code-linked audio entries, and a simple interface designed for low-literacy users. Beta testing runs within the five identified community hubs, capturing usability and accuracy feedback.\n- Year 3 – Training, hub deployment, and scaling. A Train-the-Trainer programme certifies 20 community trainers and 50 Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30). Five operational STI Hubs—equipped with solar power and mobile connectivity—become permanent access points. The app is loaded onto hub devices and users’ own phones, reaching a target base of 15,000 community members for primary healthcare query resolution and IKS access.\n\nValidation and evaluation method. System accuracy is benchmarked against a held-out test set of expert-verified IK entries, with a threshold of 90% correct retrieval and response accuracy. Quarterly household surveys track usage, health query resolution, and user trust. Knowledge Broker logs and app analytics provide real-time fidelity checks. A continuous feedback loop—with Hub Advisory Circles, healer review panels, and user ratings—feeds corrections directly into the fine-tuning cycle, keeping the model grounded in community-validated knowledge and suppressing hallucination risk.\n\nRisk controls and feasibility. The single largest execution risk—AI hallucination—is managed through a Human-in-the-Loop design: all model outputs remain traceable to curated source recordings, and hub-based Advisors review ambiguous responses. Connectivity intermittency is neutralised by offline-first architecture; solar infrastructure at each hub guarantees power for charging and sync. Data sovereignty is safeguarded by storing the primary corpus on local servers under community stewardship, with externally shared derivatives governed by Prior Informed Consent agreements. Low adoption is pre-empted by the Train-the-Trainer cascade and the deliberate choice of a voice interface, which eliminates the text barrier that has rendered earlier IKS archives inaccessible to Baiga youth. The technical core—open-source LLM plus LoRA fine-tuning—is already proven feasible in analogous low-resource settings [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025] [CITE:Bui2025], and the implementation plan grounds that capability in field-tested, hub-based delivery with strong institutional backing from the Madhya Pradesh Tribal Welfare Department.	1491	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-30 05:41:53.933	2026-04-30 05:41:53.933	2026-04-30 05:41:53.915	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": true, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:41:53.787Z", "hardFailures": [], "softWarnings": [], "missingEvidence": [], "usedPrepEvidence": ["fit_and_scope:questions_source_of_technology"], "violatedAvoidRules": [], "unmetRequiredPoints": [], "coveredRequiredPoints": ["Directly answer \\"Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project\\"."]}, "reviewerReadinessReport": {"risks": ["The draft does not name a concrete comparison point or current-practice contrast."], "score": 94, "strengths": ["Directly answer \\"Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project\\".", "Structured Grant Prep evidence is reflected in the draft.", "Draft contains concrete statistics rather than generic burden language.", "Draft includes explicit feasibility evidence or implementation precedent.", "Draft includes a measurable metric, milestone, target, or deliverable."], "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:41:53.797Z", "missingSignals":