cmojzdar700mj77yc7x7r5ubq	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	target_population_problem_socioeconomic_status	Target Population Problem Socioeconomic Status	The accelerating oral-only transmission loss among the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is erasing the community’s primary healthcare system. Knowledge of over 200 medicinal plant species is held by approximately 200 aging traditional healers, with no systematic documentation completed since 1995. The cost of inaction is direct: 70 percent of the 150,000 Baiga community members live below the poverty line, and as elder healers pass away without apprentices, curative knowledge is declining at an estimated ten percent annually, leaving families with no accessible health alternative in remote forest settlements [CITE:Shirisha2019].\n\nThe Baiga economy remains entirely forest-based, reliant on non-timber forest produce (NTFP) with no major manufacturing or industrial base as confirmed by SFURTI and ASPIRE district profiles. In Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, nutrition insecurity and health disparities are severe, compounded by low literacy and the near-total absence of culturally intelligible digital health resources. Existing interventions—fragmented NGO plant catalogues and centralised text-heavy databases such as the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library—are static, text-dependent, and structurally inaccessible to an oral-first culture. No existing tool provides an interactive, voice-based, bilingual retrieval system adapted to Baiga epistemological frameworks.\n\nThis proposal is justified now because the intersection of field readiness, open-source AI capacity, and institutional partnerships makes a scalable S&T response achievable for the first time. Our fieldwork and prior household surveys, triangulated with DTWO census 2022 and NRLM 2023 data, confirm that youth are willing knowledge brokers if given voice-driven digital apprenticeship pathways that bridge the literacy-technology gap. Without immediate digitization and active transmission infrastructure, this irreplaceable biocultural heritage will be lost within a generation.	266	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - STATE THE TARGET POPULATION RELATED PROBLEM YOU SEEK TO ADDRESS AND BRIEF OF THEIR SOCIO-ECONOMIC STATUS\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhanceme