 PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Shirisha2019] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Documents socioeconomic and nutritional disparities among Baiga children using qualitative interviews. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove burden | method: Qualitative design with 20 in-depth interviews with mothers. | use: Attach this citation to the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nImpact of oral-only transmission on the sustainability of traditional knowledge systems, Linkages between loss of Indigenous Knowledge and adverse socio-economic outcomes\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The accelerating oral-only transmission loss among the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is erasing the community’s primary healthcare system. Knowledge of over 200 medicinal plant species is held by approximately 200 aging traditional healers, with no systematic documentation completed since 1995. The cost of inaction is direct: 70 percent of the 150,000 Baiga community members live below the poverty line, and as elder healers pass away without apprentices, curative knowledge is declining at an estimated ten percent annually, leaving families with no accessible health alternative in remote forest settlements [CITE:Shirisha2019].\n\nThe Baiga economy remains entirely forest-based, reliant on non-timber forest produce (NTFP) with no major manufacturing or industrial base as confirmed by SFURTI and ASPIRE district profiles. In Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, nutrition insecurity and health disparities are severe, compounded by low literacy and the near-total absence of culturally intelligible digital health resources. Existing interventions—fragmented NGO plant catalogues and centralised text-heavy databases such as the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library—are static, text-dependent, and structurally inaccessible to an oral-first culture. No existing tool provides an interactive, voice-based, bilingual retrieval system adapted to Baiga epistemological frameworks.\n\nThis proposal is justified now because the intersection of field readiness, open-source AI capacity, and institutional partnerships makes a scalable S&T response achievable for the first time. Our fieldwork and prior household surveys, triangulated with DTWO census 2022 and NRLM 2023 data, confirm that youth are willing knowledge brokers if given voice-driven digital apprenticeship pathways that bridge the literacy-technology gap. Without immediate digitization and active transmission infrastructure, this irreplaceable biocultural heritage will be lost within a generation.	878	DRAFT	f	2	2026-04-29 11:37:30.547	2026-04-30 05:29:07.523	2026-04-30 05:29:07.518	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:29:07.498Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "word_budget", "source": "system", "message": "Section exceeds the word budget of 200.", "ruleText": null}, {"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "should be based on identification of weakest links/gaps/problems in the predominant livelihood, health, quality of life and socio-economic status of people in select area the problem identification should be supported with evidence based data may be based on livelihood system analysis, investigators fieldwork in the target area, district (industrial) reports, data from various others reports, NRLM, SRLM data etc"}, {"key": "required_should be based on ident", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: should be based on identificatio