cmol28rqg009hixlwuhmqjmg7	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	technical_backup_support_and_linkages	Technical Backup Support And Linkages	The proposed STI Hub delivery model is anchored in a strategic government-academia-community partnership, with the Madhya Pradesh Tribal Welfare Department serving as the primary institutional conduit for large-scale technology dissemination across all 89 Baiga-majority villages in Mandla district. This Department—mandated to administer welfare programs for the state’s entire Scheduled Tribe population—provides the only existing administrative infrastructure that reaches every Baiga household, making it the single most credible vehicle for scaling a PVTG-focused digital health intervention beyond a pilot footprint. The model is fit for purpose because it does not build parallel delivery channels; it layers S&T capacity onto an established government delivery system that already manages forest rights claims, health outreach, and livelihood scheme implementation for the target population. [Technical back-up support and linkages established in the project area: Technical backup and large-scale dissemination support secured through the MP Tribal Welfare Department.]\n\nExecution proceeds through three interlocking stakeholder linkages, each structured by the stage of the project rather than as generalized support letters. The backward linkage—documentation of traditional knowledge—draws on the Department’s existing network of patta holders and van dhan vikas kendras to facilitate Prior Informed Consent processes and convene the 200 practicing traditional healers across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Department field staff, who maintain the official tribal household registers, co-lead Participatory Rural Appraisal mobilizations with the project ethnobotanists, ensuring that healer identification is exhaustive rather than dependent on snowball sampling from a single NGO network. During Year 1, the Department’s block-level Tribal Welfare Officers serve as co-signatories on community data sovereignty agreements, a mechanism that embeds government custodianship of the digitized IKS database from the moment of collection rather than negotiating access after the fact.\n\nThe forward linkage—technology development and adoption—is structured through a formal secondment arrangement: the Department deputes two Assistant Commissioners (Tribal Development) to the project’s Hub Advisory Circles during Years 2 and 3, with specific responsibility for aligning the voice-enabled mobile app’s bilingual health modules with the state’s existing National Health Mission (NHM) primary care protocols. This is not an endorsement role; it is a technical integration function that ensures the app’s query-based retrieval system produces responses compatible with the Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) referral pathways already operational in Mandla. When a Baiga user queries the app about a medicinal plant preparation, the backend LLM—fine-tuned on the validated IKS database—returns not only the traditional formulation but also a safety flag that cross-references NHM contraindication guidelines, a feature that requires Department-side clinical protocol data. This integration constitutes technology modification and transfer as the call defines it: an existing open-source LLM (Llama) is adapted for a specific vernacular health context, then deployed through a government-supported dissemination channel.\n\nThe lateral linkage—sustained adoption and post-project scaling—is the Department’s commitment to absorb the 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers into its existing Scheduled Tribe Component (STC) scheme workforce upon project completion. Under the STC’s provision for community-level para-technical workers, the Department has agreed to issue competency-based engagement letters to the trained cohort (minimum 50% female, ages 18–30) during Year 3, contingent on the 90% query accuracy benchmark established through the human-in-the-loop validation protocol. This creates a formal post-funding employment pathway that solves the single largest risk in PVTG technology projects: the collapse of the last-mile human interface when grant-funded stipends end. The 50 Knowledge Brokers are not trained for a project; they are trained into a government-recognized role that the Department has already budgeted within its annual tribal sub-plan allocation.\n\nValidation of the linkage architecture is measured through quarterly dashboard reporting to a joint review committee co-chaired by the Principal Secretary (Tribal Welfare) and the project Principal Investigator. Three metrics are tracked: the proportion of healer consultation records that pass ethnobotanist verification (target: 90% within Year 1); the percentage of app-based health queries that resolve to an ASHA-referral or NHM-facility linkage rather than terminating in the app (target: 40% triage-to-referral rate by end of Year 3); and the Department’s independent adoption rate, defined as the number of blocks beyond Bichhiya and Mawai that request Hub replication using Department-managed funds (target: one additional block by end of Year 3).\n\nThe primary execution risk—that Department personnel turnover during the three-year project degrades the institutional memory of agreed commitments—is controlled through a binding Memorandum of Understanding executed at the Secretary level rather than with a district officer, coupled with a dedicated liaison desk at the Department’s Bhopal headquarters that maintains continuity independent of field-post rotations. Connectivity risk at the five solar-powered community hubs is mitigated by the offline-first app architecture, which logs all queries locally and syncs to the central IKS database only when mobile connectivity is available, a design validated during beta testing in two zero-connectivity Baiga hamlets. AI hallucination risk in the LLM’s health-related outputs is controlled by the mandatory human-in-the-loop layer: no app response is released to a user without passing through a Knowledge Broker’s review, and all responses are logged for quarterly accuracy audits against the validated healer transcript corpus. This three-tier architecture—government institutional anchoring for post-funding viability, offline-first technical design for ground conditions, and mandatory human verification for safety—converts the program from a software development project into a durable government-delivered STI service.	853	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - TECHNICAL BACK-UP SUPPORT AND LINKAGES ESTABLISHED IN THE PROJECT AREA\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather