d linkage—technology development and adoption—is structured through a formal secondment arrangement: the Department deputes two Assistant Commissioners (Tribal Development) to the project’s Hub Advisory Circles during Years 2 and 3, with specific responsibility for aligning the voice-enabled mobile app’s bilingual health modules with the state’s existing National Health Mission (NHM) primary care protocols. This is not an endorsement role; it is a technical integration function that ensures the app’s query-based retrieval system produces responses compatible with the Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) referral pathways already operational in Mandla. When a Baiga user queries the app about a medicinal plant preparation, the backend LLM—fine-tuned on the validated IKS database—returns not only the traditional formulation but also a safety flag that cross-references NHM contraindication guidelines, a feature that requires Department-side clinical protocol data. This integration constitutes technology modification and transfer as the call defines it: an existing open-source LLM (Llama) is adapted for a specific vernacular health context, then deployed through a government-supported dissemination channel.\n\nThe lateral linkage—sustained adoption and post-project scaling—is the Department’s commitment to absorb the 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers into its existing Scheduled Tribe Component (STC) scheme workforce upon project completion. Under the STC’s provision for community-level para-technical workers, the Department has agreed to issue competency-based engagement letters to the trained cohort (minimum 50% female, ages 18–30) during Year 3, contingent on the 90% query accuracy benchmark established through the human-in-the-loop validation protocol. This creates a formal post-funding employment pathway that solves the single largest risk in PVTG technology projects: the collapse of the last-mile human interface when grant-funded stipends end. The 50 Knowledge Brokers are not trained for a project; they are trained into a government-recognized role that the Department has already budgeted within its annual tribal sub-plan allocation.\n\nValidation of the linkage architecture is measured through quarterly dashboard reporting to a joint review committee co-chaired by the Principal Secretary (Tribal Welfare) and the project Principal Investigator. Three metrics are tracked: the proportion of healer consultation records that pass ethnobotanist verification (target: 90% within Year 1); the percentage of app-based health queries that resolve to an ASHA-referral or NHM-facility linkage rather than terminating in the app (target: 40% triage-to-referral rate by end of Year 3); and the Department’s independent adoption rate, defined as the number of blocks beyond Bichhiya and Mawai that request Hub replication using Department-managed funds (target: one additional block by end of Year 3).\n\nThe primary execution risk—that Department personnel turnover during the three-year project degrades the institutional memory of agreed commitments—is controlled through a binding Memorandum of Understanding executed at the Secretary level rather than with a district officer, coupled with a dedicated liaison desk at the Department’s Bhopal headquarters that maintains continuity independent of field-post rotations. Connectivity risk at the five solar-powered community hubs is mitigated by the offline-first app architecture, which logs all queries locally and syncs to the central IKS database only when mobile connectivity is available, a design validated during beta testing in two zero-connectivity Baiga hamlets. AI hallucination risk in the LLM’s health-related outputs is controlled by the mandatory human-in-the-loop layer: no app response is released to a user without passing through a Knowledge Broker’s review, and all responses are logged for quarterly accuracy audits against the validated healer transcript corpus. This three-tier architecture—government institutional anchoring for post-funding viability, offline-first technical design for ground conditions, and mandatory human verification for safety—converts the program from a software development project into a durable government-delivered STI service.	1491	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-30 05:45:44.295	2026-04-30 05:45:44.295	2026-04-30 05:45:44.284	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": true, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:45:44.213Z", "hardFailures": [], "softWarnings": [], "missingEvidence": [], "usedPrepEvidence": ["methodology:questions_technical_backup_support_and_linkages"], "violatedAvoidRules": [], "unmetRequiredPoints": [], "coveredRequiredPoints": ["State Govt. involvement for large scale technology dissemination"]}, "reviewerReadinessReport": {"risks": [], "score": 95, "strengths": ["State Govt. involvement for large scale technology dissemination", "Structured Grant Prep evidence is reflected in the draft.", "Draft contains concrete statistics rather than generic burden language.", "Draft names a comparison point or current-practice contrast.", "Draft includes explicit feasibility evidence or implementation precedent.", "Draft includes a measurable metric, milestone, target, or deliverable."], "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:45:44.215Z", "missingSignals": ["State Govt. involvement for large scale technology dissemination; forward & backward linkages; stakeholders with emphasis on extent and nature of their involvement in documenting traditional knowledge, development of technology / modification/ transfer/ adoption etc."], "recommendedActions": ["Strengthen the reviewer signal around: State Govt. involvement for large scale technology dissemination; forward & backward linkages; stakeholders with emphasis on extent and nature of their involvement in documenting traditional knowledge, development of technology / modification/ transfer/ adoption