nology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, relies on an exclusively oral tradition of medicinal plant knowledge held by roughly 200 aging healers. With no systematic documentation since 1995, a 10% annual loss of practitioners, 70% poverty, and youth outmigration, this biocultural heritage and the primary healthcare it enables are collapsing. Static archives and text-heavy NGO catalogues have failed because they ignore the community’s oral-first culture and literacy barriers, while no scalable, culturally sensitive S&T tool has existed to bridge the gap.\n\nThis proposal deploys an AI-Driven STI Hub model that digitizes oral indigenous knowledge through a voice-enabled, open-source LLM fine-tuned on transcribed Baiga ethnobotanical audio. Bilingual vernacular modules, accessible offline-first via QR codes and mobile app, are delivered across five solar-powered community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. Fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers—50% female, ages 18–30—recruited through PRA-based mobilization, will conduct household surveys, curate content, and train 15,000 community users, targeting 60% women. The intervention directly improves primary health query resolution, certifies youth in digital and knowledge-broker skills, and equips NTFP-based micro-enterprises with sustainable harvesting intelligence. By Year 3, five operational hubs, a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy, and a train-the-trainer network will demonstrate a replicable blueprint for PVTG digital capacity, aligning with DST’s STI Hub mandate for socioeconomic empowerment, livelihood enhancement, and inclusion through science and technology.	1349	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-29 11:46:47.013	2026-04-29 11:46:47.013	2026-04-29 11:46:47.008	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-29T11:46:46.984Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "should include problem, proposed solution/technology, target community/beneficiaries and name of implementing Districts and State for establishment of STI hub/ WTP/SUNIL etc."}, {"key": "required_should include problem p", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: should include problem, proposed solution/technology, target community/beneficiaries and name of implementing Districts and State for establishment of STI hub/ WTP/SUNIL etc.", "ruleText": "should include problem, proposed solution/technology, target community/beneficiaries and name of implementing Districts and State for establishment of STI hub/ WTP/SUNIL etc."}, {"key": "evidence_should include problem p", "source": "template", "message": "Required drafting evidence is missing: should include problem, proposed solution/technology, target community/beneficiaries and name of implementing Districts and State for establishment of STI hub/ WTP/SUNIL etc.", "ruleText": "should include problem, proposed solution/technology, target community/beneficiaries and name of implementing Districts and State for establishment of STI hub/ WTP/SUNIL etc."}], "softWarnings": [{"key": "reviewer_signal_1", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Reviewer signal 1", 