rt, model, diagram including methods such as Survey; Protocols, Mobilization; Technology Identification, Assessment, Transfer, Adaptation & Adoption; Demonstration & Training, Result & Impact analysis, Stakeholders engagement, Marketing, Linkages, Feedback and Follow-up from stakeholders/ben etc.\n- Narrative constraint 1: defined steps/relevant process details e.g. PERT chart, model, diagram including methods such as Survey; Protocols, Mobilization; Technology Identification, Assessment, Transfer, Adaptation & Adoption; Demonstration & Training, Result & Impact analysis, Stakeholders engagement, Marketing, Linkages, Feedback and Follow-up from stakeholders/ben etc.\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- Treat these as the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Stage families: methodology\n- Methodology | Uses a 'Train-the-Trainer' model for community knowledge transfer. ; Implementation begins with Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: PRA is a standard DST requirement for community-based STI Hubs to ensure local buy-in.\n- Keywords: Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, Capacity enhancement\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: methodology\n- Institution/ places where detailed lab/ field testing or experimentations and validation will be carried out | Field testing and validation will occur at 5 designated community hubs within the target blocks. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Directly addresses the call's requirement for field testing locations.\n- Proposed Geographical coverage Area | Geographical focus: Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, Mandla, MP. ; Infrastructure: Hubs equipped with basic solar power and mobile connectivity. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Reviewers require specific infrastructure assessments to ensure hub sustainability.\n- Technical back-up support and linkages established in the project area | Technical backup and large-scale dissemination support secured through the MP Tribal Welfare Department. | Thrust linkage: Socio-economic development | Rule note: State government involvement is a high-value signal for DST reviewers.\n- Keywords: 5 community hubs, Mandla, Infrastructure, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [Powell2016] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Presents a digital repatriation project that built digital archives in four indigenous communities, demonstrating successful partnerships between scholars, archives, and indigenous communities. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: establish precedent | method: Case study of a multi-year digital repatriation project with qualitative insight | use: Use this citation to compare against current practice or precedent.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nEfficacy of Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization in tribal areas, Validation of the 'Train-the-Trainer' model for sustainable knowledge transfer and capacity building\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The project deploys a train-the-trainer delivery model anchored in Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization, adapted to the oral-first culture of the Baiga PVTG. The model is credible because it converts 50 Baiga youth (50 % female, ages 18–30) into Knowledge Brokers who bridge 200 traditional healers and a voice-enabled AI system, ensuring that digitised indigenous knowledge is actively transmitted rather than archived in static, text-heavy repositories—an approach validated by successful digital repatriation partnerships with indigenous communities elsewhere [CITE:Powell2016]. Execution unfolds in three annual work components, each with defined technology identification, adaptation, adoption, and stakeholder engagement steps.\n\nYear 1: Documentation and Community Mobilisation. The team initiates PRA-based household surveys and focus-group discussions across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks to map healers, medicinal-plant knowledge, and livelihood-linked NTFP practices. Healers provide prior informed consent under protocols co-designed with Hub Advisory Circles that include village elders, women’s collectives, and the MP Tribal Welfare Department. Ethnobotanists and Knowledge Brokers audio-record oral IK in vernacular, transcribe and validate it through cross-healer triangulation, and ingest it into a structured, verified IKS database. Infrastructure assessments confirm that the five identified community hub sites have all-weather road access, solar-power viability, and basic mobile connectivity.\n\nYear 2: LLM Fine-Tuning and Voice-Enabled App Development. Transcribed IK text is used to fine-tune an open-source Llama-family large language model, producing bilingual (Hindi/Baigani) adaptive learning modules. A voice-enabled mobile app with query-based retrieval is built and alpha-tested with Knowledge Brokers and healers. Iterative beta testing refines the human-in-the-loop pipeline: when the model’s confidence falls below 90 %, the query is routed to a healer via a Knowledge Broker, and the corrected answer re-enters the training set. Offline-first logging ensures functionality during connectivity gaps, while quarterly surveys track usability and adoption metrics.\n\nYear 3: Training, Hub Operations, and Impact Analysis. A train-the-trainer cascade is executed: 20 master trainers (drawn from the Knowledge Broker cohort and partner institution staff) conduct in-person certification workshops at the five hubs for the remaining youth brokers, covering app use, IK ethics, data sovereignty, and basic digital skills. The 50 certified youth then deploy the app to 15,000 community members, resolving primary health queries and building a self-sustaining knowledge-transmission chain. Five operational STI Hubs serve as solar-powered, digitally equipped community anchor points. Result and impact analysis uses pre-post household surveys measuring health-information accuracy, healer-youth apprenticeship revival, and app query-resolution rates. Continuous feedback loops—quarterly Hub Advisory Circle meetings, app-usage analytics, and Knowledge Broker debriefs—feed into an adaptive management plan.\n\nValidation and Quality Control. Knowledge validity is ensured through dual ethnobotanist review of a 20 % random sample of transcribed IK entries; AI accuracy is benchmarked against healer-provided answers, with a 90 % correctness target monitored monthly. Community adoption is validated through quarterly log-file audits of unique users, repeat queries, and offline-to-online sync rates. Youth certification is tied to a competency-based assessment conducted jointly by the partner government academic institution and the MP Tribal Welfare Department.\n\nRisk Controls and Feasibility. The dominant risks—connectivity loss, AI hallucination, and low adoption—are mitigated by design: the offline-first app queues queries locally and syncs when in mobile range; the human-in-the-loop architecture caps AI error by requiring healer validation below the confidence threshold; and voice-first bilingual interaction bridges the literacy-technology gap that has caused similar text-heavy systems to fail. Data sovereignty is enforced through community-held consent registers and Hub Advisory Circle oversight, preventing extractive archiving. Infrastructure risk is addressed upfront through 75 % capital-asset allocation for solar equipment, all-weather hub retrofits, and mobile-booster hardware. Post-funding viability is built into the model: 50 certified youth Knowledge Brokers—trained as trainers, not just data collectors—can sustain the hub-based apprenticeship cycle and app maintenance through linkages with the National Health Mission, MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme, and NTFP micro-enterprises, all without relying on permanent external funding.	1790	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-30 05:46:48.854	2026-04-30 05:46:48.854	2026-04-30 05:46:48.845	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:46:48.794Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint i