cmol330z000dwixlwmzbhna04	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	lab_field_testing_locations	Lab Field Testing Locations	Detailed field testing, experimentation, and validation will be carried out at five community STI Hubs located in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh. These blocks are the demographic heartland of the Baiga PVTG, and the hub-based delivery model is purpose-built for oral-first, low-literacy, forest-dependent communities—each site is equipped with solar power, mobile connectivity, and all-weather road access, making sustained fieldwork feasible year-round while ensuring data sovereignty remains anchored within the community.\n\nThe validation approach is iterative and deeply embedded: fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, aged 18–30), recruited through Participatory Rural Appraisal-based mobilization, will work from these hubs to audio-document the knowledge of approximately 200 traditional healers. Ethnobotanist-supervised transcription and an LLM fine-tuning loop will run continuously, with the voice-enabled mobile app beta-tested under real-world conditions. Quarterly household surveys spanning 15,000 beneficiaries and offline-first app logs will feed a human-in-the-loop review cycle, allowing the system to reach the 90% AI accuracy target for primary health queries while adapting the bilingual interface to vernacular speech patterns and dialect variation.\n\nExecution risk is systematically controlled: offline-first architecture removes connectivity as a showstopper during field use; solar power at every hub eliminates grid dependency; Prior Informed Consent and community data stewardship are enforced through Hub Advisory Circles and oversight by the MP Tribal Welfare Department; and the train-the-trainer approach builds distributed expertise so the intervention does not collapse with the departure of a single individual. The lead academic institution brings over a decade of S&T deployment experience in Mandla, the infrastructure meets DST field-site standards, and the direct involvement of the State Tribal Welfare Department provides the institutional backing needed to sustain validation across three years and later adapt the model to other PVTG blocks.	286	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - INSTITUTION/ PLACES WHERE DETAILED LAB/ FIELD TESTING OR EXPERIMENTATIONS AND VALIDATION WILL BE CARRIED OUT\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Man