cmojz0mm000kg77ycabax5rif	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	part_3_problem_identification_and_proposed_st_interventions	Part 3 Problem Identification And Proposed St Interventions	The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population of approximately 150,000 spread across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—is losing an estimated 10% of its ethnomedicinal knowledge every year. The last systematic documentation of Baiga healing traditions was conducted in 1995; since then, nearly 200 remaining traditional healers have continued to practice exclusively through oral transmission, with no scalable, culturally-sensitive tool to capture or share this knowledge. Seventy percent of Baiga households live below the poverty line, and with youth migration accelerating and the traditional apprenticeship chain broken, the community’s biocultural heritage faces a silent collapse that also deepens the primary healthcare access gap in one of India’s most marginalized regions.\n\nExisting responses are structurally inadequate for an oral-first, low-literacy community. Static digital repositories—whether national initiatives like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library or fragmented NGO catalogues—remain text-heavy, English-indexed archives that cannot be accessed by Baiga youth or healers. Even recent ethnobotanical inventories that confirm the richness of Baiga medicinal plant use [CITE:JHARIYA2024] sit as printed or PDF records, inert and unavailable for real-time health queries. None of these approaches bridge the literacy–technology gap, offer vernacular voice interaction, or provide adaptive learning pathways that keep indigenous knowledge alive as a living transmission, not a museum artifact. Without a tool that speaks the community’s language and adapts to its oral culture, the knowledge will continue to vanish, and the promised benefits of digital empowerment will bypass the very people they are meant to reach.\n\nA new angle is now possible—and urgently justified—by the convergence of social need and scientific readiness. Advances in parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) for low-resource languages, using methods like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on open-source large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated that it is feasible to build high-accuracy bilingual systems from small, domain-specific oral corpora [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025]. Furthermore, fine-tuning LLMs on localized healthcare datasets has already proven effective in bridging medical information gaps for underserved populations [CITE:Bui2025]. Capitalizing on these breakthroughs, our proposed S&T intervention will fine-tune an open-source LLM (e.g., Llama) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral IK—complemented by vernacular audio recordings—to generate bilingual (local language and English) adaptive learning modules delivered through a voice-enabled mobile app. Unlike static PDF archives, the system provides query-based retrieval and adaptive learning: a community member can ask a spoken health question and receive a context-aware, healer-validated answer, while youth Knowledge Brokers use the same platform to deepen their own learning and earn certifications. This transitions indigenous knowledge from passive preservation to active, community-owned transmission.\n\nThe intervention is designed to be offline-first, solar-powered, and anchored in five physical STI Hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai, making it resilient to connectivity gaps. Fifty trained Baiga youth (50% female, aged 18–30) will act as Knowledge Brokers, collecting oral recordings in Year 1 while simultaneously building capacity through a digital apprenticeship model. By Year 3, the validated IKS database will power a scalable, query-capable tool that reaches 15,000 community users, directly improves primary health query resolution, and creates employment-ready certifications for tribal youth. This approach advances beyond any existing digital archive by embedding indigenous knowledge in an interactive, voice-first system that strengthens both cultural continuity and economic opportunity, aligning with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme and the National Health Mission’s tribal outreach, while fulfilling the STI Hubs mandate for science-and-technology-driven socio-economic transformation of PVTG communities.	554	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigat