NTRACT:\n\nFunding-call summary:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Near-term outcomes: Immediate outcome: Improved primary health query resolution for 15,000 Baiga members. ; Immediate outcome: Employment-ready certification for 50 tribal youth.\n- Longer-term effect: Long-term goal is a scalable model for other Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs) to build digital capacity. | Rule note: Reviewers value scalability as a justification for the initial STI Hub investment.\n- Target Beneficiaries: Beneficiary Profile: 100% ST (Baiga PVTG), with a 60% female and 40% male distribution. ; Scale: 15,000 direct/indirect community beneficiaries in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. | Rule note: Focus on PVTG and high female participation is a top-tier reviewer value signal for DST.\n- Details/ Mechanism for ensuring the involvement and participation of target population in the project area: Community involvement via 50 youth Knowledge Brokers who act as the primary interface for data collection and app dissemination.\n- Focus area: science and technology\n- Focus area: socio-economic development\n- Focus area: capacity enhancement\n- Focus area: sustainable development\n\nRequired points:\n- Directly answer "PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS".\n\nTemplate guidance:\nTemplate-required facts:\n- Directly answer "PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS".\n\nPrep evidence:\n- PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS: S&T intervention: LLM fine-tuned on transcribed IK text + vernacular audio recordings to generate bilingual (local language + English) educational modules and mobile app ; Advances beyond static PDF archives by enabling query-based retrieval and adaptive learning | Rule notes: Directly aligns with call’s S&T component requirement\n\nHard checks:\n- Template required fact 1: Directly answer "PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS".\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- Treat these as the factual backbone for this section when they apply.\n- Stage families: problem_definition\n- PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS | S&T intervention: LLM fine-tuned on transcribed IK text + vernacular audio recordings to generate bilingual (local language + English) educational modules and mobile app ; Advances beyond static PDF archives by enabling query-based retrieval and adaptive learning | Thrust linkage: S&T intervention, Indigenous Knowledge Systems | Rule note: Directly aligns with call’s S&T component requirement\n- Keywords: LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, S&T intervention, Indigenous Knowledge Systems\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: problem_definition, fit_and_scope\n- Proposed Objectives | Objective 1: Digitize oral IK of 200 Baiga healers. ; Objective 2: Develop bilingual adaptive learning modules. | Thrust linkage: Five objectives directly aligned with title and S&T intervention. | Rule note: Exactly five objectives, aligned with project title.\n- Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project | Source: Open-source LLM (e.g., Llama). ; Customization: Fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio. | Thrust linkage: science and technology | Rule note: Using open-source models reduces cost and increases sustainability.\n- Brief Industrial Scenario of the target area | Mandla industrial profile per SFURTI/ASPIRE data: primary livelihoods from forest-based activities, no major manufacturing. | Thrust linkage: Mandla's industrial profile is consistent with a predominantly forest-based economy; no industrial base. | Rule note: User confirmed referencing SFURTI/ASPIRE data.\n- Any other Government National Initiatives related to proposed activities to solve this problem | Coordination with National Health Mission (NHM) tribal programs. ; Alignment with Ministry of Tribal Affairs (MoTA) Digital Empowerment Scheme. | Thrust linkage: capacity enhancement | Rule note: Reviewers reward non-duplication and inter-ministerial synergy.\n- Keywords: digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Five objectives directly aligned with title and S&T intervention., Llama, open - source LLM\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nEVIDENCE DIGEST (one line per citation - use as grounding, not verbatim):\n\n- [JHARIYA2024] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Provides a documented inventory of ethnomedicinal plants used by Baiga and Gond tribes in Mandla district, establishing the existence and reliance on oral IK. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: show gap | method: Phytosociological survey and ethnobotanical interviews with local healers. | use: Use this citation to name the limitation, barrier, or unmet need.\n- [Khoboko2025] [MUST-CITE]: BACKGROUND | Provides a comparative analysis of PEFT methods for low-resource language translation, showing that Mistral 7B with LoRA achieves strong performance. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove burden | method: Experimental comparison of several PEFT strategies on a low-resource translation | use: Attach this citation to the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n- [Liang2025]: BACKGROUND | Introduces Language-Specific Fine-Tuning with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to improve machine translation for low-resource languages using open-source LLMs. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove burden | method: Uses LoRA to train a subset of parameters, comparing performance against general | use: Attach this citation to the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n- [Bui2025]: BACKGROUND | Provides a reproducible methodology for fine-tuning LLMs on low-resource language healthcare datasets to bridge medical information accessibility gaps. | strength: strong | stance: neutral | role: prove burden | method: Selects a base LLM, compiles a domain-specific dataset, and fine-tunes using sta | use: Attach this citation to the exact burden statistic or urgency fact.\n\nEVIDENCE GAPS:\nEffectiveness of query-based retrieval and adaptive learning for community knowledge dissemination\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population of approximately 150,000 spread across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks—is losing an estimated 10% of its ethnomedicinal knowledge every year. The last systematic documentation of Baiga healing traditions was conducted in 1995; since then, nearly 200 remaining traditional healers have continued to practice exclusively through oral transmission, with no scalable, culturally-sensitive tool to capture or share this knowledge. Seventy percent of Baiga households live below the poverty line, and with youth migration accelerating and the traditional apprenticeship chain broken, the community’s biocultural heritage faces a silent collapse that also deepens the primary healthcare access gap in one of India’s most marginalized regions.\n\nExisting responses are structurally inadequate for an oral-first, low-literacy community. Static digital repositories—whether national initiatives like the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library or fragmented NGO catalogues—remain text-heavy, English-indexed archives that cannot be accessed by Baiga youth or healers. Even recent ethnobotanical inventories that confirm the richness of Baiga medicinal plant use [CITE:JHARIYA2024] sit as printed or PDF records, inert and unavailable for real-time health queries. None of these approaches bridge the literacy–technology gap, offer vernacular voice interaction, or provide adaptive learning pathways that keep indigenous knowledge alive as a living transmission, not a museum artifact. Without a tool that speaks the community’s language and adapts to its oral culture, the knowledge will continue to vanish, and the promised benefits of digital empowerment will bypass the very people they are meant to reach.\n\nA new angle is now possible—and urgently justified—by the convergence of social need and scientific readiness. Advances in parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) for low-resource languages, using methods like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on open-source large language models (LLMs), have demonstrated that it is feasible to build high-accuracy bilingual systems from small, domain-specific oral corpora [CITE:Khoboko2025] [CITE:Liang2025]. Furthermore, fine-tuning LLMs on localized healthcare datasets has already proven effective in bridging medical information gaps for underserved populations [CITE:Bui2025]. Capitalizing on these breakthroughs, our proposed S&T intervention will fine-tune an open-source LLM (e.g., Llama) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral IK—complemented by vernacular audio recordings—to generate bilingual (local language and English) adaptive learning modules delivered through a voice-enabled mobile app. Unlike static PDF archives, the system provides query-based retrieval and adaptive learning: a community member can ask a spoken health question and receive a context-aware, healer-validated answer, while youth Knowledge Brokers use the same platform to deepen their own learning and earn certifications. This transitions indigenous knowledge from passive preservation to active, community-owned transmission.\n\nThe intervention is designed to be offline-first, solar-powered, and anchored in five physical STI Hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai, making it resilient to connectivity gaps. Fifty trained Baiga youth (50% female, aged 18–30) will act as Knowledge Brokers, collecting oral recordings in Year 1 while simultaneously building capacity through a digital apprenticeship model. By Year 3, the validated IKS database will power a scalable, query-capable tool that reaches 15,000 community users, directly improves primary health query resolution, and creates employment-ready certifications for tribal youth. This approach advances beyond any existing digital archive by embedding indigenous knowledge in an interactive, voice-first system that strengthens both cultural continuity and economic opportunity, aligning with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme and the National Health Mission’s tribal outreach, while fulfilling the STI Hubs mandate for science-and-technology-driven socio-economic transformation of PVTG communities.	2891	DRAFT	f	2	2026-04-29 11:27:39.384	2026-04-29 11:41:15.099	2026-04-29 11:41:15.092	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-29T11:41:15.070Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\"."}, {"key": "required_directly answer part 3 p", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\".", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\"."}, {"key": "evidence_directly answer part 3 p", "source": "template", "message": "Required drafting evidence is missing: Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\".", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\"."}], "softWarnings": [], "missingEvidence": ["Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\"."], "usedPrepEvidence": ["problem_definition:sections_part_3_problem_identification_and_proposed_st_interventions"], "violatedAvoidRules": [], "unmetRequiredPoints": ["Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\"."], "coveredRequiredPoints": []}, "reviewerReadinessReport": {"risks": ["Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "Required point is still missing: Directly answer \\"PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS\\".", "Required drafting evidence is missing: Directly answer 