cmol3dx6500ktixlwrqtm3voi	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	project_duration	Project Duration	The project will be executed over 36 months—the maximum allowed under the STI Hubs call—through three phased work packages that build sequentially from documentation to AI development to community-scale operation. Phase 1 (Months 1–12) focuses on participatory mobilization and knowledge documentation. Using a Train‑the‑Trainer model and Participatory Rural Appraisal in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, the team will identify and engage the first cohort of 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50 % female, ages 18–30). Under institutional supervision and with Prior Informed Consent protocols, these brokers will work alongside ethnobotanists to audio‑document the oral medicinal plant knowledge of approximately 200 traditional healers—the last systematic documentation dates to 1995. The output is a validated, transcribed Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) corpus in vernacular language, structured for machine‑learning ingestion. Simultaneously, solar power units and mobile connectivity are procured and installed at five identified community‑hub sites, ensuring the physical backbone is ready before the digital layers go live.\n\nPhase 2 (Months 7–24) develops the AI‑driven voice‑enabled app. It begins with LLM fine‑tuning on the validated IKS database, establishing a bilingual (vernacular/Hindi) query‑based retrieval system with human‑in‑the‑loop guardrails to minimize hallucination. An offline‑first mobile app is built, beta‑tested with a subset of healers and youth, and iteratively refined until the AI accuracy benchmark of ≥90 % is reached. This phase’s milestone is a deployment‑ready app that bridges literacy gaps and can operate in low‑connectivity settings; its completion is a hard dependency for Phase 3 community rollout.\n\nPhase 3 (Months 19–36) operationalizes the five STI Hubs and scales adoption. The 50 Knowledge Brokers receive formal certification and stipend‑supported apprenticeships; they then train community members through in‑person sessions and Health Tech Melas, using QR‑code‑guided access. Milestones: 5 fully functional, solar‑powered hubs with live advisory circles; 50 youth certified; 15,000 Baiga community members directly accessing improved primary‑health query resolution. A quarterly survey mechanism tracks user engagement, accuracy, and health‑seeking behavior changes, feeding a final impact assessment.\n\nDependencies and sequencing are tight. Phase 2’s model training cannot begin without a minimum viable IKS corpus (Phase 1 milestone at Month 12). Hub launch in Phase 3 requires both the tested app (Month 24) and the capital‑intensive infrastructure (solar/mobile install completed by Month 18). No international travel is budgeted; all 15 lakhs of local travel are confined to Mandla district, reinforcing feasibility. Governance rests with the Project Director from the host government academic institution, supported by a dedicated AI team, a field coordinator, and the Hub Advisory Circles comprising healers, PRI members, and MP Tribal Welfare Department nominees. Monitoring checkpoints include quarterly progress reports to DST, six‑monthly reviews tied to milestone achievement, and real‑time app‑usage analytics, ensuring course‑correction well within the 3‑year window.	441	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - PROJECT DURATION\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Doc