ing call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The project will be executed over 36 months—the maximum allowed under the STI Hubs call—through three phased work packages that build sequentially from documentation to AI development to community-scale operation. Phase 1 (Months 1–12) focuses on participatory mobilization and knowledge documentation. Using a Train‑the‑Trainer model and Participatory Rural Appraisal in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, the team will identify and engage the first cohort of 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50 % female, ages 18–30). Under institutional supervision and with Prior Informed Consent protocols, these brokers will work alongside ethnobotanists to audio‑document the oral medicinal plant knowledge of approximately 200 traditional healers—the last systematic documentation dates to 1995. The output is a validated, transcribed Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) corpus in vernacular language, structured for machine‑learning ingestion. Simultaneously, solar power units and mobile connectivity are procured and installed at five identified community‑hub sites, ensuring the physical backbone is ready before the digital layers go live.\n\nPhase 2 (Months 7–24) develops the AI‑driven voice‑enabled app. It begins with LLM fine‑tuning on the validated IKS database, establishing a bilingual (vernacular/Hindi) query‑based retrieval system with human‑in‑the‑loop guardrails to minimize hallucination. An offline‑first mobile app is built, beta‑tested with a subset of healers and youth, and iteratively refined until the AI accuracy benchmark of ≥90 % is reached. This phase’s milestone is a deployment‑ready app that bridges literacy gaps and can operate in low‑connectivity settings; its completion is a hard dependency for Phase 3 community rollout.\n\nPhase 3 (Months 19–36) operationalizes the five STI Hubs and scales adoption. The 50 Knowledge Brokers receive formal certification and stipend‑supported apprenticeships; they then train community members through in‑person sessions and Health Tech Melas, using QR‑code‑guided access. Milestones: 5 fully functional, solar‑powered hubs with live advisory circles; 50 youth certified; 15,000 Baiga community members directly accessing improved primary‑health query resolution. A quarterly survey mechanism tracks user engagement, accuracy, and health‑seeking behavior changes, feeding a final impact assessment.\n\nDependencies and sequencing are tight. Phase 2’s model training cannot begin without a minimum viable IKS corpus (Phase 1 milestone at Month 12). Hub launch in Phase 3 requires both the tested app (Month 24) and the capital‑intensive infrastructure (solar/mobile install completed by Month 18). No international travel is budgeted; all 15 lakhs of local travel are confined to Mandla district, reinforcing feasibility. Governance rests with the Project Director from the host government academic institution, supported by a dedicated AI team, a field coordinator, and the Hub Advisory Circles comprising healers, PRI members, and MP Tribal Welfare Department nominees. Monitoring checkpoints include quarterly progress reports to DST, six‑monthly reviews tied to milestone achievement, and real‑time app‑usage analytics, ensuring course‑correction well within the 3‑year window.	1985	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-30 06:17:44.237	2026-04-30 06:17:44.237	2026-04-30 06:17:44.233	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T06:17:44.205Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\"."}, {"key": "required_directly answer project ", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\".", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\"."}, {"key": "evidence_directly answer project ", "source": "template", "message": "Required drafting evidence is missing: Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\".", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\"."}, {"key": "evidence_no preserved grant prep ", "source": "prep", "message": "Required drafting evidence is missing: No preserved Grant Prep evidence is reflected in the section draft.", "ruleText": "No preserved Grant Prep evidence is reflected in the section draft."}], "softWarnings": [], "missingEvidence": ["Directly answer \\"Project Duration\\".", "No preserved Grant Prep evidence is reflected in the section draft."], "usedPrepEvidence": [], "violatedAvoidRules": [], "unme