cmojz3mnw00kx77ycu8ybpwjx	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	proposed_subject_area	Proposed Subject Area	This proposal addresses an urgent and accelerating crisis: the oral-only Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) of the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) are vanishing at an estimated 10% per year, with the last systematic documentation conducted in 1995. For over 200 remaining traditional healers in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population that is 70% below the poverty line—the transmission chain has broken as youth migrate out and find static, text-heavy archives culturally inaccessible. The project deploys a voice-first, AI-driven STI Hub model that fine-tunes an open-source large language model (LLM) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed vernacular audio, creating an interactive, bilingual mobile app for query-based retrieval and adaptive learning. Over three years, 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women) will validate and disseminate this knowledge through 5 solar-powered community hubs, directly reaching 15,000 users with improved primary healthcare access and enabling NTFP-based micro-enterprises through sustainable harvesting intelligence.\n\nThe intervention translates oral healing traditions into a living, evolving digital resource without reducing them to static archives—directly advancing the STI Hubs call’s mandate for science and technology-led socioeconomic empowerment of SC/ST communities. By bridging the literacy-technology gap with voice AI and offline-first functionality, it puts agency back into the community: 50 youth earn employment-ready certifications, 5 hubs become sustained knowledge assets, and a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy powers a replicable model for other PVTGs. The project aligns with the National Health Mission’s tribal programs, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme, and NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indian knowledge systems, while leveraging Vocal for Local to create livelihood value from traditional ecological knowledge. This proposal merits immediate funding because it converts an irreversible biocultural loss into a scalable, institution-backed digital capacity that outlasts the grant—precisely the high-social-urgency, high-feasibility investment that the STI Hubs program was designed to catalyze.	299	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - PROPOSED SUBJECT AREA\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI 