SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: fit_and_scope, outcomes\n- Source of identified Technology/ package of practice under project | Source: Open-source LLM (e.g., Llama). ; Customization: Fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio. | Thrust linkage: science and technology | Rule note: Using open-source models reduces cost and increases sustainability.\n- Target Beneficiaries | Beneficiary Profile: 100% ST (Baiga PVTG), with a 60% female and 40% male distribution. ; Scale: 15,000 direct/indirect community beneficiaries in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: Focus on PVTG and high female participation is a top-tier reviewer value signal for DST.\n- Any other related information, if not covered under fields of proforma | User confirmed no other conflicting government information exists for this proposal.\n- PUBLICITY, PROMOTION & DISSEMINATION PLAN | Dissemination via regional workshops with MP Tribal Welfare Dept. ; Community-level promotion through monthly 'Health Tech Melas' at hubs. | Thrust linkage: Publicity and Promotion | Rule note: Leveraging state departments increases credibility for scaling.\n- Keywords: Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, science and technology, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Capacity enhancement\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	This proposal addresses an urgent and accelerating crisis: the oral-only Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS) of the Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) are vanishing at an estimated 10% per year, with the last systematic documentation conducted in 1995. For over 200 remaining traditional healers in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh—a population that is 70% below the poverty line—the transmission chain has broken as youth migrate out and find static, text-heavy archives culturally inaccessible. The project deploys a voice-first, AI-driven STI Hub model that fine-tunes an open-source large language model (LLM) on a proprietary corpus of transcribed vernacular audio, creating an interactive, bilingual mobile app for query-based retrieval and adaptive learning. Over three years, 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (60% women) will validate and disseminate this knowledge through 5 solar-powered community hubs, directly reaching 15,000 users with improved primary healthcare access and enabling NTFP-based micro-enterprises through sustainable harvesting intelligence.\n\nThe intervention translates oral healing traditions into a living, evolving digital resource without reducing them to static archives—directly advancing the STI Hubs call’s mandate for science and technology-led socioeconomic empowerment of SC/ST communities. By bridging the literacy-technology gap with voice AI and offline-first functionality, it puts agency back into the community: 50 youth earn employment-ready certifications, 5 hubs become sustained knowledge assets, and a validated IKS database with 90% AI accuracy powers a replicable model for other PVTGs. The project aligns with the National Health Mission’s tribal programs, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme, and NEP 2020’s emphasis on Indian knowledge systems, while leveraging Vocal for Local to create livelihood value from traditional ecological knowledge. This proposal merits immediate funding because it converts an irreversible biocultural loss into a scalable, institution-backed digital capacity that outlasts the grant—precisely the high-social-urgency, high-feasibility investment that the STI Hubs program was designed to catalyze.	930	DRAFT	f	2	2026-04-29 11:29:59.42	2026-04-29 11:44:24.269	2026-04-29 11:44:24.263	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": false, "generatedAt": "2026-04-29T11:44:24.244Z", "hardFailures": [{"key": "template_required_fact_1", "source": "template", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Template required fact 1", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"Proposed Subject Area\\"."}, {"key": "required_directly answer proposed", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required point is still missing: Directly answer \\"Proposed Subject Area\\".", "ruleText": "Directly answer \\"Proposed Subject Area\\"."}, {"key": "evidence_directly answer proposed", "source": "template", "message": "Required drafting 