cmol1ve7i005aixlwbu695zhj	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	existing_solutions_for_problem	Existing Solutions For Problem	The Baiga PVTG in Mandla, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its oral biocultural heritage at an estimated 10% per year—the last systematic documentation of approximately 200 traditional healers occurred in 1995, and 70% of the community lives below the poverty line, leaving this knowledge system critically endangered. Existing national and international responses remain fundamentally mismatched to the problem. India’s Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) successfully documented codified Ayurvedic, Unani, and Siddha texts but was designed for patent protection of written classical systems, not for the oral, vernacular, and practice-based transmission that sustains PVTG medicinal plant knowledge. International models such as the Māori digital archives in New Zealand and UNESCO’s local knowledge repositories have demonstrated successful partnerships between scholars, archives, and indigenous communities [CITE:Powell2016]; however, these initiatives remain text-heavy, static archives that assume literate intermediaries and centralized access, failing to bridge the literacy-technology gap in oral-first cultures. Critically, none of these systems incorporate interactive voice-based retrieval, youth knowledge broker models, or adaptive bilingual learning—the three elements that can convert passive documentation into active, intergenerational transmission. Fragmented NGO cataloguing efforts in central India over the past two decades have produced inaccessible digital silos that Baiga youth neither contribute to nor use, perpetuating the apprenticeship chain break that drives out-migration. This proposal’s voice-AI-driven STI Hub model—fine-tuning an open-source LLM on transcribed oral IK and deploying it through offline-first, voice-enabled mobile app infrastructure operated by 50 trained youth Knowledge Brokers—directly addresses the gap that static archives, national databases, and international repositories have left unresolved. Implementation in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, in coordination with the Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment Scheme and the National Health Mission’s tribal health programs, avoids duplication and extends existing policy frameworks into a domain where no scalable, culturally sensitive S&T intervention currently operates.	293	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - EXISTING REGIONAL/ NATIONAL/ INTERNATIONAL SOLUTIONS FOR THE PROPOSED PROBLEM\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya