ject staff and purchase of Capital as milestone/ target. Refer evaluation and monitoring indicators in Guidelines of scheme/ programme of SEED division, refer Annexure- A. Work plan/Milestones/ targets (minimum 5-6 per year). Expected deliverables (anticipated quantitative output, outcome & socio-economic impact on target beneficiaries)\n- Narrative constraint 1: please don't give baseline survey, recruitment of project staff and purchase of Capital as milestone/ target. Refer evaluation and monitoring indicators in Guidelines of scheme/ programme of SEED division, refer Annexure- A. Work plan/Milestones/ targets (minimum 5-6 per year). Expected deliverables (anticipated quantitative output, outcome & socio-economic impact on target beneficiaries)\n\nAUTHORITATIVE SECTION PREP POINTS:\n- No finalized section-mapped Grant Prep points are available for this section.\n\nRELATED SECTION AWARENESS:\n- Awareness only: use this to maintain coherence, not to invent new required claims.\n- Stage families: budget_strategy, methodology, workplan\n- Budget overview | Total project budget set at 2 Crores INR. ; Allocation of 1.5 Crores (75%) for Capital Assets to support solar power and mobile units. | Rule note: 75% capital is significantly higher than standard DST SEED caps; must be justified as 'Infrastructure/CFC' for PVTG hubs.\n- Project Staff | 60 Lakhs allocated for project staff, including technical AI developers and community coordinators. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: Must align with latest DST SEED OM for JRF/SRF/Project Associate scales.\n- Travel | 15 Lakhs for local field visits across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks and DST review meetings. ; No international travel is included, adhering to DST SEED rules. | Thrust linkage: Infrastructure | Rule note: Reviewers check for 'International Travel' as a disqualifier; explicitly stating its absence is a safety signal.\n- Methodology | Uses a 'Train-the-Trainer' model for community knowledge transfer. ; Implementation begins with Participatory Rural Appraisal (PRA) for community mobilization. | Thrust linkage: Capacity enhancement | Rule note: PRA is a standard DST requirement for community-based STI Hubs to ensure local buy-in.\n- Keywords: 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, Capacity enhancement, 15 Lakhs, Local travel\n\nPROJECT / TECHNICAL CONTEXT:\nTitle: Grant: STI Hubs\nResearch Question: How should the proposal satisfy PART 3: PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND PROPOSED S&T INTERVENTIONS while addressing the funding call priorities?\nMethodology: OTHER\nContribution: APPLIED\nKeywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improved primary healthcare access, Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, Government Academic Institution, capacity enhancement, IKS digitization, NEP 2020, 3 - year timeline, 200 healers, 50 youth, NHM, MoTA, Digital Empowerment Scheme, voice - enabled app, QR codes, in - person training, Llama, open - source LLM, fine - tuning, NTFP, micro - enterprises, sustainable harvesting, no conflicting information, 2026 - 05 - 16, solar infrastructure, youth broker stipends, 60 : 30 ratio, 2 Crore total, STI Hub alignment, 2 Crores, 75% Capital Assets, Infrastructure - heavy, 60 Lakhs, 30% manpower, 15 Lakhs, Local travel, No international travel, AI hallucination, low adoption, connectivity, Data sovereignty, Prior Informed Consent, Hub Advisory Circles, IKS Integration, Vocal for Local, AI - Driven, PVTG, Social Urgency, Indigenous Knowledge, medicinal plants, oral - only transmission, loss of biocultural heritage, ~200 traditional healers, last systematic documentation 1995, 70% below poverty line, 10% per year loss, fragmented NGO catalogues, inaccessible to youth, no scalable culturally sensitive tool, LLM fine - tuned, transcribed IK text, vernacular audio, bilingual modules, mobile app, query - based retrieval, adaptive learning, Baiga tribe, forest - based livelihoods, demographic details, weakest link, loss of healer knowledge, poverty, health access, minor tourism, no major manufacturing, SFURTI / ASPIRE, Maori databases, UNESCO repositories, lack oral - vernacular integration, lack youth engagement, interactive voice - based bilingual learning, advances beyond static archives, youth engagement, digitize oral IK, bilingual adaptive learning modules, voice - enabled mobile app, train 20 community trainers, establish 5 knowledge hubs, Healers, Institutional Support, Field Access, hub viability, Community brokers, Post - funding viability\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Return ONLY clean Markdown. No JSON, no code fences, no preamble.\n- Preserve [CITE:key] anchors exactly when mapped evidence is used.\n- Default to a single compact block unless the template clearly requires bullets.	The project is organized into three annual phases that systematically build the technical, human, and institutional infrastructure needed to convert the Baiga PVTG’s endangered oral indigenous knowledge (IK) into an AI-powered STI Hub platform—improving primary healthcare access and creating durable youth livelihoods. The work plan is grounded in five community hubs in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, each governed by a Hub Advisory Circle of traditional healers, youth Knowledge Brokers, ethnobotanists, and project staff, ensuring data sovereignty and local accountability from the start.\n\n### Year 1 – Documentation and Hub Activation\n\n1. Complete participatory rural appraisal and obtain Prior Informed Consent from 200 traditional healers across 50 villages, establishing culturally grounded data ownership protocols.\n2. Audio-document oral IK pharmacopoeia (minimum 5,000 vernacular herbal formulations) and transcribe the recordings into a bilingual Baiga-Hindi text corpus, closing the 1995 documentation gap.\n3. Achieve 100% validation of all documented plant-based remedies through a panel of ethnobotanists and senior healers, yielding a validated IKS database ready for AI training.\n4. Make five solar-powered STI Hub physical infrastructures fully operational—each with mobile connectivity, all-weather road access, and solar charging stations—serving as the backbone for all subsequent activities.\n5. Form Hub Advisory Circles at every hub (healer elders, 60% women youth members, NTFP micro-enterprise representatives) that will govern data stewardship, set community access rules, and monitor quarterly progress.\n\n### Year 2 – LLM/App Development and Youth Broker Deployment\n\n1. Fine-tune an open-source LLM (Llama-based) on the validated bilingual IK corpus and verify ≥90% query accuracy under a human-in-the-loop evaluation protocol, ensuring safe, culturally appropriate health guidance.\n2. Deploy a voice-enabled, offline-first mobile app (Android) with QR-code access, bilingual audio queries, and adaptive learning modules explicitly designed to bridge the literacy-technology gap in an oral-first culture.\n3. Recruit, train, and certify 50 Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30) through a stipend-supported digital apprenticeship in app usage, IKS-driven primary health triage, and data log maintenance.\n4. Beta-test the app with 500 community members supervised by the newly trained Brokers; incorporate iterative refinements using quarterly user feedback surveys and ethnobotanist audits.\n5. Organize five block-level Health Tech Melas in partnership with the MP Tribal Welfare Department and NHM staff, demonstrating the app to 5,000 residents and enrolling new users.\n6. Establish real-time monitoring dashboards that capture offline-first query logs, demographic data, and resolution outcomes, enabling weekly QA checks and linkage to SEED evaluation indicators.\n\n### Year 3 – Training, Scale-Up, and Hub Sustainability\n\n1. Scale active user base to 15,000 community members across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks, delivering primary health query resolution within 24 hours for at least 80% of cases through the human-in-the-loop broker network.\n2. Secure employment-ready digital certification for 50 Knowledge Brokers from MP Tribal Welfare Department; at least 30% of Brokers transition to NTFP-based micro-enterprises using the IKS database for sustainable harvesting guidance.\n3. Complete a train-the-trainer cycle that equips 200 healers to independently contribute new IK entries via voice annotation, ensuring the system enables active transmission rather than remaining a static archive.\n4. Achieve post-funding viability for all five STI Hubs through licensed NTFP enterprise linkages, Ayushman Bharat-integrated digital health service channels, and community contribution mechanisms approved by Hub Advisory Circles.\n5. Publish the final validated IKS database (≥7,000 entries) in a citable digital repository with access protocols governed by Hub Advisory Circles, fully compliant with MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme and national Prior Informed Consent norms.\n6. Submit a policy brief and PVTG scalability blueprint to DST and the Ministry of Tribal Affairs, providing a replicable model for other tribes such as Sahariya and Bharia.\n\n### Expected Deliverables: Output, Outcome, and Socio-Economic Impact\n\nQuantitative output: One validated bilingual oral IKS digital corpus (5,000+ formulations); one fine-tuned AI health-query app with 90% accuracy; 5 solar-powered community STI Hubs; 50 certified tribal youth Knowledge Brokers; 15,000 community users accessing primary health guidance. Outcome: Tangible improvement in healthcare access with reduced unnecessary travel to distant PHCs for non-emergency queries; 50 youth gain verifiable digital skills that break the apprenticeship chain and reverse distress migration; healer knowledge transmissions become continuous, arresting the 10% per year loss rate. Socio-economic impact: A cadre of tech-enabled youth brokers drives equitable growth, with measurable income augmentation from NTFP micro-enterprises informed by sustainable harvesting knowledge. The Hubs evolve into self-sustaining, community-owned knowledge centres that embed Indigenous Knowledge into local health and livelihood ecosystems—a replicable PVTG digital capacity model aligned with NEP 2020 and Vocal for Local.\n\n### Monitoring and Governance Checkpoints\n\nAll activities are tracked quarterly against the milestones using SEED Division Annexure‑A indicators: healer documentation count, database validation rate, app accuracy, adoption curves, query resolution time, youth certification numbers, and hub sustainability scorecards. Each Hub Advisory Circle reports directly to the PI, and an external expert committee conducts mid-term and end-line evaluations. Human-in-the-loop QA audits of AI responses, offline-first log analytics, and quarterly household surveys ensure the technology remains accurate, culturally embedded, and responsive to the oral-first community it serves.	3311	DRAFT	f	1	2026-04-30 05:50:24.998	2026-04-30 05:50:24.998	2026-04-30 05:50:24.994	single_pass	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"grantDraftReadiness": {"issues": [], "warnings": []}, "grantComplianceReport": {"stage": "pass2", "passed": true, "generatedAt": "2026-04-30T05:50:24.964Z", "hardFailures": [], "softWarnings": [{"key": "reviewer_signal_1", "source": "guideline", "message": "Required grant constraint is missing: Reviewer signal 1", "ruleText": "please don't give baseline survey, recruitment of project staff and purchase of Capital as milestone/ target. Refer evaluation and monitoring indicators in Guidelines of scheme/ programme of SEED division, refer Annexure- A. Work plan/Milestones/ targets (minimum 5-6 per year). Expected deliverables (anticipated quantitative output, outcome & socio-economic impact on target beneficiaries)"}, {"key": "reviewer_please don t give baseli", "source": "guideline", "message": "Reviewer-facing signal is weak or missing: please don't give baseline survey, recruitment of project staff and purchase of Capital as milestone/ target. Refer evaluation and monitoring indicators in Guidelines of scheme/ programme of SEED division, refer Annexure- A. Work plan/Milestones/ targets (minimum 5-6 per year). Expected deliverables (anticipated quantitative output, outcome & socio-economic impact on target beneficiaries)", "ruleText": "please don't give baseline survey, recruitment of project staff and purchase of Capital as milestone/ target. Refer evaluation and monitoring indicators in Guidelines of scheme/ programme of SEED division, refer Annexure- A. Work plan/Milestones/ targets (minimum 5-6 per year). Expected deliverables (anticipated quantitative output, outcome & socio-economic impact on target beneficiaries)"}, {"key": "narrative_please don t give baseli", "source": "template", "message": "Narrative guidance is not clearly reflected: please don't give baseline survey, recruitment of project staff and purchase o