cmojz4yqi00la77ycmr8k60ix	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	project_summary	Project Summary	The Baiga Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group in Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, is losing its Indigenous Knowledge system at an estimated 10% per year. Approximately 200 traditional healers—the sole custodians of plant-based healthcare and forest livelihood knowledge—practice in a 100% ST population where 70% live below the poverty line, literacy is low, and the last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995. Existing records are fragmented text-heavy NGO catalogues, culturally alien static archives, or external databases like TKDL that lack oral-vernacular integration and offer no pathway for youth engagement or active knowledge transmission. The result is a broken apprenticeship chain, accelerated by youth out-migration, and an urgent need to bridge the literacy-technology gap with an oral-first, AI-driven solution.\n\nThis proposal establishes five AI-Driven STI Hubs across Bichhiya and Mawai blocks to digitize, validate, and redeploy oral Indigenous Knowledge through a voice-enabled bilingual mobile application. The core S&T intervention is an open-source large language model (Llama) fine-tuned on a proprietary corpus of transcribed Baiga oral audio, delivering query-based adaptive learning modules and sustainable NTFP harvesting guidance. Fifty Baiga youth Knowledge Brokers (50% female, ages 18–30) serve as the primary interface—conducting household surveys, collecting audio documentation, and disseminating the app via QR codes in all-weather solar-powered community hubs. A Human-in-the-Loop validation process with ethnobotanists and healers ensures 90% AI accuracy while maintaining data sovereignty and Prior Informed Consent.\n\nOver three years, the project will generate a validated IKS database covering approximately 200 healers’ knowledge, certify 50 youth as community health and livelihood facilitators, and improve primary healthcare access for 15,000 Baiga community members. By aligning with MoTA’s Digital Empowerment Scheme and NHM tribal programs, and anchoring livelihood augmentation through NTFP micro-enterprises, the model establishes a replicable pathway for scaling digital capacity across other PVTG communities—directly advancing the STI Hubs mandate of socioeconomic transformation through science and technology.	303	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - PROJECT SUMMARY\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral IK digitization, Audio documentation, Beta testing, Community hubs, Ethnobotanists, Validation, Bichhiya, Mawai, Solar power, Mobile connectivity, All - weather roads, 5 community hubs, Mandla, Train - the - Trainer, PRA - based mobilization, Bichhiya block, Mawai block, 50 Baiga youth, 50% female, ages 18 - 30, 150,000 Baiga community members, improve