cmoi88a2t00di40wt0veafunk	cmodzbetg008nxrslnjerg39y	existing_solutions_for_problem	existing_solutions_for_problem	Existing regional solutions for the Phulkari craft, primarily found in MSME clusters, rely on fully industrial models that often destroy traditional aesthetics . This approach is unavailable to micro-clusters and fails to address the specific needs of 2,000 marginalized women artisans, leading to slow technology adoption and a risk of abandoning the craft due to low wages. While national initiatives like the MSME SFURTI scheme aim for cluster development, they typically do not offer context-appropriate, semi-mechanized solutions tailored for traditional crafts, creating a significant technology gap. The urgency arises from the potential loss of this invaluable Indigenous Knowledge System (IKS) and the socio-economic precarity of the artisans. This proposal is justified now by its focus on developing and disseminating context-appropriate, semi-mechanized tools that respect traditional aesthetics, thereby providing a necessary baseline for why a new S&T intervention is required.	139	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - EXISTING REGIONAL/ NATIONAL/ INTERNATIONAL SOLUTIONS FOR THE PROPOSED PROBLEM\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: 2,000 artisans, Technology adoption, Generational preservation, Phulkari craft, MSME clusters, Scale\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment o