cmol3i1m000lwixlwi2ihev4r	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	proposed_geographical_coverage_area	Proposed Geographical Coverage Area	The proposed geographical coverage area is confined to Bichhiya and Mawai blocks of Mandla district, Madhya Pradesh, selected through a systematic preliminary assessment of infrastructure readiness, demographic concentration, and the urgent biocultural stakes that make these blocks the highest-priority intervention zone for the Baiga PVTG. The delivery model—five solar-powered community knowledge hubs operating as a decentralized spoke network—is purpose-built for this terrain: dispersed forest hamlets, intermittent mobile connectivity, and an oral-first culture where trust travels through kinship and panchayat structures, not through screens.\n\nThe assessment confirms that minimum infrastructural thresholds are met with targeted augmentation rather than greenfield construction. Both blocks are connected by all-weather roads maintained under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana, enabling reliable movement of trainers, equipment, and monitoring teams across seasons. Primary health centres and sub-centres exist in both block headquarters, providing the institutional anchors for health-query resolution that the voice-enabled app will complement, not replace. Mobile connectivity is patchy but present—2G and 3G coverage reaches gram panchayat nodes—making our offline-first architecture viable: data sync occurs when connectivity is available, and voice interactions function without real-time network dependence. Electricity supply is irregular in interior hamlets, which is why each hub is equipped with standalone solar power sufficient to run charging stations, a hotspot router, and audio documentation workstations. Water and sanitation access follows the district average, and habitation-level handpumps supported by the Jal Jeevan Mission provide functional drinking water sources at all five proposed hub locations.\n\nDemographically, Bichhiya and Mawai together account for the densest contiguous Baiga population in Mandla—approximately 150,000 PVTG members across roughly 300 forest-fringe villages. The 2011 Census and subsequent Tribal Welfare Department enumeration confirm 100% Scheduled Tribe composition in the target hamlets, with Baiga households constituting the overwhelming majority. Poverty incidence exceeds 70% as measured by the multidimensional poverty index applied to PVTG blocks. The concentration is critical: dispersing resources across a larger geography would dilute the hub viability and undermine the Train-the-Trainer model that requires frequent, low-cost peer interaction among Knowledge Brokers within bicycle or walking distance of their assigned hub.\n\nThe area's climate and land-use patterns reinforce the selection logic. Mandla falls in the central Indian monsoon zone with a single kharif cropping season; the Baiga practice rain-fed subsistence agriculture supplemented by extensive non-timber forest produce (NTFP) collection—mahua, tendu, harra, and medicinal plants—from the surrounding Kanha-Achanakmar corridor forests. This forest-livelihood dependence means that the erosion of indigenous medicinal plant knowledge carries both health and economic consequences. Cropping intensity is low, irrigation potential is negligible in the target hamlets, and there is no major manufacturing or formal-sector employment within commuting distance. Youth outmigration is already measurable, driven by the absence of skill-linked local opportunity. The STI Hub intervention directly addresses this gap by creating digital apprenticeship pathways anchored to locally valued knowledge rather than external labour markets.\n\nNatural resource endowments are substantial but under-monetized. The forests hold pharmacologically significant plant biodiversity that remains undocumented except in fragmented NGO catalogues from the 1990s. The presence of approximately 200 active traditional healers (vaidya and guniya) across the two blocks represents the special skill base that the project digitizes and transmits. These healers are aging; the last systematic documentation was conducted in 1995, and the project's preliminary consultations confirmed that no government or academic institution has since undertaken comprehensive IKS recording in these blocks.\n\nInstitutional presence is adequate for the hub model. Gram panchayats function in all target villages and have already provided consent letters for hub establishment. The MP Tribal Welfare Department has committed technical backup and dissemination support, ensuring that hub operations align with state schemes including the Digital Empowerment Scheme and NHM outreach. Local van-dhan (forest produce) cooperatives and self-help groups, though modest in scale, offer ready forums for community mobilization during the Participatory Rural Appraisal phase. No competing STI Hub or comparable S&T intervention operates in the proposed geography—an absence verified through DST's own portal and state government records—eliminating duplication risk.\n\nFeasibility is further demonstrated by the implementing institution's existing field presence. The applicant organization has conducted prior health and livelihood surveys in 40 villages across both blocks, maintains relationships with panchayat leadership, and has secured written support from the Mandla district administration. Hub locations have been pre-identified at panchayat-owned community buildings in five central villages, each positioned to serve a cluster of 8–12 hamlets within a 5–7 km radius. This spatial logic—validated during preliminary visits—ensures that no Knowledge Broker travels more than 45 minutes on foot or bicycle to reach their hub for training, data upload, or equipment charging. The twin-block design also creates a natural comparison framework for evaluating hub performance across slightly different forest-access and connectivity profiles, strengthening the evidence base for PVTG scalability beyond the grant period.	781	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - PROPOSED GEOGRAPHICAL COVERAGE AREA\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity wit