cmol20cod0061ixlw6lmltaom	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	technology_gaps_and_solutions	Technology Gaps And Solutions	Unlike the static text archives that define the current state of the art in Indigenous knowledge preservation—from India’s own Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) to New Zealand’s Maori databases and UNESCO’s intangible cultural heritage repositories—this project deploys a fine-tuned bilingual Large Language Model (LLM) that transforms orally transmitted Baiga ethnobotanical knowledge into an interactive, voice‑queryable adaptive learning tool, directly bridging the literacy‑technology gap that locks out the 70% below‑poverty‑line youth of a Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group. The existing gold‑standard solutions were built to catalogue and defend against biopiracy; they are text‑heavy, designed for patent examiners and researchers, and offer no culturally resonant interface for oral‑first communities where the transmission chain is breaking at 10% per year due to youth migration and the absence of structured apprenticeship. No scalable, culturally sensitive system exists that turns living oral knowledge into a query‑retrieval engine a semi‑literate Baiga youth can speak to in her own dialect and immediately receive bilingual (Baiga‑Hindi) educational modules validated by community healers and ethnobotanists. That gap is the project’s target, and closing it matters because this is not an archival exercise: it is a survival intervention for biocultural heritage and for the primary healthcare of 150,000 Baiga community members who rely on rapidly disappearing healer knowledge in forest‑based hamlets.\n\nWhat makes the proposed solution genuinely differentiated is the shift from static archiving to active, human‑in‑the‑loop transmission. Unlike TKDL’s text‑only patent‑protection format or UNESCO’s curated multimedia repositories that stay locked in institutional portals, the planned AI‑Driven STI Hub will ingest audio‑documented, transcribed, and validated IK from ~200 traditional healers—the last systematic documentation was in 1995—and fine‑tune an open‑source Llama‑based LLM entirely on that oral‑vernacular corpus. The resulting voice‑enabled mobile app will deliver bilingual adaptive learning modules, allow free‑form voice queries (e.g., “Show me the plant for fever found in the upper forest”), and function offline‑first on solar‑charged devices inside five solar‑powered community hubs, because mobile connectivity in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks remains unreliable. This is not a design modification of an existing digital library; it is a ground‑up re‑engineering of the knowledge pipeline so that young Knowledge Brokers, 50% of them women aged 18‑30, become the primary learning interface, not passive app users. The model directly responds to the call’s emphasis on sustainable livelihoods: the 50 youth earn certifications, receive stipends, and operate the hubs as micro‑enterprises, while the LLM’s adaptive feedback loop improves accuracy toward a 90% benchmark validated quarterly by ethnobotanists and healers.\n\nCredibility in this context rests on four pillars already in place. First, deep field anchoring: 20 years of grassroots presence by the partner institution among the Baiga, a “Train‑the‑Trainer” methodology, and PRA‑based mobilization ensure that Prior Informed Consent and community data sovereignty are embedded through Hub Advisory Circles, not retrofitted. Second, institutional backing: formal technical support and large‑scale dissemination commitment from the MP Tribal Welfare Department, aligned with the National Health Mission and Ministry of Tribal Affairs’ Digital Empowerment scheme, de‑risk post‑funding viability and enable integration into public health outreach. Third, a deliberately low‑risk technology stack: fine‑tuning an open‑source LLM on a domain‑constrained IK corpus avoids hallucination and cost‑escalation, while human‑validated curated responses—not raw model outputs—service community queries, preserving healer authority and preventing conflicting information that erodes trust. Fourth, a realistic implementation arc: Year‑1 audio documentation and transcription, Year‑2 LLM fine‑tuning and app beta testing inside hubs, Year‑3 training of 50 Knowledge Brokers and scaling to 15,000 community users. In contrast to the best‑known international initiatives that digitised objects but lost the oral, adaptive, and livelihood‑creating dimensions, this project assembles a replicable PVTG‑tested blueprint that moves IKS from a passive archive to an active, youth‑driven health and livelihood infrastructure.	604	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - TECHNOLOGY GAPS AND NEW INNOVATIVE S&T COMPONENT/ ADAPTIVE R&D SOLUTION PROPOSED AS PER NEED IDENTIFICATION\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. Government Academic Institutions (Central and State Government), Government S&T bodies, R&D Labs etc. Private Academic Institutions and R&D Institutions (Universities/ Colleges/ Institutions and Government Aided Colleges recognized or regulated by UGC/AICTE/ MCI/DCI/PCI etc, having SIRO recognition etc). S&T based Voluntary Organizations (NGOs) with legal status or as a society registered under the Societies Registration Act 1860 or a Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act 1982 or Charitable or Religious Act 1920 or under the corresponding State Act. Minimum 10 years of field level experience in technology development, dissemination, delivery and management. The submission of proposals is open to all categories and the investigators submitting proposal may not necessarily belong to SC Community.\n- Deliverables: STI Hubs to facilitate socioeconomic empowerment of SC communities through improvement of the existing livelihood systems, besides creation of newer and viable options, equitable growth, productivity enhancement, social enterprise development, and equity with inclusion. These projects should enhance welfare system efficiency through Science, Technology, and Innovation (STI). By strengthening delivery mechanisms and applying S&T interventions, it must build NGO capacity, expand institutional outreach, and promote sustainable knowledge for community development.\n- Focus areas: science and technology, socio-economic development, capacity enhancement, sustainable development, livelihoods, Indigenous Knowledge Systems, Infrastructure, Equipment Grant\n- Global keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG, 60% Women, Knowledge Brokers, Household surveys, regional workshops, MP Tribal Welfare Department, Health Tech Melas, Year 1 : Documentation, Year 2 : LLM / App, Year 3 : Training / Hubs, Validated IKS database, 5 operational STI Hubs, 50 Knowledge Brokers, 3 years, 90% AI accuracy, 50 youth certifications, 15,000 community users, Human - in - the - Loop, offline - first logs, quarterly surveys, Infrastructure, Capacity, Product, digital apprenticeship, voice AI, literacy gap bridge, active transmission, vs static archiving, TKDL, youth migration, 70% poverty, apprenticeship chain break, literacy - technology gap, static archives, text - heavy, oral - first culture, AI - Driven STI Hub, IKS preservation, LLM fine - tuning, Oral