cmojz99w600lo77yc1fo6l49d	cmojtx277009k77ycv5pu74az	target_beneficiaries	Target Beneficiaries	By training 50 Baiga youth—30 women and 20 men—as Knowledge Brokers and equipping them with a voice‑first AI app, the project will put culturally grounded primary‑health guidance directly into the hands of 15,000 community members in Bichhiya and Mawai blocks. This immediate intervention changes outcomes for a population that is 100 % Scheduled Tribe (Baiga PVTG), with 60 % female and 40 % male beneficiaries; no transgender individuals are recorded in the target hamlets. The proposed geographical area is entirely ST, yielding 0 % General, 0 % SC, and 0 % OBC. Of the total 15,000 beneficiaries, 9,000 are women (60 %) and 10,500 fall within Economically Weaker Sections (70 % of households live below the poverty line). Over three years, the Knowledge Brokers will embed the AI‑driven STI Hub into daily life, ensuring that women—who disproportionately bear the burden of untreated illness—receive timely, voice‑mediated health advice, while 50 youth earn industry‑recognised digital certifications, seeding a scalable livelihood pathway for other PVTGs.	162	\N	\N	TASK: GRANT SECTION DRAFT - TARGET BENEFICIARIES\n\nYou are writing a grant proposal section for reviewers inside Grapsi.\nWrite reviewer-facing proposal prose, not a journal manuscript section.\n\nGRANT CONTEXT:\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Prep keywords: digital health access, youth employment, PVTG scalability, digital capacity, 100% ST, Baiga PVTG\n\nSEEDED CONTEXT:\n- Treat this frozen workspace context as grounding. Reuse it where relevant; do not contradict it.\n- Project: Grant: STI Hubs\n- Funding call: STI Hubs\n- Agency: Department of Science and Technology\n- Deadline: 2026-05-16\n- Project duration: 3 years\n- Eligibility: The following organizations/institutes are eligible for receiving financial support for establishment of STI Hubs. 